      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-09-01_WidnesExaminer_art0021" n="art0021">
        <p>LADIES' LETTER</p>
        <p>ZdY DEAR NELLIK,</p>
        <p>With September close at hand, it will not be a taatter for surprise if we have changes in fashion almost amounting to a revolution. The sun has become less hot and scorching, but still retain a beat sufficiently comfortable as to be reasonably endured by us all. Millinery has not yet, as far as I am aware, taken a decided turn, and I fancy that the picture hat and those with broad brims will very easily hold their own for a few weeks to come. A lady I met yesterday had a light straw bat trimmed with lace and two red flowers slightly to the left side on the forehead. Projecting above these were two feathery decorations, which un- deniably added a charm to her otherwise pretty features. I liked the headgear immensely, and I confess it, I really felt a little envious of a very becoming article of dress.</p>
        <p>Seeing that the weather will soon be getting showery the need for the mackintosh coats will be again with us. There are practically no end of patterns in vogue, and some of them which I have seen have been composed of covert coating in all colours. Some of them have been made single-breasted, and if I had my choice I should certainly choose one of that style. Many prefer •the double-breased ones, but divergent views will ever show themselves.</p>
        <p>Afternoon dresses are of that lovely fine texture which everyone of us values in the glorious summer time. The zephyr of the bodice is crossed by transverse checks of the insertion, and a similar trimming appears on the sleeves. Three straight lines of the lace form a yoke, outlined by frills like those on the skirt. The dress may be pure white, or in a pale, soft tone of pink, yellow, mauve, green, blue, or string colour. The point on the skirt, back and front, simulates a polonaise, the chiffon frills, edged with the narrowest black ribbon velvet, being headed with a line of lace in- sertion, to which a second is added, some two inches above.</p>
        <p>The marriage of a friend of mine reminds me that a good deal of the hitches which present themselves to those at the altar arise from ignor- ance, and I will therefore offer a little advice. The bride, on her arrival at the church, takes, to proceed to the altar, the right arm of her father, or of the person who acts the part of father and gives her away. She stands during the marriage service at the left hand of the bridegroom and at the conclusion of the ceremony takes his left arm to proceed to the vestry, and on leaving the vestry it is again hie left arm she takes; also later, when they lead the way together in to breakfast, it is his left arm she takes, and at his left hand she sits. When the wedding breakfast is a "sib down" one, the bride and bridegroom sit together, either at the head or the centre of the table. At the present times fashion rules that none but short coats are to be seen, and of these two parti- cular specimens are chief favourites: the one with short coat-tails, and the other, with scalloped out basquen some three or four inches deep. The latter have a pocket on either side. and a small handker- chief pocket higher up on the left. The little basque in the middle of the back eometimes extends all round, but more often the sides are plain, while the fronts dip down in backward-curving points. Now and then the neck is scooped away and finished with lines of braid or rows of stitch- ing, the whole outlined by a small army of buttons, a very favourite trimming just now. Again, the fronts may be almost wide enough to meet, and covered with a rich design in braiding. From an experience of a few years I am of opinion that the garment pre-eminently worthy of attention by shose who seek the pleasures of other people's country houses or their own, is the tea. gown, and for this there arc few better fabrics than velveteen, and these gowns shon/d always be made with a suggestion of the picturesque. No straight lines and rigid following of fashion is advisable in the making of the velveteen tea- gown. Jewelled girdles, lace collars, open sleeves and flowing draperies should be the dominant notes, and the colours should be light not dull. It is a great mistake to choose the darker shades of velveteen, whose best beauties appear as its lights develop and shade.</p>
        <p>Your loving JENNIS</p>
