      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-09-01_WidnesExaminer_art0024" n="art0024">
        <head>" WOMEN'S CHAT."</head>
        <p>" WOMEN'S CHAT."</p>
        <p>In all the Qneen's long life, nothing stands out more clearly than her profound attachment to the Eli4lalands, and the fact is recalled by Her Majesty once more taking up her residence at Balmoral for the autumn. The "Leaves from the Journa.i of Our Life i 6 the Highlands," testify again and again to the affection with which the Sovereign regards her northern home, its life, and sur- roundings. Writing on September Bth, 1343 the Queen records—"We arrived at Bemoral at a quarter to three. It is a pretty little Castle in the old Scottish style. There is a picturesque tower and garden in front with a high wooded hill. At the back there is a wood down to the Dee, and the hills rise up all round." Eight years later the old castle gave place to the new building which was designed in every detail by the Prince Consort, and on October 13th, 1856 stands the entry. "Every year my heart becomes more fixed in this dear paradise." Suc- ceeding years have fully borne out this promise, and they have seen the Queen return with increasing satia- faction to the home and the life in the north to which she is so attached.</p>
        <p>From an artistic point of view the interior of Balmoral is scarcely a success. Everything is as it was forty years ago, even to the numerous alabaster figures pro- tected by glass shades ; and the colours employed in the decoration of the various apartments are of that terribly crude variety, en evidence half a century back, and at which we nowadays, one and all feel constrained to shudder. The drawing roam, with its carpet,and chair upholstery of" Victorian" tartan, is certainly a some- what fearsome department, and one of our Princesses, whose artistic tendencies are well-known to all,has been heard to remark en more than one occasion, that the briefest sojourn in that room makes her ill for a week. Among foreign royalties, the Dowager Empress of Russia is undoubtedly the most artistic dresser. Her clothes are always exquisite, and in the best possible taste. The Queen of Portugal comes next perhaps, and the Duchess of Saxe-Coburg is not far behind. Among the worst dressed may he ranked the Empress of Ger- many, the Queen of Italy, the Empress Frederick, and the Crown Princess of Denmark. The Crown Princess is very tall and stately ; and elegantly and tastefully garbed would make a nice-locking woman. But as matters stand, the crude striking toilettes she affects only serve to accentuate her bad points, and distribute headachss among her family and household. Princess Charles (our Princess Maud of Wales) has ever been noted for her excellent taste in dress, and her m ther- in-law must indeed prove a trial to her in this particular direction.</p>
        <p>Speaking of Denmark, an interesting arrangement is in operation in that country, whereby a very large number of children are pr )vided with holidays at a small cost. During the summer some thousands of children from CDpenhagen and the larger towns are sent to farm houses in the country, where they are boarded by the proprietors free of ckarge. In the winter the farmers' children aresent in turn to the homes of the dwellers in the towns, who entertain them in a similar manner. During the present year this system of ex- change has proved a great boon to the dwellers in towns.</p>
        <p>One of the most remarkable women in this country is Miss Percival, who has just entered upon her ninety sixth year. She is one of the twelve children of the Right Hon. Spencer Percival, Prime Minister and Chan- cellor of the Exchequer, whose assassination in the House of Commons created BO profound an impression in the early part of the century. Miss Percival retains a vivid recollection of the tragic circumstances of her father's death, as well as of the visit which she paid with her mother and nine of her brothers and sisters, to George 111. and his Queen at Windsor Castle. Everyone will be glad to hear that " London Jack" has been restored to his friends. He is one of those rail- way dogs who serve as collecting agents for various charities, and was missed frem the London and South- western Railway systems some few weeks ago. The doe is a handsome black retriever and has now been found by the police at an alleged deg stealers place, where about fifty or sixty animals were discovered of the value of sev7ea hundred pounds. Needless to say" London Jack" is back again on duty for the Railway Orphanage Fund, lie is of course only one of several colleeting dogs on the various lines about the country, There is " Tim "at Paddington, who lately attracted the kindly notice of the Queen. There used to be another named " Help " on the Brighton line, and another " Jack " be- longing to Derby, who could distinguish between the trains and servants of the different companies. He used to take long trips by trains, hut always knew how to find his way back again to Derby. In Germany, it has long been part of the military system to train dogs of war. The idea is to utilise tli ern in operations and on the battlefield to carry messages and deepatchee. For this purpose they are taught to distinguish between the German uniforms, and those of other countries. Dummies are dressed up to represent the enemy whom the does are trained to attack or avcid. To many this practice of using animals in warfare is offensive, and it was a matter for regret that nothing was done at the Peace Conference to restrict or abolish the awful sufferings which horses are subjected to en the battlefield. The dog has however been put to a more useful purpose during the recent military manceuvres in Germany. Fur some years Herr Bungartz, the animal painter, has been training Scotch shepherd dogs to assist relief parties in quickly discovering the wounded in battle. Their value for this purpose has teen amply demonstrated, and it is now proposed to greatly extend the system of rearing and educating these dogs of war in Germany. Browneeerns likely to enjoy A wide vogue this autumn and coming winter, and already some dainty costumes in this tint are in course of construction. When care- fully manipulated, in the way of hleneing with other tinte, few colours are more successful, but it must be acknowledged that we British women do not shine in this direction as do our French sisters. Brown is not n-</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-09-01_WidnesExaminer_art0025" n="art0025">
        <p>teresting in itself, like purple or green, cr gray, and we &amp;timid leave it alone If unable to exploit it. Our headgear is slowly, but surely, creeping back- wards, a fact te:be deplored. The tilt forward is mach more chic and desirable from every point of view. The most refined face looks anything but pleasing with a millinery halo surrounding it, and those individuals less favoured by Nature, positively vulgar. Veils have, been less worn of late, than for some time past, but those patronised cover the face and chin and are drawn up round the neck, as of yore. Housekeepers would do well to remember that all fish should be used as soon as possible after it is caught. In fresh fish the eves should always be bright and prominent, the body stiff, the gills a bright red colour. As food for invalids, white fish snch as ling, cod, soles, skate. turbot, etc., are the best. Salmon, mackerel, berrings,and trout decompose the soonest after they are killed. Flat fish will keep much longer.</p>
        <p>" MADGE."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-09-01_WidnesExaminer_art0026" n="art0026">
        <head>NEGLECT OF FAALILY AT WARRINGTON.</head>
        <p>NEGLECT OF FAALILY AT WARRING- TON.</p>
        <p>A BRUTAL FATHER DESERVEDLY PUNISHED.</p>
        <p>At the Warrington Police Court, on Monday, before Mr. F. Monks (in the chair) and other magistrates, Frederick Noton, 29, Leicester-street, was charged on a warrant with ill-treating and neglecting his five children in a manner likely to cause them unnecessary suffering and injury to health. Mr. A. Browne prosecuted on behalf of the N.S.P.C.C. He said the case was a very bad one. The man was served personally with a summons and should have appeared at Court on Friday. As he did not come a warrant was issued, and he was apprehended on Satur- day by Detective Delahay. When arrested be said" You are just in time. I was off to Liverpool." The man was going to leave his wife and children, without doubt, to get out of the way of the police. He was a stonemason's labourer and his regular wages were 255. a week. Be had given his wife very little for the past two months. He bad also illtreated his daughter Bertha, on one occasion hitting her in the mouth with his fist and bursting one of her lips. Annie Noton. defendant's wife, said her husband bad been working for seven years, but bad not had much employment last week. In six weeks he had given her 13s. 6d. altogether, and in the fortnight previous to that she had received ss. Her eldest daughter earned 2s. 6d. a week. Neighbours had had to give the cbildren foed. Defendant illtreated his daughter Bertha, and on one occasion took a carving knife upstairs with him. Defendant denied all the charges against him. He said he had been walking about lately, as Messrs. Wil- liams and Clay had no work for him to do. The Chairman : Messrs. Williams and Clay don't say so in their letter to us, as they say they have a pretty fair amount of work on band.</p>
        <p>Defendant: I only get sd. an hour. I have not been drawing the amount of money my wife says I have been drawing. I have given her more than she says. How could she have lived otherwise?</p>
        <p>Councillor Shaw Green: Did you not hear her say she has had aasiitance from her neighbours Defendant: I have given her all I have drawn... . _ .</p>
        <p>Bertha Noton, daughter, said her fattier once hit her in the face because her little brother was crying for clothes, making her lips bleed. He had threatened to cut their throats. She was afraid of him because he said he would murder her. Annie Leader, 21, Leicester-street, SSW she bad given the children food several times in each week. She bad seen defendant under the influence of drink, and his family had come to her for protection. Detective Delahay said he visited the house on the 7th August, and saw the girl Bertha. Her face was very much swollen, and her gums were inflamed. Be was presented at the Police Court on the 141:13 August, when defendant was fined 105 and costs for drunkenness. Defendant said be was sober and if he bad appeared at Court he would have been let off.</p>
        <p>P.C. Turner said he visited the house on July 25th in company with Inspector Hackett. They went upstairs and examined the bedrooms, and found them to be in a most filthy state. There were no bedclothes, and he and the Inspector were covered with vermin after be- ing in the house for a time. He (witness) had known the man for a considerable time; he was a drunkard, but his wife was an industrious woman. P.S. Evans said he reported the man for being drunk and disorderly after 11 o'clock at night on the 14th August. He had turned his wife and children into the street, and called her foul names. Inspector Hackett said he had visited the house time after time. He did not consider the wife was te blame for the neglect. She kept the house fairly clean, but had no money to buy soap or anything .to keep It in a proper condition. Defendant sAid he was very sorry for what had occurred. If the magistrates would look over the offence, he would agree to his wife having 17s a week ; of his wages. She could get it from Williams and Clay's where he worked. The Chairman said the Bench considered the case a very aggravating one, and defendant would have to go to gaol for three months. Defendant: Oh, don't gentlemen. Mr. Birchall : This is the only way to deal with men, like you.</p>
      </div>
