      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-09-01_WidnesExaminer_art0037" n="art0037">
        <head>EXAMINER, FRIDAY, SEPTEMBER 1, 1899. BRE WSTER S ESSIONS.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, FRIDAY, SEPTEMBER 1, 1899. BRE WSTER S ESSIONS.</p>
        <p>another tussle for it, and the Bench refused it again ; and, last year, he recollected after his case had le-?en heard the Chairman stated "We have considered there is no alteration in the circumstancee from those of last year, and refuse to grant the application."—Mr. Davies: Do you suggest there has been an alteration this time ? —Witness: There are five new houses.—Mr. Davies : Have you the tenants as witnesses ?—Witness : They are not tenanted yet. (Laughter.)—Mr. Davies: 0, These are the changed circumstances? (renewed laughter).—Mr. Davies: You have made this application year after year.—Witness : Yes, and meant to come until I got it.—Mr. Davies : D on't you have any respect for the opinion of the Bench ?—Wit- ness : I want to respect them always, and do not dis- respect them now--Mr. Daviel : There are a great number of neighbours that do not want the license ? Witness: And there are a great number that do. (Laughter.)—Mr. Davies further questioned witness with regard to the rateable value of the several houses, but witness replied he could not exactly state. William Sands, carpenter and sub-contractor, Hale View, Weston-road. gave evidence to the effect that he dwelt about a quarter of a mile from Mr. Partington's shop, and that it would be a convenience to him to have the license. The "Lion Hotel" was about four or five hundred yards further off. He was rated on a £22 assessmeut, and there were other houses in the district similar. He thought the license would be a greet con- venience to other people besides himself. He was one of those who asked for the license.—Mr. Davies : You live nearer to the "Travellers Rest" than to Mr. Parting- ton's ?—Witness: It is very small then.—Mr. Giles : Let U 9 clear that up. You want a more convenient house ? You are not one of those who care to send your family to a public-house ?—Witness: Yes. I do not want to send my family to a public-house though I have no objection to going myself. (Laughter.)—Mr. Davies: Is it not a fact that you are nearer to the " Prospect Inn" than t Mr. Partington's P—Witness : I cannot say.—Mr. Davies : Will you swear you are ?—Witness: Perhaps you have measured it; will you swear I am not. (Laugnter.) Samuel Buckley, 9, Byron-street, soap-maker,; stated his rent was ss. a week. He lived about 100 yards from Mr. Partington's shop and occasionally did business with him. It would be a convenience to the neighbourhood if Mr. Partingtou had the license. Be was one that did not care to send his family to a public-house, and only wanted beet in small quantities.—By Mr. Davies : He lived on the lower side of the railway and had resided there for the past 12 months ; previous to tut he re- sided in Pico-street. Tne neighbours generally gat their beer from the " Lion Hotel," but, if Mr. Partington had a license, it would be better to Read the children there. His children were about 13 or 14 years of age. He did not care to go for the beer himself to the public-house for he might get too much (laughter).—Mr. Davies: You admit the publichouse offers a kind of temptation 2 Witness: Yes.—Mr. Davies : As a workingmen, you think there are too many licensed houses in Runcern ? Witness : It would be better if there were less licensed houses of a sort in Runcorn.—Mr. Giles : Let us clear that up, You want more publicaouses in the right piece? —Witness: Yes.—Mr. Giles : That is ; in the more congested parts of the town there are too many. You want more convenient ones in the growing districts.— Witness . Yes:—Mr. Giles : You would sooner send your children to a grocers shop where they cannot get anything to drink for themselves than to a public- house where they might ?—Witness: Yes that's it. John Clay, joiner, of Weston, stated he worked in Runcorn for a master. He resided about three quarters of a mile from Mr. Partineten's shop and considered the granting of a license to the shop would be a convenience te him. The nearest place of refreshment to his abode was the " Prospect Inn," but the ale was id. cheaper at the " Traveller's Rest "so he went there. (Laughter.) And it was as good ale too ! (Increased laughter.) Still, going to the Traveller's Rest, was dangerow, and on one occasion he had had to spend a night there. It was so dark that when he attempted to go home, he found himself right up azainst Mr. Norman's wall. (Renewed laughter.) Mr. Gibe: I take it you missed your way 2—Witness : Yes, that's it, air. (Loud laughter.) Witness added he would sooner go to Mr.Partington'a shop on account of the better road.—Mr. Davies . What you are in want of I should think is a residential hotel where you can spend the night, if you are belated. (Laughter.)— Witness here entered into an explanation of the dangers of crossing the quarries to the " Traveller's Rest " at night-time.—Mr. Davies: You admit the fact that you have two fully licensed houses nearer to you than Mr. Partington's.—Witness : Yes.—Mr. Davies : That will do. I simply want the facts. Tne Bench will judge of them.</p>
        <p>That concluded the application. Mr. Da vies stated he wished to be respectful to any case he was opposel to, but of all the licensing cases he had ever come across to use a vulgar expression he must say" it took the cake." He had never heard a case of that kind, seriously taken, which was so unsup- ported by evidence. It was a question of the conveni- ence of the people, that their worships had to consider. There was a gentleman who came forward after five times meeting with adverse decision from the Bench,and 1 who came forward after an intimation last year that the application was refused on the ground that the Bench saw no changed circumstances. There he was renewing his application. He was there to try and induce the Bench to grant the license,theugh there was no evidence of any changed circumstances, and that time the appli- cation was not even decently supported. Having reviewed the evidence submitted ifor the application Mr. Davies stated it was his opinion that such as appli- cation reduced licensing to a farce. He contended that there was no evidence coming from the people who re- sided in the nearer portion of the district—which was within a radius marked on the plan produced, which could be covered by the palm of the hand—and which was the district the application should have eminated jrom. As for the "need "of the license, he contended "that he had nothing to answer on that score, no "need " had been shown. On the other handfhe was there, re- presenting 45 property owners and 142 occupiers—all residing within the immediat;district—to oppose the application. They were brought there year after year at a great expense to onpnse this application, brought by a gentleman whose last intimation was that it was to be an annual affair ; and one who seemed to take no notice of the decisiens of the Bench and submitted no further evidence.—At this juncture the Chairman intim- ated that the Bench consider.d there were no altered circumstancee, and unanimously refused the applica- tion.</p>
        <p>interests of the public should he subordinate to the in- terests of the brewers • and if such were the ca=e,it was for their worships to take such things into c3nsideration and make the publicans bring their business more into conformity with the public needs. Surely thenoince the public needs were provided for amply, it must be a case of private interest. He further contended that the need of the license had not been proved. Surely it was not to be granted because the applicant sold somebody's special oatmeal stout for in- valids ;if that was so then possibly they would have people continually coming and because they sold " Snookson's Oatmeal Stout," or "Simons' Oatmeal Stout," or something of that nature for which no one else had the right to sell they must have a license. Such procedure was not licensing, and the public needs of Runcorn shoald or ought to be, adequately met by the existing number of licensed houses in the district. Again he felt sure that if the house were needed the license would n3t have "gone begging" for practically three months, especially if there was such a b Jsiness in it as represented. rhey did not doubt the applicant would not do a " jug " trade, if that undertaking was given • but, once a license was granted, it had a habit of sticking and could not be done away with, and likely enough the" jug V trade and the Sunday trade would follow. He, therefore, asked that the license be re- fused.</p>
        <p>The Superintendent of Police ( \lr. D. Pearson) also opposed the license on behalf of the constabulary, on the grounds that it woull increase the drinking facilities of the district. .</p>
        <p>The Chairman stated that the application W 3,3 unani- mously refused. BILLIARDS FOR THE FRIENDSHIP HOTEL.</p>
        <p>Mr. Joseph Burton, solicitor, Runcorn, applied on be- half of Mr. William Cobbler of the "Friendship Hotel," Fryer-street,Runcorn. for a billiard license for his prem ises.—Applicant haying submitted evideace of character and the due notices having been admitted, the super- intendent of Police stated they had no objections and thought the building a fit one.—Thi application was granted. SAD DROWNING FATA.LITIES AT DARESBURY.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-09-01_WidnesExaminer_art0038" n="art0038">
        <head>DARESBURY.</head>
        <p>DARESBURY.</p>
        <p>These sessions were held ar, the courtroom, Daresbury, on Tuesday, the magistrates on the Bench being Mr. J. C. Parr (chairman) and Colonel Ridgway. Police-Superintendent Pearson submitted the following mesa :—" Gentlemen. I have the honour to submit my annual report with reference to the conduct of licensed vict,uallcrs and beer retailers in this edivision during the past year. There are 23 licensed victuallers, one beer- seller, arid two others who sell off the premises, total 26; giving an average of 341 of the population to each lcense. No licensed victuallers have been proceeded against during the year, none being proceeded against in the preceding year. No beersellers have been proceeded against, none being proceeded against in the preceding year. Thirty-three persons have been pro- c eded against for drunkenness during the year, 33 of whom were convicted, as compared with 22 persons proceeded against, and 22 convicted during the preced- ing year. There is an increase of eleven persons pro- ceeded against and eleven convicted as compared with the preceding year." The Superintendent aided that on looking over the record he found it was hardly fair to assume that all the drunkenness had occurred in the distric., or that the publicans were responsible for it. Four persons were summoned for being drunk in charge wh.le pusliog through thedistrict,.and five were tramps. A good portion of the remaining 21. were Irish tarm labourers and not the usual residents. The space in the report relating to publicans supplying children under 13 years of age with drink was blank, and he was glad to say that the publicans were co-operating with the authorities, and were putting a stop to the practice of supplying children of tender years. The,Chairman : I think, ea the whole, the report is satisfactory, and does not really point to any increase in actual drinking. Of course we would wish it were less, but on the whole the report is satisfactory, and we are pleased .to recognize that the publicans are d ;Log their best to prevent the sale of liquor to children under age. The Clerk: Is there any opposition to any of the licenses in the Daresbury Division? Superintendent Pearson: The police don't oppose any of the existing licenses. The Clerk: All the licenses are renewed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-09-01_WidnesExaminer_art0039" n="art0039">
        <head>ItIINCORN.</head>
        <p>ItIINCORN.</p>
        <p>APFLICATIONS FOR OFF-LICENSES REFUSED.</p>
        <p>A PERNICIOUS SYSTEM.</p>
        <p>The annual Brewster Sessions, for the Runcorn Licens- ing Division, were held at the Police Station,on Monday, when the following magistrates presitiei .—Messrs. James Handley (chairman), F. J. Norman, A. R. Nor- man, Samuel faylors W; Timmins, amd F. Wiswall.—Mr. F. Johnson (magistrates' clerk), and Superintendent D. Pearson were also present. THE SUPERINTENDENT'S REPORT.</p>
        <p>Superintendent D. Pearson reported that in the Run- corn Division tnere were 44 licensed victuallers, 52 beer- sellers, and seven others who sold off toe premises; total 103; giving an average of 246 of the population to each license. Two licensed victuallers had been pro. eeeded a.gainst during the year. loctla of whom were convicted; being an increase of one proceeded against:, and one convicted in the' preceding year. One beeveller had been preceded against and convicted, being an increase cf one proceeded against and one con- victed in the preceding year, 137 persons had been proceeded against for arunkenness, during the year, 128 of whom were convicted as compared with 133 persons I proceeded against and convicted daring the preceding year. During the year one licensee had been reported for selling or delivering intoxicating liquors to a child apparently under the age of 13 years. The Superintend- ent further reported that in the case of the police v. Walters, of the Royal Oak, Weston, heard on the 6th June, it came out in evidence that the licensee was manager for Mr. Thomas Jones, and that he was paid 5 per cent on all takings over £2O. lie (the superintend- ent) had brought that matter before the Chiet Constable, and had been directed by him to bring the matter to the notice of the justices of the Brewster Sessions, as he (the Chief Constahle)thought it was a pernicious system. Ile(the superintendent)would say,however,in fairness to Mr. Jones, that he had seen him afterwards, and Mr. Jones had discontinued the practice of giving a percen- tage. In answer to the Bench, Mr. Thomas Jones, who was present in court, said he had now done away with the practice altogether.—The Superintendent continuing stated there were other licensed houses in the town be- longing to Messrs. Walker and Sons, Warrington, which were under management, but he did not know whether they too received a percentage.—The police did not ob- ject to the renewal of any licenses.—After a brief re- tirement, the Chairman on behalf of the Bench intimated they had given the Superintendent's report their fullest consideration. They were all of the same opinion as the Chief Constable as regards the payment of managers by results, and though they had no evidence of other people I carrying on the practice, they hoped the mere :nen- tion of their opinion would have the desired effect. With regard to the serving of little children. They did hope that the publicans would,co-operate with the authorities in this matter, and that they would not have I to deal with any such case again.</p>
        <p>THE TRANSFER LIST.</p>
        <p>The following transfers were granted :—Devonshire Hotel, Runcorn, from Archibald !laming to John Henry Lloyd; Royal Oak, Westoo.George Walters, to Archibald flaming ; Duke of Edinburgh, Runcorn, Thomas Brad- shaw to Samuel Buxton ; Red Lion, Halton, Frederick Taylor, to Thomas Parker; Sloop Inn, Runcorn, John Nicholls to Thomas Harrison; Fox Tavern, Runcorn, Thomas Harrison to Joseph Hodgkinson ; Cornwall and De% onshira Arms, Runcorn, Sarah Ann Urmson, to Martha Sinitham.</p>
        <p>The Bench fixed the adjourned licensing day for the 25th of September. MR. PARTINGTON AGAIN REFUSED. AN ANNUAL APPLICATION.</p>
        <p>Mr. Giles, solicitor, Chester, stated he appeared on behalf of Mr. George Partington, grocer, Ba!bur-road, Higher Runcorn, to apply tor an off-license for the sale of beer. In making the application he believed it was rather a case of " old friends with new faces." though personally he had not had the honour of appearing pre- viously before the Bench; yet he believed the applicant had appeared before them previously. In stating the pre3ent position of the application, he contended that Mr. Partington was in the position, as a grocer, to have a wine" off " and a spirit " off license," which were meters of great convenience to his customers, and he was there that day to ask for a beer off-license. The police, he understood, did not oppose it at all, or only in the way of making suggestions. At this point Mr. Giles submitted extracts from the recent report of the Commission on Licensing tending to show that it was the opinion of the majority that the granting of such licenses to grocers did not in any way facilitatedrinking. Mr. H. Davies, (of Me-srs. Herbert Lewis and Davies Liverpool), who appeared te object to the license,begged to point out that, although he felt sure his friend would not be guilty of any misrepresentation of tacts, the license referred to in the quotations used referred to the sealed bottle trade and not to off-beer licenses, for which the present application wee,. Mr. Giles then set forth the arguments, which have been so often reported, setting out that the district was practically a new one and a growing one and that the application was made to accommodate the needs of a large number of residents in the immediate neighbour- hood; that there were no nearer public-houses or houses of refreshments than the ." Lion Hotel and the "Travellers Rest;" and that the people who resided in the neighbourhood were a highly respectable claqi largely made up of the better class of artisan, clerks, and small tradesmen. etc., the dwellings in which they lived being of a rateable value of about £2O or £3O. Another contention nut forward by Mr. Giles was that of the advantages to be gained by the working classes in sending for their drink to such nieces as grocers shoos in preference to public-houses. Further. Mr. Giles submitted that on a previsus occa- sion Mr. Partington had an agreement with a brewery cor-op Iny ; but he was prepared, now, to give an under- taking that he had no such agreement, nor WWI it his in- tention of entering into one. They were absolutely free and had no intention of tying themselves down to any-</p>
        <p>George Partington, Balfor-road, Runcorn, said he was a groc r and provision dealer. The house and shop were the property of his wife, and he was the t-nant. He paid no rent, hut the property was rated at £25. The notices of application were duly served, and there was no objection recorded against them. There were 32 houses on the right hand side of his shop in Balfor-street. There was also a street running opp-site Ligbtburn-street which had about 18 houses on one side and 10 on the other. There were also other streets of housss of newly erscted appearance.. The value of the houses there would be about £l6, and those in the Holloway about £2O to £25. The class of people residing there were of the better artisan class, and clerks, and small tradesmen. He bad been asked only so recently as Saturday to supply a number ef his customers with be.r. He was some time ago under an agreement with a brewery company, but was now able to state he was free from any such agreement that he had no intention ot entering into another one.—Cross-examined by Mr. H. Davies : He had made five previous applicatioas to the Bench for a lic,nne and been refused each time. tie did remember in 1896 that the police stated their objection on the ground that th, re were sufficient public houses in the district and perhaps too many. In 1897 they had</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-09-01_WidnesExaminer_art0040" n="art0040">
