      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-09-01_WidnesExaminer_art0041" n="art0041">
        <head>ANOTHER APPLICATION REFUSED. ENOUGH DRINKING FACILITIES IN THE DISTRICT.</head>
        <p>ANOTHER APPLICATION REFUSED. ENOUGH DRINKING FACILITIES IN THE DISTRICT.</p>
        <p>Mr. Lewin, solicitor, Widnes, applied on behalf of Mr. Francis George Burns, of Burnside, Queen's Park, St. Helens, for an off-license so sail off the premises at 65, High-street, Runcorn.—Mr H Davies also appeared to object to this application; this time on behalf of Mr Harry Baker, the Rev C W L Christien, and the Rev J A B Malvern.—Mr Lewin in the course of his opening contended that inasmuch as Mr Burns, was already a holder of several licenses of a wholesale character for the premises in High-strf et, ho should be granted the application on the following four grounds : (1) It placed his premises,—which at present were not—under police bontrol. (2) They would have to be closed on a Sunday. (3) General convenience to customers through- out Runcorn, Aston, Sutton, Rock Savage, and else- where. (4) The employment of persons for the bottling of his stuff. Further he contended that the premises were fully licensed for about twenty years prior to last September. and during tbat time no objection or com- plaint had been lodged against the house so that it was practically a renewal of that license which was asked for.</p>
        <p>Mr Francis George Burns, of Barn-side, Queen's Park, St. Helens, gave evidence of the extent and nature of his business at Widnes and Runcorn, and said he was the occupier of the premises described as 65, High-street, Runcorn. He required the license more particularly so as to be able to simply his customers with small quan- tities, which the wholesale license would not allow him to do. At present he was forced to get his customers to send an orier upon a postcard to Widnes, whereupon he could supply them from kthere. But that method caused great inconvenience to both the customers and himself. He was prepared to give an undertaking that he was not going to do a" jug "trade at any time. Having spoken of the specialities he sold, and of the majority of licensed victuallers being tied to one kind of beer, and of the number of customers requesting the license, and of his ability to supply what other publicans could net</p>
        <p>Mr. Davies cross-examined applicant, in the course of which he admitted there wer,.. too many licensed houses in Runcorn. He had a a Is right to sell a certain com- modity which no one could get within a radius of 20 miles.—Mr. Davies : Just so. Don't you leok upon that as an equivalent to being tied P—Witness did not. He was not tied ; nor was he going to be.—Mr. Davies further poicted out that the license if granted would be a seven day one, and that tle magistrates could not alter it not even if they wished. True, it was an anomaly—but it was the law—the six days' limita- tion only referred to the" on " license, and not to the "off," so it was likely that a Sunday trade Would be done.—Witness stated he would give an undertaking that neither a " jug" trade nor a Sunday trade would be done. He simply wanted to be able to supply one, two, or half-a-dozen bottles to his customers more con- veniently. Mr. Davies then addressed the Court on behalf of the objectors, whom he stated obj,eed on public grounds, their contentions being that the house was not required ; and, on applicant's own showing, the customers could be supplied on the " gratis postcard system ; and if pnople were too lazy to fill one up and post it they did not deserve to be served. They had to face the admitted fact that there were too many licensed houses in Runcoro,and especially in the vicinity of the one the application would effect. These licensed premises were for the sale of "all kinds of intoxicating liquors " and not for any specific kind. Therefore, he contended, if these publicans were tied and restricted to the selling of one kind only, and did not do that for which they were grantel a great monopoly and privi- lege, he for one thought it was high time they were forced to do it. It was a very serious question that the</p>
        <p>TOBACCONISTS Commencing.—See Illustrated Guide 559 n4ges) 3d. Tobacconists' Outfitting Co. 186. Buston-road, London, Hiirdressers fitted,—Manager, H. MYER&amp; Advt</p>
        <p>OUR LOOA.L CRURCIIES.</p>
        <p>WYCLIFFE P.S.A. SOCIErY.-OPEN NIEErINCI</p>
        <p>Wycliffe Church was well filled on Sunday afternoon on the Occasion of the "open meeting " when members of the fair sex were admitted. Mr. W. El. J. Jenkins presided and said he congratulated himself upon having another opportunity of presiding over a P.S.A. meeting. It was a very wholesome dis:lipline to come into that pulpit. He would like to mention that it was the inten- tion of the committee who arranged the Christmas treat fo: children last year to have a similar treat this year. They were expecting to be in a position to provide a treat for at least 2,500 next Christmas. Such an under- taking could not be carried out without the generous support of the public generally, and he took that early opportunity of mentioning the matter with a view to enlisting their sympathies, The Chairman proceeded to refer to thb following sent- ence in a work by Mr. Richard Harris,which he termed a beautiful thought admirably expressed :—" The power that formed the eye to delight in beauty and the ear to music made also the heart to love justice, which is the perfect adjustment of all things in their manifold rela- tions. In the lowest sphere of our existence it cannot be perfectly administered by human agencies but to per- form this duty with the approval of our fellows is the highest aspiration of the not:lest mind." After pointing the lesson the author was endeavouring to teach, Mr. Jenkins said 30 or 40 years ago it would have been impossible for a member of the Church of England to preside at such a meeting as that he was now addressing. Things were improving gradually in this direction and it was such movements as the P.S.A. which were bringing it about. He hoped to live to see the time when Canon Willis would preach in that church and the Rev. Jahn Yonge at the Parish Church. It seemed extraordinary to him that in the Christian church such barriers existed.—The speaker for the occasion was Mr. Whalley, of Manchester. In the course of an excellent address he said that in their endeavour to do right and to make the world brighter and beter for their living in it, the very things which seemed to be hindrances or difficulties would be made means of grace and helps to things higher and better still.—Mr. W. H. Wallington sang two solos very feel- ingly, entitled "The King of Life my Shepherd is" and the "Crown of Calvary." Mr. W. H. Payton presided at the organ.—The service was also taken part in by Mr. Thomas Wallington and the Rev. J. Clough of Cadishead.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-09-01_WidnesExaminer_art0042" n="art0042">
        <head>Tag VICAR'S GALLANT CONDUCT.</head>
        <p>Tag VICAR'S GALLANT CONDUCT.</p>
        <p>A shocking bathing fatality occurred on Wednes- day last week, at Daresbury,near Warrington. Two schoolboys named John Greenhalgh, of Norton,and Edward Bodle, of Tayby Cottage, Acton Grange, were drowned in Betsy Nursery pit at Daresbury under distressing circumstances. It appears that about half-a-dozen schoolboys went for a bathe. John Greenhalgh got out of his depth, and it was at once seen that he mi.; in difficulties. One of his companions named Bodle gallantly went to his assistance, but he too got into difficulties, and both lads sank to the bottom of the pit before the eyes of their schoolfellows. Neither of them could swim. News of the sad affair was immediately conveyed to the school-house, an 4 a number of persons quickly assembled at the pond. Dragging operations were commenced, but without success, and eventually the Vicar of Daresbury divested himself of his clothing, and succeeded, after more than half an hour's dfort, in bringing both bodies to the bank. The inquest on the bodies was bald by Mr. J. E. Woraley at the Ring o' Bells last Thursday, Mr. C. W. Phillips being foreman of the jury. Pater Bodle, who said he was a foreman at a coal wharf, residing at Taylor Cottage, Acton Grange, stated that deceased, Edward Bodle, was his son and 12 years of age. Deceased left home at 8.15 a.m. on the 23rd inst to attend school, at Daresbury. Witness did not see him alive again. George Rovle, son of Roger Royle, of Preston Brook sail Baffle asked him to go and bathe and witness went with him as did also John Greenhalgh. Greenhalgh entered the water and they (Bodle and witness) tell him to come out as he could not swim. Bodle then went to him and Greenhalgh held him and both went under. Bodle was quite safe but went to save Greenhalgb. . _ _</p>
        <p>Henry Taprell Clark, Vicar of Ihresbury, said he received information and recovered the bodies. Wit- ness undressed and, being able to swica a little re- covered the bodies from the bottom of the pond. Tie was in the water about three quarters of an hour. Witnes recovered the second body about half an hour after the first.</p>
        <p>John Fryer, of Warford Farm, Norton, said the deceased, John Greenhalgh, who was 12 years of age,was his grandson. The deceased lad Greenhalgh was the son of James Groenhalgh, a machanic, but witness did not know whether the latter was living. lie did not know deceased went bathing. George Boyle, son of Mr. Roger Boyle, of Preston Brook, said he went to Daresbury school with deceased. Bodle asked him to go bathing. The latter went into the water and John Greenhalgh followed. The latter went into the deep part and went under. Bodle, who was in shallow water at the time, went to Greenhalgh's assistance. The latter got hold of Bodle and pulled him under,both going down. Witness went and told Mr. Wright, the schoolmaster.</p>
        <p>The Rev. H. T. Clark said the schoolmaster came and told him there were two boys drowning in the pond. Any person bathing in the pond would be a trespasser. Witness thought the depth of the pond would be about 12 feet. The Coroner, in addressing the jury, said in the case of John Greenbalgh the deceased was accident- ally drowned whilst bathing, and if they agreed with him they would return a verdict accordingly. With regard to Edward Bodle it was evident that this poor bny lost his life in a brave endeavour to save the life of his companion. It would be a last- ing consolation to his parents to remember that his death was caused by a very laudable attempt to save his companion's life. The Vicar of Daresbury was to be highly commended for the efforts which he made to recover the bodies by diving into the water and remaining therein, according to the evidence, for the space of three-quarters of an hour to an hour.</p>
        <p>The Jury agreed with the Coroner's remarks and expressed sympathy with the parents of the chil- dren. They considered that the Vicar of Daresbury had acted in a very humane and gallant manner. A verdict of "Accidental death" was returned.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-09-01_WidnesExaminer_art0043" n="art0043">
        <head>CLERGY DAUGHTERS SCHOOL.</head>
        <p>CLERGY DAUGHTERS SCHOOL.</p>
        <p>_ The following are the results obtained by stu- dents at this school in the Oxford Local Ex- aminations :—Senior Class lii: M. Lamb Passed; C F Adamson, Et Scott, E F Taylor —Junior: passed: Coulton, D E Norris, M G GF V Temoerlev, D V Temperley, R Wright, C C Wright. Passed (over age) C M C Cotton. E C Gibbon.—Preliminary: Passed: I Wilcockson, passed (over age), C M Collinson, N 141 II Fletcher, E II J Gerdiner, and M M Percival.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-09-01_WidnesExaminer_art0044" n="art0044">
        <head>CLARION VAN AT WARRINGTON.</head>
        <p>THE CLARION VAN AT WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>The Caroline Martyn Memorial Van in connection with the" Clarion " paid a two days' visit to War- rington on Saturday and Sunday and was stationed at Bridge Foot. It was in charge ot Mr. Willie Wright who delivered lectures on various aspects of Socialism before large gatherings. At the meeting held on Sunday morning his subject was the national- isation of mines. At the evening meeting there was an unusually large number present. "Clarion' cyclists attended from various parts of the district and rendered assistance in selling literature, &amp;c. They were entertained to tea at the Druids Hall by the local " Clarion " cyclists. The meeting commenced with the singing of "Eng- land Arise" which was heartily taken up. In the course of his lecture on " Christianity and Social- ism," Mr Wright combated the statement that all Socialists were Atheists. They believed in a gospel for the present as well as the future life, and were advocating the carrying out of Christ's principles in every day life. Collections were taken at each meeting and realised a satisfactory sum.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-09-01_WidnesExaminer_art0045" n="art0045">
        <head>ST. MARY'S CATHOLIC CHURCH, LATCHFORD.</head>
        <p>ST. MARY'S CATHOLIC CHURCH, LATCHFORD.</p>
        <p>Large congregations a,seinbled in this church on Sun- day, both morning and evening, when the annual sermons in aid of the schools were preached by the Very Rev. Father Andrew, 0.P., rector of St. Anne's Retreat, Sutton. He delivered two elovent addresses on sub- jects engaging attention at the present time, his sermon at the morning service being on " Religious Education " and in the evening on " Irreligious Education." In speaking on the last-named subject he stated that what blindness was to a man in the physical world so ignoranee was to a man in the intellectual and moral world. In fact all the evils of the earth could be traced to ignorance. Whilst the whole civilised world cried out for education —the statesman, the churchman, the priest, the philosopher, the Catholic and the non-Ca.tholic—the question arose, "In whom is vested the right of educa- tion?" The 19th century declared to them that the State was the educational authority, and that the parent and the church must stand aside. They were told to ad- minister education of a purely secular kind, and that no form of religion must be expounded. That was where the difficulty arose. Every child born into this world was born a member of two societies. There was the society of the family circ,e, and there was a state or civil society. They as Catlnlici maintained that the right of education was vested in the hands of the parents. In proof of this contention he stated that nature adapted the parent and pointed out the right of the parent to educate the child. It did not follow that the State had nothing whatever to do with the educ tion of the ch;ldren. The State had a duty to perform, and that was to provide all the materials necessary fmr a thorough education. Secular education simply was not education in the com- plete sense of the word. It was not enough to educate children in reading, writing, arithmetic and other secu- lar subjects. There was something far above human science ; there was the will whieh controlled the moral life;and the heart and the affections of the heart. Hence if they wished the child to be thoroughly educated, not only must the intellect be strengthened and perfected to fiehtthe battle of life but the will, the heart and the affections must b 3 subjected to the influence of religion. A man might be a very wise man and shine as a great literary man or as a philosopher but he might also be dishonest, impure, or one who polluted his own home with all kinds of vice and be a disgusting member of society. If religion were severed from human science there was nothing to control the passions of their nature. It was the duty of parents to educate their childre i's hearts and souls. .Religion must be taught in childhood's days, for when children grew into man's estate it was almost impossible to instil religion into them. The preacher pictured the ill Welch was likely to follow a purely secular education, and in conclusion appealed , for generous support in carrying on religious education in connection with their schools.—Special musical ser- vices were effiaiently given. There was a full orchestra both morning and evening under the leadership of Mr. R. H. Pearson. Mr. Peter Bate, the esteemed organist and choirmaster was the conductor,and for this occasion Mr. John Eckersley presided at the organ. At the morn- ing service Weber's mass in E flat was given for the first time in Warrington by an orchestra. Another interest- ing feature was the performance of an orchestral sketch composed by Ur. F. H. Crossley, the conductor of the Warrington Musical Society. Mendeissohn's Volun- tary March from "Athalit" was also given. , The solo parts were efficiently taken by the Misses Eastens, Eckersley, Haelehurst, Caldwell, Messrs. Joseph M. Bate and J. W. Bibby. Mr. Crossley's orchestral sketch was the initial item at the evening ser- vice. Miss A. Eckersley afterwards sang "Ave Maria" (Cherubini) with bassoon obligate by Mr. P. Walsh. The other pieces played were "Evultet " (S. Moorat) " Benediction Service No. 7," .(Father Murphy) and a concluding voluntary by the choir—" 12th Gloria ' (Mond). The soloists were Misses Hazlehurst, Ross and Mr. J. M. Bate. The musical portioa of the ser- vices was most efficiently gives and was greatly ap- preciated.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-09-01_WidnesExaminer_art0046" n="art0046">
        <head>I RUNCORN OWNER'S NEW COASTING</head>
        <p>I RUNCORN OWNER'S NEW COASTING</p>
        <p>STEAMER.</p>
        <p>On Thursday last week the steamer Blanche proceeded up the Firth from Ayr to the measured mile,at Skelmorlie, for her trial trip. The Blanche is a fine screw-steamer ot the raised-quarter-deck type, constructed in accordance with the most modern ideas for the coasting trade. She has been built by the well-known firm of S. M'Bnight and Company, Limited, of Ayr.for Mr. William Rowland of Runeorn and Liverpool. The registered dimensions of the vessel are 145 by 2415 by 10•05,aud she is capable of carrying over 400 tons of Fargo on the small net register tonnage of 61 tons ; while at the same time her capacity is such as to give her a large ratio of cutic feet per ton deadweight. The Blanche has been con- structed in excess of the highest class at Lloyd's, under their special survey, and her bottom has been extra strengthened so far as to obviate any danger from grounding in port. She is fitte I with powerful steam steering gear on the bridge midships, also steam winches, steam warping capstan aft and windlass for anchor-gear forward. She has excellent steering capabilities, the trials in this respect showing that when going at full speed she was able with the helm hard over to make a complete circle in about twice her own length, this being a necessity for the owners' trade when passing up canals, &amp;c. In order that a true measure of the vessel's steaming quali- ties when in service might be obtained it was arranged between the owner and the builders that a loaded trial should be given. The vessel, therefore, received her cargo at Ayr, and on the measured mile four runs were taken at the highest speed, two with and two against the tide, when a mean speed of eleven knots was at- tained. The machinery has been supplied by Messrs. Ross and Duncan, engineers of Govan, and has also been constructed under Lloyd's rules for the highest class. It consists of a set of cornoouned surface condeF.sing engines, having cylinders 18 and 49 by 27 incheo stroke, supplied with steam from a large horizontal return tube marine boiler, the ample capacity of which was shown by the fact that steam was blowing off at the safety- valve during the whole of the trial. The workmanship generally is of the highest claes—in fact, in many res- pects in excess of what is usual in this type of vessel— and during the trial the machinery worked with exceed- leg smoothness. The owner and a large party of inter- ! ested friends were on board, and expressed their ' satisfaction at the results attained. After completion of the trials, the vessel proceeded on her maiden v.)yaee to Liverpool.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-09-01_WidnesExaminer_art0047" n="art0047">
        <head>"i 5 I NIVICK.</head>
        <p>"i 5 I NIVICK.</p>
        <p>CRICKET AT WINWICK.—.I match was played in the Park at Winwick on Saturday, between tho contractors (Manchester Office staff) and a team composed of the workmen engaged in the erection of the ra,'w asylum buildings. After an exciting contest the visiting team won by two runs, the scores being—home team 67, visiting team 69. Subsequently a half-hour a-side match was played, with the result that the vistlng team again won. Scores—visitors, 51 for four wickets ; borne team 44 for three wickets. The not rble features of the match were the brilliant batting of Mr Alan Neill for the visit- ing team, who scored 32 not out, the wicket keeping of Mr. E. R. Thomas, and the b)wling of Robinson for tho home team. This gentleman was responsible for the greater number of the wickets of the visitt.rs, and made what would otherwise have been a virtual walk-over for the visiting team a neck and-aeck race. Subsequently the teams and friends atijiurned to the Swan Inn where as the guests of Mr. R. W. Neill, they partook of tea, and subsequently an entertainment was held. The meeting broke up at 8.30 p.m., the visiting team and friends returrainE to Manchester by the 8.50 train.</p>
      </div>
