      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-09-01_WidnesExaminer_art0050" n="art0050">
        <head>WARRINGTON.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>YOUNG MEN'S CHRISTIAN ASSOCIATION.-P3A,</p>
        <p>M RETING. A meeting was held in the Lecture Hail on Sun- day afternoon, presided over by Ur. W. Clegg. Mr. W. A Sandford gave a thoughtful address. Mr Levi Richardson rendered two solos with his usual. ability. The President referred to the severe illness of Mr. David Stone, a late member, and voiced the sympathy of the members with him and his friends in their time of sorrow. Mr. Drinkwater and Mr. Taker also took part in the service.</p>
        <p>The proprietors of the Royal:Oak Soap Works tender their apologies to their patrons for any inconvenience that may have been felt in getting their Coupons for free Tablets of Britannia Herb Soap exchang.sd. This has been due to the excessive call for these Tablets and the makers beg to intimate that they have now very considerably increased their plant in this department to cope with the growing demand so that no further delay will be experience?. They ake this opportunity of mentioning that they have now ncroduced a best Pink Carbolic Soap under the brand "Giant Oak" with which the free tablets of Herb Soap are given saute as with the Royal Oak. Everybody should use hese high class soaps,—Advt. UNDER THE CLOCK GOOD BOOTS.—Advt.</p>
        <p>L&amp;DIES BEST GLACE BOOTS AND SHOES UNDER THE CLOCK. 'BRIDGE-STREET.—Advt. Y. Isl C.A. AT GRAPPENHALL HEYES.</p>
        <p>Lovely weather favoured the members of the Warrington Y.M.C.A.. on Saturday afternoon, when by the kind invitation of Mr. J. U. Parr, J.P., D.L., president of the Association, they journeyed to Grappenhall Heyes. The party numbered 140 per- sons who were conveyed to this charming Cheahire retreat in 'buses and on arrival the proceedings took the form of a garden party. The visitors were received by the host and hostess, Mr. and Mrs. Parr, and were then made welcome to all the enjoyments of tie residence and its sur- roundings. Of these the members and their friends freely availed themselves and the afternoon sped all too quickly, amid these bright surroundings. There seemed to be pleasures to suit every taste. There were the well-stocked fish ponds for the disciples of lzaac Waltou,the beautifully kept lawn for the nimble-footed tennis players, and for the more meditative minds, there were quiet nooks and shady walks through the sylvan grounds where the übiquitious " snap- shotters " found ample material for the exercise of their an. The pleasure of the company was further enhanced by the sweet strains of the St. Paul's band which took up its position in front of the hall. Whilst the mental enjoyments of the visitors were so well catered for, their physical re- quirements were not overlooked, for during the afternoon refreshments were served in a marquee at the rear of the house. When all were satisfied,Mr. John Fairclough, 'LP., arose and proposed a vote of thanks to the host and hostess for their kindness in inviting the association to their home anti in contributing so largely to their enjoyment. The proposition was carried with acclamation and Mr. Parr on behalf of his wife and himself suitably acknowledged the vote of thanks. Tea over the company again dispersed over the grounds and as the sun lost his power and the evening shades ap- peared, they made their way to the 'buses and were conveyed home, after having spent a most enjoy- able afternoon. CYCLES,—Speci al Ladies and Gents Machines at £7 15s. See Windows, J. S. GARLAND, Bridge-st., Warrington. W. T. WILLIAMS' SALE is now proceeding, Millinery Mantles. Dresses. Silks. Prints, Muslins. Blouses, Shirts, Ties, Sunshades. Sm.. at greatly reduced prices. 74. SANKEY-gTREET. Warrington.— Advt. BEST BOOTS FOR ALL. UNDER THE CLOCK.— Advt. BURNING FATALITY AT WARRINGTON—RESULT OP</p>
        <p>PLAYING WITH MATCHES.</p>
        <p>A dii-tressing burning fatality was reported to the police on Monday It appears that Maud Banner aged two years, daughter ot William Banner, joiner, of 7, New-street, Howley, was lett alone in the kitchen on Saturday atternom. She took some matches from the dresser, and her mother shortly afterwards missing her, came into the room and was alarmed to see her child running through the back door with titr clothes on fire. The flames were extinguished, and the girl was found to be very badly burned about the left arm, neck, and legs. She was attended by Or. Smith, but died at 7.15 on Monday morning. The child and its parents were deaf and dumb.—The inquest was held at the Police Station on Tuesday by Mr. S. Brighouse, county coroner.—John Thomas Banner, brother of the deceased's father, said that Banner, and his wife and child were deaf and dumb. Banner was away from home when the accident happened.— Margaret Atherton, 12, Mason-street, said she knew Mr. and Mrs. Banner. At ten minutes to two on Saturday afternoon, she was in the yard chopping sticks when she heard screams. She saw Mrs. Banner in the back, holding the child in her arms. Her clothes were smoking. Witness tore the clothes off the child's back and took her ta the docuor. She was present when the child died on Monday morning. From what the motlaer,who could speak a little, had made her understand, she (Mrs Banner) was washing pots in the back kitchen. The child, who was in another room, had got a chair, stood on it,and taken some matches from a drawer and struck them. Missing the girl, the mother went into the parlour, but did not find her there. On coming back into the kitchen she saw the child coming into the house, with her clothes in flames.—A verdict of "Accidental Death" was returned, the jury express- ing their sorrow the. Mr. and Mrs. Banner had lost their child.—This was conveyed to Mr. Banner in the deaf and dumb alphabet by his brother. Mr. Banner in a like manner acknowledged the ex- pression of sympathy.</p>
