      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-09-01_WidnesExaminer_art0063" n="art0063">
        <head>TIM EXAMINER.</head>
        <p>TIM EXAMINER.</p>
        <p>SEPTEMBER Ist 1899,</p>
        <p>VISCOUNT HALCFAX, who is Ecclesiastical in effect the lay leader of Anarchy. the Ritualistic party in the Church of England, has issued to the members of the Church Union a lengthy manifesto on the re- cent decision of the Archbishops touching the Ceremonial use of incense and candles. Lord Halifax has previously uttered more than one note of defiance of the constituted authorities in the Anglican Church. when their attitude was adverse to the more extreme practices,of the High Church -clergy. The tone of the present manifesto is,how- ever,not so aggressively revolutionary as that of a previous declaration. Its main contentions appear to be two: first, to assure the lay members of the Union that the Archbishops are not infallible—a Point it seems unnecessary to labour; and,secondly, that their decision on the incense question is not absolutely binding. Congregations, he says,should stand by their priests on the matter. If the priests yield on the question "grudgingly, and because of the present distresF,and under moral compulsion," well, let the congregations also submit, yet strictly "without prejudice to whatever future action may be thought wise and right." But, whether priests deem it wise to submit or not, "stand by them." That is the sum of Lord HALIFAX'S counsel. The manifesto, though mild in tone, aims at the negation of all authority in the Church of England save, apparently, that of the individual priest. It is very easy to pick holes in the late archiepisco- pal decision. Admittedly,it was not the judgment of a legally constituted court. But Lord HALIFAX'S Point, that it was an opini 3n delivered "after a 'hearing' rather than a 'trial,'" strikes us as being little more than a quibble. It had all the moral authority, if not the legal force, of a judgment by the highest ecclesiastical authorities In the land. Yet Lord HALIFAX tells the Priests to please themselves about observ- ing it. This advice, and indeed the whole tenour of the manifesto, makes for ecclesias- tical Anarchism within the Establishment, The lay head of the Ritualists practically repudi- ates all existing authority. He will not recognise the right of any civil court to control the clergy In matters of doctrine or ritual. He has said, rather Disestablishment than Erastianism. But neither will he cousel submission—except grudg- ingly and under pressure—to any purely ecclesias- tical tribunal. What authority, then, will Lord HALIFAX recognise ? He says nothing of the or- dination oath taken by each priest to adhere to the Prayer-book. Is that" authority "also to be dis- regarded? Though to the priests Lord HALIFAX says in effect : "Please yourselves," he counsels the laity to obey their priests. It comes to this, then: each priest within the Church of England may be a law unto himself, but congregations are to have no option but to submit to whatever ritual the priests may impose upon them. We hardly think that interpretation will answer. It only makes the situation more difficult. Perhaps, after all, nothing but disestablishment will cut this knot.</p>
        <p>THE inaugural address de- Modern Sanitation. Moses and livered by Sir W. H.PREEcE, at the opening of the Sani- tary Congress at .Southampton, was at once scientific, popular and comprehensive. Sir WILLIAM did not confine himself to his own science of electricity; he dealt with sanitary en- gineering in all its varied applications to the pre- servation of life and health. We have made many advances in sanitary, as all other, science in late days. Yet even here there is nothing new under the sun, and Sir WILLIAM PREECE boldly pro- claimed that MOSES was at once the " greatest sanitary engineer the world has ever known "and the father of modern sanitation. The doctrines of MOSES are in the ascendant still, for they are based upon principles which the discoveries of -science have proved to be sound and enduring The essentials of life, to secure which the Mosaic Code of laws was framed, are the aim of the sani- tary engineer to-day. They are summed up succinctly by Sir WILLIAM P.REECE as: (1) Pure air, (2) pare water, (3) pure food, (4) pure soil, (5) pure dwellings—cleanliness, (6) proper Clothing, (7) warmth. Our social legislation is Mainly directed to securing these ends. All that science has taught us has reference to the methods by which Nature herself works, and by following Which we may more readily secure these essentials. Sir WILLIAM PREECE went further, and pointed Out that in certain matters, such as the use of re- fuse destructors, we are only now learning to follow MOSES'S lead. Moss knew nothing of bacteriology; he never heard of the übiquitous baicrobe, still less had he ever seen him. Yet modern bacteriology has shown us the correctness of his code of purification. Sir WILLIAM PREECE, without departing from the foundation of Mosaic principles, made many sug- gestions as to improvements still desirable for Mtaining a higher standard of national health. We need to give more attention to the ventilation of our dwellings, we are too fearful of" draughts " ; we want electricity in the home, to prevent its air being vitiated by gas and oil lamps, which consume oxygen; we should use sea-water to Purify our cities, and then we should have more "'ash water available for domestic purposes; we Ileed to guard more carefully against the infection that lurks in such an article of diet as milk—which should be sterilised by boilingbefore use; and the septic" or biological treatment of sewage may iszlable us in time to solve what is probably the ast and most difficult problem of the sanitary</p>
        <p>engineer. In regard to the art of living, we may, look forward to a good time coming; but, atter all, we can only more perfectly apply the sanitary teachings of Moses.</p>
        <p>The yacht race for the America Cup is exciting a large amount of interest, and the hopes and fears of the contestants constitute a pleasurable element in yachting circles. After the failure of Lord Dunraven to win the cup, and the wrangling which followed, it was hardly thought likely that any other yachtsman from this side of the Atlantic would venture to enter for the race. Sir Thomas Lipton, however, has been bold enough to build a yacht expressly for the competition,which has duly crossed the Atlantic for the purpose, the spirited owner having followed it in the Campania. The terms of the competition make it necessary that the foreign yacht shall sail across the Atlantic, and the Shamrock has partly done this, and has been towed a portion of the way. No objection has been raised to these proceedings,and the Shamrock is now being got ready for the work she has to perforott The yacht is built of some light metal to a special design, and American critics are by no means agreed as to build or her chances. Sailing in American waters is not the same thing as sailing in our own waters. The airs are lighter, more canvas can be carried, and some of the other conditions differ considerably. The Columbia is the American yacht the Shamrock will have most to fear from, and hr owner, Mr. Iselin, is bent on getting the best possible speed out of her. The Shamrock is evidently a fine boat, and we shall all be pleased if she should be steered to victory. A fair race is what all true yachts- men desire.</p>
        <p>How stands the record as regards shipwreck and loss of life at sea ? Is the tale increasing or de- creasing ? Last winter was a disastrous one; we had several calamitous shipwrecks, attended by heavy loss of life, around our costs. These do not appear in the rather belated annual return of shipping casualties just issued by the Board of Trade, the figures given being for the year ending June 30th, 1898. There was in that year, however a very gratifying reduction, both in total losses of British vessels and in lives sacrificed. The number of vessels totally lost was 419, which is 89 lower than in the previous year, and 28 lower than in 1895-6. Compared with the average of twenty-two years the decrease is 181. The number of lives lost in British vessels at sea was 681 in the past year, of which 629 were crew and 52 passengers. The number of seamen lost was lower than in any lof the previous twenty-one years, and the number of passengers .lower than in any of those years but five. Compared with the average losses of twenty-two years, the figures shew a decrease of 864 seamen and 199 passengers. The sacrifice of life is still heavy, but it appears to be satisfactorily diminishing. Unfortunately, now and again a winter comes when a great disaster takes place and runs up the total. Thus the loss of life in 1895-6 was swollen by the 247 lives lost on the Drummond Castle, and 277 by the sinking of the On Wo, of Lond 311, by collis•on with the Nowchwanct. We are afraid last winter's disasters will cause the 1898-9 figures to mount again.</p>
        <p>The Warrington Licensing Committee on Thurs- day decided to grant the application of the War- rington Corporation for the reconstruction of the Feathers Hotel. We are sorry that the committee di A not act upon tbe suggestion which was made and once for all rid the Corporation of this un- desirable incubus by allowing the license to lapse. By granting the transfer of the license the Licensing Committee have given their sanction to increased drinking facilities for the neighbourhood inasmuch as the small and decrepit hotel which has existed there is to be replaced by a huge drinking establishment with all its allurements. As was aptly pointed out by Mr. F.R. Roberts, this will embolden the owners of the other liceosed houtes in Bridge-street who are affected by the street improvement scheme, to fol- low suit, and we shall therefore have three more fine and "commodiouehotels brought to the street front within a stone's throw of each other. No one will venture to think that such a large number are necessary for the needs of the neighbourhood, and if they are not, then the magistrates are fail- ing in their duty in granting licenses for them.</p>
        <p>The Chief Constable reports a marked increase of drunkenness in the Borough. The number of charges of drunkenness heard by the magistrates last year was 276 whilst this year the prosecutions total up to 418. This is an increase of more than fifty per cent. and calls for special consideration. The Chief Constable admits the difficulty of accounting accurately for the fluctuations of such statistics but he holds good trade and extensive building operations in the town and neighbourhood as largely responsible for the increase. The figures in themselves, as we have on previous occasions pointed out, are no criterion as to the amount of drunkenness which takes place in the town. Unless a drunken person becomes obstreperous his name seldom finds its way on the police books. Anyone who has seen Warrington streets on a Saturday night cannot fail to have noticed the great amount of drunkenness amongst both men and women. To presume thnt the year's drunken- ness in Warrington is adequately represented by 400 cases at the Police Court is simply absurd. We have no desire to painkthe character of the town in its darkest colours, but there are some people who will persist in whitewashing it, and when challenged, screen themselves behind the Chief leonstable's report. Another suggestive fact in the report is that despite the large increase in the number of persons summoned for being drunk not a single license holder was summoned for per- mitting it.</p>
        <p>Those who read our report of the sad drowning fatalities at Daresbury the other day will notice that two acts of great courage mark the occur- rence, though unhappily one of the actors in the tragedy lost his life. Some school boys went to bathe in a pond, the extreme depth of which was about 12 feet. One lad named Greenhalgh got into the deep end and being unable to swim went under the water. His school-fellow, Bodle, also unable to swim, like a little hero, went to his assistance and both lost their lives. Dragging operations proved ineffectual. The Vicar of Daresbnry arriving on the scene divested him- self of his clothing, and though only able to swim a little, tried to recover the bodies and after an hour's desperate and dangerous exertion succeeded in bringing both to land. These brave acts prove that courage is stilt a dominant quality with Englishmen notwithstanding their matter of fact lives. All will sympathise with the relatives of the unfortunate lads but in the case of Bodle, the relatives sorrow must be tempered by the fact that he lost his life through a deed of signal courage.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-09-01_WidnesExaminer_art0064" n="art0064">
        <head>RUNCORN'S CHANCES AUAINST WIGAN.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN'S CHANCES AUAINST WIGAN.</p>
        <p>[By THE "SCORCHER:I</p>
        <p>Saturday next will see the "linnets" opposing Wigan,in the Northern Union Lancashire Champion- ship (first division), at Wigan, and the general question stems to be "What are Runcorn's chances? Well, I think they should be two " points up." Judgiag by the practice matches at Runcorn during the week,Runcorn give strong evidences of opening in a style at least worthy of the days gone by, and are certain to give a good account of tbemselve4 After many quibbles and the usual amount of timidity the selection committee have issued their team, as follows :—S Houghton (full back), D Jones, J Butterworth, T Warder,T Wilkinson (three- quarter backs) ; J Jolley, and J Faulkner (half back ) ; Taylor, 8 Walkcx, F Darlington, J Lang- ey 8 Oayter, W Lightfoot, H Farmer, and Tomlinson. with Owen Hughes as reserve (Inwards). The selection appears to have given general satisfaction though personally I sh oul I have liked to 803 0. Hughes figure in the place of Langley against Wigan, he being in my opinion till intter man against that notorious rough team, but perhaps the cocrmittee have kept him' in store for Swinton on Wednesday next. Run- corn's previous performances against Wigan (last year), are: at Wigan. Runcorn wcu 4 to 3 ; and at Runcorn, they won by 25 points to none. Against Swinton on Wednesday next, there should also be a further march upward if Runcorn are going to main- tain their recotd of beating Swinton at Runaorn, tbough Swinton always, or nearly so, turn the tables in the return engagement.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-09-01_WidnesExaminer_art0065" n="art0065">
        <head>EXAMINER, FRIDAY SEPTEMBER 1, 1899</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, FRIDAY SEPTEMBER 1, 1899</p>
        <p>AN ENGINE DRIVER IN TROUBLE.</p>
        <p>At Widnes Police Court. on Saturday, before Major Lewis, Sam Rumhull, of 94, Kendrick-sreet, Seaforch, was charged witb being drunk on Friday night.—P.C. Dickinson stated that at a quarter past six on Friday night, he received a complaint that a man was drunk in charge of a traction engine in Liverpool-road, Dittun. On going to the spot he saw Rumhull repairing the engine, which he had run into a hedge. He was then druak.—P.C. Hindley said that at six o'clock he received a complaint that a traction engine and waggon were blocking the road near Hough Green. He found the engine lying crossways. and the defendant was trying to repair it. The chain was broken, and he was unable to look after it. Defendant got on the box while his man was under- neath, and tried to start it.—lt was state' that prisoner wished to see a doctor respecting his condition. This he was allowed to do, and the doctor certified that the man was not capable to look after a locomotive.—The Magistrate, who was an eye- witness of the whole occurrence, said that he was quite convinced that the constable's evidence was correc.:.—Defeadant in defence pleaded that he bad only two pints at dinner, and owing to an injury received some time ago to his heal, it had taken effect.—Tne bench imposed a fine of 103 and costs.</p>
        <p>...............41........</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-09-01_WidnesExaminer_art0066" n="art0066">
        <head>RUNCORN AND WIDNES.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN AND WIDNES.</p>
        <p>AN UNENDURABLE STENCII AT WIDNES.</p>
        <p>At Widnes yesterday morning, B. W. Connell, jun., tallow manufacturer, was sumrrione.i by the Corporation for causing a nuisance by carrying on an offensive trade. The defendant receives offal from Birkenhead lairages, and the boiling process emitted a sickening efflavia. The case had been adjourned to allow him to remedy the nuisance, but this had not been done, and he was fined £2 and six Ruineas costs, and for ctusing the nuisance since the adj iurnment, 24 an six guineas costs.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-09-01_WidnesExaminer_art0067" n="art0067">
        <head>TRAP ACCIDENT IN CHURCH-STREET.</head>
        <p>TRAP ACCIDENT IN CHURCH-STREET.</p>
        <p>An accidetit, which luckily was unattended by serious results, was that which occurred in Church-street, Run- cord, on Wednesday morning. As Mr. A. Abel, of Green way-road, was driving with a friend, the pony shied when opposite r 'ckett's shop, wad forcing itself from the harness, caused the trap to fall so that the oc- cupants were pitched out. No one was,, bur:, and the only damage done was to the trap and harness which suffered slightly. COMMENCING ON THE WE ONG ROAD .—TH REE GIRLS</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-09-01_WidnesExaminer_art0068" n="art0068">
        <head>IMP RISONED .</head>
        <p>IMP RISONED .</p>
        <p>At the Runcorn Police C mrc, on Saturday, before Mr. F. J. Norman, E,izabeth Jackson, aged 17, Elizabeth Parker, 18, and Sarah Ann Hughes, 19, wore charged with disorderly behaviour at Top Locks, High-street. on Friday night.—P.C.'s Fry and Bowyer proved the charge. Hughes who is an old offender, was committed to gaol for 14 days' hard labour, Parker, 10 days' and Jackson 7 days' hard labour, with a view to separating them. The presiding magistrate requested the police to communicate with a lady in Che.ster with a view to getting the prisoners into a home, so that they might be saved from a life of degradation. FIRE AT SUTTON WEAVER.—COTTAGES DESTROYED.</p>
        <p>Last Friday afternoon the that:lied roof of three old cottages on the Chester highway at Sut:on Weaver caught fire by means of a spark from the chimney and being exceedingly dry the roof was soon in a mass of flames. The families and their furniture were quickly removed. An old man named James Antrobus, who had been bedridden for 14 years, was found in his bed- ro om endeavouring to perform the unusual operation of clothing himself, and he too was carried to a place of safety. The Runcorn Fire Brigade were on the spot with admirable promptitude, but were unable to save the cottages, which were gutted. The property belonged to Mr. H. Talbot, of Aston Hall, and the damage is esti- mated at about £2OO. COLLISION IN THE SHIP CANAL.</p>
        <p>On Thursday morning as the steam-tug St. Winifred, wis towing flats down the canal, when opplsite the Bridgewater Lock, at Runcorn, she passed the tug " Helen" also towing flats. The steamers passed each other safely, but the flat " Snaefell," the last of the St. Winifrel's line of boats, fouled the " Helen." with the result that the flat's side was almost staved in. The damaged craft was, however, speedily docked, and her Argo of crates discharged before any further damage was done. Had she attempted her journey, there is no doubt but that she would have sunk. SIR jOHN BRUNNER AT WINSFORD,—ANOTHER GENEROUS GIFT.</p>
        <p>On Wednesday night Mr.E. Hamlett,J.P.,announced to the Winsford Urban Council that Sir John Brunner,Bait., M.P., had intimated his intention to defray the entire cost of furnishing throughout the newly-erected Wins- ford Guildhall, which is Sir John's jubilee gitt t Wins. ford. The announcement was receiyrd with much gratification. The Gui,dhill. which is a handsome structure,and one of the finest of its kind in the country, will be opened on Novemb?.r 4 by Sir John, who has promised to entertain the trade and friendly societies to ti aon that day. EKteusiye preparations are in progress for publicly receiving the gift. --</p>
        <p>DEATH AND FUNERAL OF A VENERABLE METHODIST. 0 a Saturday, there was interred at the hist)ric church of Aston-by-Sutton the mortal remains of James John- son, aged 84, who had ben a familiar figure in the dis- trio by reason of his official connection with Wesleyan Methodism. Deceased for many years carried on the business of blacksmith at the Norton warehnuse,Preston Brook. He held office as choirmaster at Preston Brook Chapel, and was highly relpected by everyone who was brought in contact with him. The sole2an obsequies on Saturday were attended by representatives of tne Wes- leyan Church and a large number of parsonal friends.</p>
        <p>DISGRACEFUL SCENE IN HIGR-STREET, RUNCORN</p>
        <p>Oa Wednesday, before Mr. G. L. Davies, at the Run- corn Police Court, John Flaherty, described as a harvest-man, was br melt up in custody charged with being drunk in High-street, the previous day.—He pleaded guilty.—Mr.Davies : You were here yesterday, and were fined 24 61 for being drunk P—Prisoner : Yes, sir.—Mr. Johnson : He promised to leave 'he town, but must have immediately gone and got drunk after leav- ing the Court far I saw him with his clothes in a dis- graceful condition--Police Constab'e Wilson stated he found the man drunk in High-street. His clothes were very much disarranged, and the prisoner was so help- le sly drunlo,that he had to prop him against the wall whilst he rearraneed his clothes for decency's sake. Sub- sequently he locked him up.—Prisoner : I am quite ashamed of myself, sir.—Mr. Davies: You will be tined 104. or 7 days.—Prisoner said he would pay if given time.</p>
        <p>MERSEY MISSION TO SEAMEN,</p>
        <p>The Rev. Jam Fdll, who for tho last five years has been a missionary at San-Francisco A.S.A. 'occupied the pulpits of the Parish Church, and St. Michaels All Angels, Runcorn ma Suniay last, when he preached eloquently on behalf of the Mission to Seamen. He said there were two texts applicable to the subject of Mission to Seamen with respect to the British Empire :—" She etretched forth her boughs onto the sea,aod her branches to the rivers," and " She was founded on the seas and prepared upon the floods." They were texts, which fully set out the claims that the British seamen had to the consideration of those ol shore. Having shown how the British Empire was built up, and kept up, by the Royal and Merchantile Navy, the Rey. gentleman, con- cluded with a most pathotic appeal for the where withall to continue to provide attractions, other than the streets and publichouses, where the sailor from home might find a friendly shelter and adviser. The congregations were large at both places of worship. THEFT FROM A FELLOW-WORKMAN AT RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>Norman and Mr. J. Ockleston were the pre. siding magistrates at an occasional court held at Run corn on Thursday morning, when Harvey Fenimmons was brought up before them on a charge of stealing tw° pair of scicsors, valued ss,the property of William Parr. on the 30'h of August.—Parr stated that the scissors were his property and valued at 54. Fitz+imcnons was a fellow worker of his and both were emplovel by Mr. John Yates, tailor, Church-street, Runcorn. Witness had left the scissors in the workshop, and on Wednesday p3isoner went to the workshop and got the scissors which he afterwards pledged in Liahtfoot's pawnshop. Thomas Brown testified to the prisonee pledg- ing the articles as stated.—Police Constable Carrington gave evidence of the prisoner's apprehension and of the recovery of the scissors.— Prisoner stated he had committed the act as alleged. He was hard up and did not mein to steal the articles, as he fully intended to redeem them and restore them to the workshop.—The Magistrates, taking a lenient view of the case. bound prisoner over in the sum of £5 to appear for judgment if called upon within the next six months.</p>
        <p>BRUNSWICK WESLEY GUILD FOOTBALL CLUB.</p>
        <p>The—no-e7nWs—of the Brunswick Wesley Guild Foot- ball Club Runerrn. on Wednesday .evening promoted a most successful social gathering with tha objlct of augumenting their funds, at the Brunswick-st. School- room, when about 80 of their friends and supporters assembled. The proceedings commenced with a capitally served tea. Provided by those excellent caterers. Mr. and Mrs. Nancollis- During the feasting, Mr. George Johnson sang " Hearts of Oak," the refrain being hev tily joined in by those who were not too heftily engaged otherwise. Mr. Harry Dodd followed with a selected song of hunaourous stra,n. Then came the item of the evening. being Mr. A. Casson's selection on the " Fairy bells." an item which was loudly redemanded: Tea being by this time fairly cleared off,numerous games were indulged in. A double q •srte't9 party consisting of Messrs W J Loathwood, A Hollin.on, H Dodd, S Dutton, George Johnson, J Simkin, T Watson, and A Price contributed "Jingle bells." Then foliowed a dessert of fruit, and another round of games, the party eventually dispersing at about 10.30. The accompanist as Mr. W. L atlawood ; and the M.C.'s. Messrs. Loath. wood and H. Dodd. The secretarial duties were effi- ciently discharged by Mr.J.Simkin,and those of treasurer by Mr. W. J. 149athwood. We understand the object of th gathering was fully realised, and we also wish the club a successful season. RUNCORN FREE LIBRARY COMMITTEE.</p>
        <p>The monthly meeting of the committee was held at the Town Hall on Thursday evening, last week. Mr. W. Handley presided, and there were present—Messrs. S Pope, W Hankinsin, T Riles', F Hutton.D Bishrown,and J D Jones (librarian). The librarian submitted the annual report, a .copy of which appeared in our last issue.—The committee appointed to consider the advisa- bility of holding a course of lectures expressed the opinion that such a course would be helpful to the read- ers and the following gentlemen were mentioned as likely leeturers :—Mr. Stainer, headmaster of the , Widnes Higher Grade School ; Mr. T A Lawrenson, 8.A., headmaster Runcorn Technical Schools; Rev. Canon Maitlaud Wood. M.A., Rev. C W L Christian, Rev J Osborne, 8.A.., Dr Finlay McDougall, Mr F M Jackson, Manchester Mr James Wilding. sur- veyor to the Runcorn Urban District Council; Mr A Lancaster, librarian, St. Helens ; Mr W Handley, chair- man of the Runcorn Free Library Committee ; Mr New- stead. curator, Grosvenor Museum. Chester; Mr Made- ley, Warrington Museum; Mr H E Marston, Runcorn Ttrhnical Institute. It was decided that the librarian should write to these gentlemen with a view to securing their co-operation. Friday evening at eight o'clock was recommended as being the most suitable time for the lectures. It was resolved that Mr F Wiswall, chairman of the Urban District Council, be invited to take the chair at the first lecture, and that the Technical In- struction Committee be asked to place the new wing of the Institute at the disposal of the Lecture 4 Committee. The committee decided to take steps to secure a course of G:lchrist Lcctures for 1901..</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-09-01_WidnesExaminer_art0069" n="art0069">
        <head>DETERMINED SUICIDE AT RUNCORN. ---- HORRIFYING SPECTACLE.</head>
        <p>DETERMINED SUICIDE AT RUNCORN. ---- HORRIFYING SPECTACLE.</p>
        <p>A. MAN'S THROAT cur FROM EAR TO EAR.</p>
        <p>An inquest was held at the Runcorn Police Station, last Saturday morning, en the body of Tnomas Hodgette, aged 42. a married man, who resided apart from he; wife at 10, White-street, Runcorn, and who was found lying with his throat cut, by his sister, on Friday morning. Ann Hodgetts, 10, White-street, Runcorn, stated deceased was her brother; was 42 years of age, and resided with her mother at 10, White-street. He nal a labourer' and when employed, woried at the Chemical Works. Deceased was a married man, but lived apart from his wife. He and his wife were very friendly, visiting one another from time to time. In May last, deceased met with an accident, when he fell from a bridge at Rock Savage and his legs were injured. Dr. Robinson then attended her brother for the injuries re- received, but had left off attendance about 6 weeks back. With regard to her brother's con- dition, he had been in vary low spirits, and was always very quiet. Dr. Robinson did not , caution her about her brother being disposed to commit suicide. Deceased went to bed, about 10 o'clock, on Thursday night, at wnich time there were no indica- tions of anything unusual in his habits, and witness saw nothing to excite her alarm. Deceased was in the habit of shaving himself.—The Coroner: Did you notice his beard was long the day before, and that be required shaving ?—Yes—Witness continuing said about half- past four, on the morning of the 25th, she heard her brother grdaning and went to his room, where she found him lying on the floor. Thera was no one with him. Witness went and called her other brother' who was asleep in the next room. She also alarmed the neigh- inure. When she first aiscovered deceased,he was lying on the floor with his throat cut. She did not find the razor, but heard that it had been found in the room.— rho Coroner: Did you not think there was aufficient curiousness about him requiring him to be looked after? —Witness : No.—He has never mentioned anything about taking his own life. I had no idea of anything lof this sort happening. He was thinking of going to work the following day.—Was your brother in work ?—Yes, up to a week previously to falling off the bridge at Rock Savage.—Didn'c I the D entor cautien you at all after what happened in May ?—Yes ; D ctor did say he wanted looking after the first month.—Why wasn't he looked after ?—He was looked after as best we could.—Did Dr. Robinson ever tell you dangerous things should be kept out faf his way ?—Yes, and we kept them out of his way until he got better.—When did Doctor Robinson say that 2—lt was in May.—Then when you allowed him to shave twice a week you thought he had got better ?—Yes.— When did dec2asod get to work after the accident in l!day ?—He never worked, sir ; but I think, on Monday, he was to have gone to work.—How often has Dr Robin- Ism] seen him since tho accident in My ?--He attended him about two months then.—Dr. Robinson did?—Yes. —Did Dr, Robinson tell you in May that he considered the accident in Rock Savage was an attempt to take jaway his life ? Yes, I think he did.—Di ihe say any- thing about his being put into confinement, or to that effect P—No ! no ! sir.—The Coroner here requested that Dr. Robinson should be summoned, but, later in the inquiry, it was intimated that Dr. Robinson had gone out of town, and could not be found.—Questioned as to the exact time the doctor was in attendance of her brother witness stated she could not exactly say. It would be about fourteen weeks since the accident and the doctor had been in attendance abut two months.— The Coroner: Then be gave over attending him about six weeks ago.—Witness : Yes.—Who paid the doctor? —He is reS paid yet.—The Coroner; 0 !—When the doctor ceased attending him did he say he was better ? Yes : He said he was getting better.—When did you give over watching him, and keeping things out of his way ?—I cannot exactly say, but it is not long since. About when the doctor ceased to attend.—The Coroner: Ah ! I see, at first you watched him and kept things out of his way ; then, thinking he was better, you did not consider it necessary to continue to watch?—Witness: Not when we thought he was better.—The Foreman : Did your brother appear strange in his manner before Ithe accident at Rock Savage 2 —Witnese : No.—Did he explain how the accident occurred P—No further I than to say he get on the bridge and over- balanced himself.—The Coroner : You know the matter was reported to the nolice.—Witness : Yes I knew that.—The Foreman : Has your brother been drinking heavily lately P—Witness : I have not noticed him.—Superintendent Detective Pearson : Did you know whether your brother had been drinking prior to the accident at Reck Swage ?—Witness : No I can- not say he had.—Hew long has he been shaving himeelf since the aceident ?—Atout 6 weeks.—He did not shave himself whilst under the care of Dr. Robinson P—No.— Die yen think there was any danger ?—No. I have never heard him threaten and had no reasen to think he would take his life.</p>
        <p>A Filor questilned whether the deceased WAS shavel or not —TM Coroner observed that he thouzht they were all aware a that. He had shaved.</p>
        <p>John Henry Hodeetts,brether,corrohoratcd his sister's statement about being called to deceased. He was in e.nmpanv with the deceased from 4 until 9 p.m. on the 25th. Witness retired to bed at 9.30, and left hie brother "up "with his sister. When called by his sister he went into his brother's heiroom and found hi Tr lying on the floor moaning. He had his shirt, drawers, trousers and braces. as well as his socks, on. He was lying in a pool of his own blood. He went for Dr. Robinson and came back with him. Dr. Robinson ordered him to the police-station and also gave him an order on Dr: MoDougall. When he came back to the house his brother was dead, thoulb he was alive when he left.—By the Cornner : Witnesa remembered the accident that happened at Rock Savage.—After the accident he kept the things from him ; and after he saw him vetting better he did not think it neeessary —The Foreman : Did your brother speak at all P—Wit- nese : When my sister called " Tom " three times, he said faintly" Hello."—The Coroner: How long did he live ? Witness: He was alive when I went for the doctor and officer, but dead when I returned.—By Superintendent D. Pearson : Did you know whether he was drinking heavily before the accident at Rock Savage P—No not then. He had been about six months ago. Mary Hndgetts. wife of the deceased, residing in Brunswick-street, stated she had two chiliren by the deceased, a boy and!a, girl. They had lived apart for 12 years. Ttere was nothing wrong between them ; it was simply her husband's wish. She last saw her hus- band on the Wednesday and Thursday. He did not seem strange, and said he was going to Widnes after a "job." She had no fear, and did not think he contem- plated self-destruction .—The Coroner : D. you remem- ber the accident in May.—Witnes : Yes Sir,—He was looked after, after that ?-0h yes, sir he had all the care possible.—Did you think it was necessary ?—Yes sir. It was necessary then ; ant he got all right and I did not think it necessary. Police Constable Charles Nicholls, stationed at Run- corn, said about 5.20 on the morning of the 25h, he received information from John Henry Hodgetts of the oceurrence, He found deceased in the bedroom lying on his left side with his head resting on his right arm. Hisl threat was cut from ear to ear, and deceased was lying in a pool of blood, dead. He found the razor produced which was lying about a yard from the body. It was a double bedded-room. One bed had the appearance of being used, the other was intact. Deceased slept by himself, and from appearances he (witness) would say he had been to bed.—ln answer to the Superintendent, witness said deceased had the appearance of being re- cently shaved. Superintendent Detective Pearson, stated be did not I know the deceased, The accident in May was reported Ito him on the following day as" an attempt tAt commit suicide." Consequently he went the day after, and investigated the occurrence. As the result of those in- vestigations no action was taken against the man. acting-sergeant Samuel Gregory, at his instructions visited the man for a period el about 9 weeks, and had him under observation. That was in consequence of the doctor submitting a statement to the effect thatfthe man should be looked after. It was then thought that homi- cidal tendencies bad left the man, end that the danger lof suicide was passed. He. the Superintendent, had some doubts in his own mind as to whether the fall from the bridge was accidental nr an attempt at suicide. That was the reason he instructed Acting-Sergeant Greenly to visit the man. But seeing he was going on satisfactorily, and having no desire to cause his people further pain, no action was taken against him upon his recovery.</p>
        <p>After a few minul'es deliberation the jury returned a verdict of "Suicide whilst of unsound mind," and thought that sufficient care had been taken of the deceased.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-09-01_WidnesExaminer_art0070" n="art0070">
        <head>RUNCORN RURAL DISTRICT COUNCIL.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN RURAL DISTRICT COUNCIL.</p>
        <p>The usual fornightly meeting of the above Council was held at the WorkhousN Dutton, on Tuesday, when there were present : Messrs. J Knowlesa P.,(hhairmln), S Woodward, T Wright, J B Chadwick, W Fletcher, E Clarke, G Pilling, J Mzey.Williams, P Leather, E A Wallington, T Lewis, with G F Ashton (clerk), W E Hough (assistant clerk), W Diggle, and J Swinton (sur- veyors), J Farrington, (inspector), and Dr. Adams (medical officer.)</p>
        <p>THE WATER SUPPLY OF HELSEY AND ALVANLEY. WHAT IS THE TRUE POSITION?</p>
        <p>Mr. W Woods, secretary to the Helsby Water Com- pany wrote stating that the directors were gratified to fled from the animal report of Dr. Adara 4, medical officer of health, that their efforts to provide an efficient water supply had been duly appreciated by him. With regard to Alvanley the directors wou'd point out that from the outset they had endeavoured to induce the residents to take their supplies trom the Helsby mains. As far back as July 1895 Mr. G. C. Taylor was in com- munication with the solicitors and agents to the Alvanley estate, and in May of last year the company's proposed scale of charges was, after a full inquiry, of which several of the principal ratepayers of Alvanley were present, approved by the Inca! Government &amp;lard. The Council would see, therefore, that the direcwsrs bad anticipated the Medical Officer's de?ire, and they confi- dently hoped that the District Council would now co- operate with them in their efforts to extend the scheme. If the ratepayers of Alvanley preferred to provide their own mains the directors were quite willing Co supply the water by meter at a reasonable rate per 1,000 gallons. ' The Clerk observed it was practically an attempt to compel klyanley to connect with the Helsby water scheme.</p>
        <p>The Chairman questioned whether there were not,some conditions entered into with Alvan ley and Helsby. The Clerk stated he could not tell exactly whether there were, nor the exact nature of them. Me. Woodward: Is there an absolute necessity for Alvanley to connect. The Clerk thought the Council hal powers to compel them to connect at certain rates. _ _</p>
        <p>Mr, S. Woodward sugpsted they alp-mid do nothing further at present thin get the Inspectar to replrt upon the exact state of the water supply as affictiug Helsby and Alvanley. Mr Farrington : I will do that with 'pleasure. This was cnsequently agree' to. THE WEITON SEWERAGE WORBS.-AN ENGINEER NOT</p>
        <p>REQUIRBD.</p>
        <p>Mr J. B. Chadwick, speakin' on the question of appointing an engineer to prepare a scheme tor the ex- tension of the Weetm Sewerage Works, stated it was his opinion they did tilt require an engineer. If they had one they would have to pay too much monfty ; and be- sides he did not know who they c laid get in the distric:, to undertake that kind of work. . .</p>
        <p>Mr. Wallington : HOW Wilt it be if you undertake it yourself Mr. Chadwick? (Laughter.) Mr. Chadwick : I cannit undertake anything myself.—Continuing, Mr. Chadwick said he rather favoured Mr. Diggle doing the work as a kind of over- time. In fact, in face of the stroag feelli,g amongst the membera of the Council tag their Surveyors should not undertake that work, how would it be if they con- sidered the raising of Mr. Diggle's salary to such a flzure as would remunerate him sufficiently and allow of the work being done. The Chairman remarked that such a consideration was not before the Council.</p>
        <p>Mr. S. Woodward said he was sure if Mr. Diggle would undertake to do the work for nothing the Council could do nothing but accept. (Laughter.) Mr. Diggle assured the Council nothing would give him greater pleasure than to do ,o. He would like to take that opportunity, however, of acquainting the members of the Council with the fact that the prepara- tion of extra plans etc., entailed a great deal of work, and often enough he had to work day and night, and even Sundays too, many and many a time. He would do this work for nothing will pleasur:.. Mr. Chadwick again pointed out that if an engineer was employed, Mr. Diggle would have to be his overseer and on the spot just the same, and they might as well get Mr. Diggle to do the work as have the extra man for Mr. Diggle to superintend. Mr. PUEley Williams : Just so. Mr. Chadwick added he was anxious that the work should go on, as the cottages would soon be ready for tenants.</p>
        <p>Mr. Dinh, stated he had a lett r from the owner of the property, stating he had seen by the local press that the matter was put off for a fortnight, and daploring the delay.</p>
        <p>Cnadwick thought they could go on with thl work at once.</p>
        <p>The Clerk: Yes you can go on with it. The only question will be the canction for a loan. If you don't get the sanction you will have to meet the expense Put of the current rate. But Ido not anticipate you will be refused sanction to borrow.</p>
        <p>After further discussion Mr. T. Wright proposed and Mr. Chadwick seconded that Mr. Diggle's offer bead- canted. Mr. S. Woodward whilst having due recollection of the resolution not to employ their surveyors outside their own sphere of labour, was quite at one with Mr. Chadwick in thinking it a mistake to employ outaicle engineers for Ruch work. He clnsiriered that the Dig- trict Council had talent enough in their employ to do all their work; it simply wanted re-arringement. (Hear, hear.) Such re-arrangement would enable them to do the work themselves.</p>
        <p>Mr. Moz'ey-Williaml eonsid.rei it WAR rather a qa.s- tion, not so mirth of the pavm.nt of Mr. Diggle as the taking him from his regular work.</p>
        <p>The Chairman: Yes. Mr. William,' : There Was a very strong feeling that he should not do anythinr; else. - ._ .</p>
        <p>Mr. E. Clarke: E4pctally upin a Stinchy. (Latrzh- ter:) _ _ _ _ _ . .</p>
        <p>Mr. Chadwick again reiterated his arguments against the employment of an engineer. The Clerk stated he had a letter from Mr. Thomas Rowlands, of Sandiway, Northwich, referring t) the matter. . . _</p>
        <p>Mr. Wallington propose-I that that letter be left on the table, a course uninimously agreed to. Mr. Diggle again expressed his willingnels to do the work gratis, and hopld there was not a single member of the Cauncil who objected. Mr. Wallington sail he did not think that a single member would object to accepting anything without ply- ing for it. (Laughter.) The resolutica wis then pot and unanimouily car- ried.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-09-01_WidnesExaminer_art0071" n="art0071">
        <head>SLNITARY CONGRESS</head>
        <p>THE SLNITARY CONGRESS</p>
        <p>Mr. E. White-Vili snr.tary of the Sinitary Insti- twe, wrote inviting the Council to appoint two (Negates to attend the Sanitary Clnzress to be held at Southampton from August 29th to September 2nd in- clusive. Be enclosed the necessary information and the us ial tickets. Mr. T. Wright proposed and Mr. Mozley Williams seconded that the Chairman, vice-chairman, awl the clerk be appointed delegates to th Congress, and that their expenses be paid by the Council. Several members having spoken of the benefits to be derived from attending the rlongre=s, the resolution was unanimously agreed ta.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-09-01_WidnesExaminer_art0072" n="art0072">
        <head>MEDICAL OFFICER'S REPORT.</head>
        <p>MEDICAL OFFICER'S REPORT.</p>
        <p>Dr. Adams reported one fresh cage of scarlet fever at 16 Walton-road ; one in 37, Walton-road; two cases in Down Farm, Norley. It was his opinion that the fever had been imported from Scotland. There were out- break; of diptheria at 10, Bank Cottages. Frolsham and in the last house in Townfield-lane, Frndsham. The usual precautions were ordered to be taken.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-09-01_WidnesExaminer_art0073" n="art0073">
        <head>CLOSING OF ITEL9BY SCHOOLS</head>
        <p>THE CLOSING OF ITEL9BY SCHOOLS</p>
        <p>Mr. J. B. Chadwick asked the Medical Officer whether he had attended to the schools at He!sby. It, was re- ported that mornine to the School Attendance Com- mitt,* that 100 children were absent from school through measles?</p>
        <p>ThP Medical Officer statei that the schools had been visited but had since been closed for holidays. He had that morning, however,received a letter from the Vicar, stating there were 90 children absent through measles and that the school had ben closed by him on bis own responsibility, pending a visit from himself (the merlinal officer), He would visit Helsby and ascertain whether those 90 children were really absent through the disease.</p>
        <p>It was finally decided that the doctor should visit Helsby and report.</p>
        <p>THE SMOKE NUISANCE FROM WESTON,</p>
        <p>Mr. E. Clarke strongly complained of the smoke nuisance from the chimneys of the Castner Kellner Works„ Weston ; at times the smoke was so thick, one could " cut it." There was a greater per cent. coming out of the chimneys than should do. Mr. J. B. Chadwick explained that it was so. He could assure them that it was no fault of the company; in fact it was a great loss to them to have so much un- consumed fuel go up the chimney to waste. It was the result of some patent stokers, but he could assure the Council that the company were only as desirous ax the public to cease the nuisance, and it would be attended to immediately. This was considered satisfactory. THE MANCHESTER SHIP CANAL.-" A GREAT NIIIRANCE. "</p>
        <p>Mr. Wallington. in complaining of the great nuisance caused by the slimy ueposit from t he Manchester Ship Canal, and the stench arising therefrom, asked whether there were any conditions drawn up with the District Council for the working of the "tidal openings." He contended there was a great nuisance at Walton, and was sure Mr. Pilling would agree with him. Mr. Pilling : Yes.</p>
        <p>Mr. Wallington further argued that the salt water, if it came up &amp;a far as Latchford. upon its return along the Canal, never got as far as Eastham before the tide was back again and carri.d the deposit with it back. Some discussion ensued, in which it was the unani- mous feeling that the Canal was a great nuisance, after which</p>
        <p>The Clerk stated be wind look the matter up. This course was agreed tl, and the report ordered to In submitted to the next meeting.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-09-01_WidnesExaminer_art0074" n="art0074">
        <head>RUNCORN BOARD OF GUARDIANS.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN BOARD OF GUARDIANS.</p>
        <p>The usual fortnightly meeting of the Runcorn Board ot Guardians, was held at the Workhouie, Dutton, on Tuesday. There were present :—Messrs. J Moziey. Williams (chairmln), A R Norman, T Reynolds, Samuel Taylor, the Rural District Councillors, with Mesam G F Ashton (clerk), %V E Hough (assistant clerk) and James Cunnah (master).</p>
        <p>THE FIRE AT SUTTON WEAVER. Mr. Cooke, relieving officer, reported that he was in- formed by the Superintendent of Police at Runcorn, Mr. Pearson, that a fire had occurred at Sutton Weaver at some cottages and that an old man who lived in one of the cottages had been compelled to go into an empty house in consequence. Mr. Cooke proceeded to Sutton Weaver for the purpose of taking the man to the Work- house. When he mentioned it to the old man he cried most bitterly. His granddaughter who was there also commenced to cry. Whilst he was trying to pacify the old man word came by a young man on a bicycle that he was not to be removed to the Workhouse on any account, as a relative at Runcorn wished to provide a home for him. Mr. Cooke said he therefore took the man to Runcorn and he was now at a loss to know how he should be refunded the expenses incurred in the re- moval. In similar C 3903 where the amount bad been small he had paid the expenses himself, but he did not think he was called upan to pay the expense in this case. The expenses were granted. THE NURSES UNIFORM.</p>
        <p>The Local Government Board wrote assenting to the proposal of the Guardians of the Runcorn Union to pay to M. D. Housley, nurse at the Worknouse, a sum of £2 per annum in lieu of uniform.</p>
        <p>T-11Ig C3ST OF PAI7PERISH.--•.RIINCORN BELOW THE AVERAGE</p>
        <p>Mr. Dansey's statement of pauperism and expenditure was submitted for the whole of his district. The cost per head on the population for Runc;rn Union was is 21 which compared favourably with the other unions of Cheshire, the average cost for the whole district being Is 4id.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-09-01_WidnesExaminer_art0075" n="art0075">
        <head>MATTER FOR CONGRATITLATION.</head>
        <p>A MATTER FOR CONGRATITLATION.</p>
        <p>Mr. Alexander Elornby, clerk to the committee of the County Asylum, Chester, wrote under date of 15th of August, stating that William Cox, admitted on the Ist of July 1897, had that day been discharged cured, and had left the institution in cornpsny with his wife.—lt was remarked this was a matter for cmgratulation.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-09-01_WidnesExaminer_art0076" n="art0076">
        <head>BETTER TERMS ELSEWHERE.</head>
        <p>BETTER TERMS ELSEWHERE.</p>
        <p>Mr. J. W. W. Mymper, writing on behalf of the secre- tary of the Royal Exchange Assurance. under date 23rd August, stated" Subject to the security pruving satis- factory to the company's solicitor, the directors were willing to advanc to the Guardians of the Runcorn Union the sum of £l5OO at 31 per cent, per annum, interest on security of the common funds of the Union ; such amounts to b repaid by instalment4 of prirolipat and interest combined, spread over a period not exceed- ing 20 years. The Chairman said they could borrow the money at a better rate from the Local Government Board and else- where and that further enquiries had better be made. This was agreed to. THE SHEDDING ACCOMAIODATION AGREED TO.</p>
        <p>The Clerk read a letter from the Local Government Board transmitting copies of an Order which they have issued authorising the Guardians of the Runcorn Union to cause a stone-breaking shed to be erected at the Workhouse of the Union at a cost not exceeding £l3O and also authorising the Guardians to borrow t amount.—The Local Government Board also consented to the repayment of the loan within a period not exceed- ing 20 years.—This was considered satisfactory.</p>
        <p>THE CHILDREN AT PANTASAPH ORPHANAGE.A SATISFAC•</p>
        <p>TORY REPORT.</p>
        <p>The Clerk reported that the Committee appointed to visit the chilrlren boarded out at the Pantasaph Orphan- age had visited the institution on Thursday last. The Commitiee consiste of Messrs. Fletcher, Chadwick and Edwards with himself. Mr. Chadwick stated that they had been very pleased with the outing. Pantasaph was abeut 4 miles from Holywell Stotion and very pleasantly situated. He should think they had accom- modation for about 200 children, and had about 180 there when they visited it. They saw the ten children belonging to the Union, and very well they looked. They were all from Runcorn, and one of them the eldest, named Keogh, had been there abont 7 years, and she would now be about 14 years of ate. She was trained as a nurse, and would soon b' ready to leave the institu- tion. All the children had the appearance of tying well looked after, and were really jolly. He thought it was quite the right thing to send the children to Pantasaph. They got a good education and were fitted out for life. They got a good industrial training, including instruc- tion in cookory, the laundry, and the nursery, as well as acquaintance with the sewing room. Whilst on the visit the deputation were shown a large number of articles worked by the children which had been sent to Cardiff to be exhibited at some show there. It was told them that the work was considered so good that the exhibits would be sent to the Paris Exhibition next year. In fact, it was ap- parent, that no child, no matter what its inclinations might be, was left unprovided for, and he hought the Guardians did a wise thing in sending the children there.</p>
        <p>Mr. Fletcher supplemented the remarks of Mr. Chad- wick. remarking that a better managed institution it had never been his privilege to go through. The chil- dren were bright, cheerful, and pleasant, even in the presence of three of the sisters, which went to prove they were not in any way afraid. They also all seemed very fond of work and well pleased with their surround- ings. The Chairman said from his own experience he could well endorse the report submitted. He had been there, and thought it was an institution worthy of their sup- port. (Hear, hear.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-09-01_WidnesExaminer_art0077" n="art0077">
        <head>DEPUTATION TO VISIT THE ASYLUM.</head>
        <p>DEPUTATION TO VISIT THE ASYLUM.</p>
        <p>On the motion of Mr. A. R. Norman, chairman of the House Committee. it was unanimously decided to send the following eentlemen as a, deputation to visit the Asylum at Chester: Messr; Pilling', Reynolds, A R Notmln. and the Clerk ( Vfr G F Ashton.)</p>
        <p>CONTEMPTIBLE EL&amp;CKPOOL. -THE WORICIIOI7SE CHILDREN</p>
        <p>REFUSED.</p>
        <p>In submitting his report. the Master (Mr James Cunnah), rEferred to the children's treat and explained that originally it was arranged that the children should have gone to Blackpool for their out, to sime " hydro " or other there, but when the people found lout that the children were from the workhouss,they wrote cancelling the arrangement. He had subsequently taken the children far an outing to Lymm, which was very well enjoyed, and it was intended to make up for the" de- finiency "by taking the children for a half-day:to Rhyl. (Hear, hear.) Mr. A. R. Norman : I hope Mr. Cunnah did not let the children know the contemptible reason:for which the arrangements were cancelled. Mr. Cunnah : Oh! no.</p>
        <p>Mr. S. Woodward remarked that last Board day he had waited upon the members of the Board of tivar- dians for the purpose ot collecting any donations to- wards the children's outing. He would remind those present that the list was not closed and he would be pleased to receive anything further. (Hear, hear.)</p>
