      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-09-01_WidnesExaminer_art0092" n="art0092">
        <head>BEFORE the MAGISTRATES.</head>
        <p>BEFORE the MAGISTRATES.</p>
        <p>ASSAULT CASE IN WELLFIELB-STREET WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>At the Warrington Borough Police Conn, on Friday, nefore the Mayor (presiding) and other magistrates, Patrick Tomlinson, sent., Patrick Tomlinson. junr., Mrs: Tomlinson, Joseph Langdale. and Men Bolton, were charged with assaulting John Newell on the 19:h August. —All the parties live in Wellfieid-street,'with the exception of Mrs. Bolton who resides in Selby-street.— Mr. N. H. J. Jenkics appeared for the defendants, and Mr. A. Browne represeired the complainant.—Mr. Browne, in hie opening statement said the parties—the Tomlinsons aud Newall—were on friendly terms up to a few months ago, when some difference arose which culminated on Saturday night. His client was standing at the bottom of Welifield-street at about 9.30 when Tomlinson, sear., came up to him and said' Why the h-- don't you stand on your own footpath which you pay rent for. His client did not speak, but moved off, when Mrs. Tomlin- son came up and rushed at him, striking him in the face. Tomlinson also struck him. Mrs. Tomlinsin called to her daughter (Mrs Bolton), her son, and a man named Longdale, who lodged with them, and they all " peppered " him right and left. He had to be attended by Dr. Bowden, as :there were bruises all over his body. Two young men interferedwhilst they were assaulting his client aud separatAthem or he (Mr Browne) did not know what would have been done to him. —Mr. John Newalls the complainant, who said he was a joiner at Whitecross Works bare out his solicitor's statement.—Other evidence was given for the corn- plainani's case.—Mr. Jenkins said it was a pitiable Equabble. It was a case of six of one and half a dozen of the other.—Evidence for the defence was then given by Constable Cooper, Lottie Barlow, 62, Fox-greet, and all of the defendants. The evidence submitted was very conflicting, the Bench eventually fined Tomlinson, sea., IN. and costs, and Tomlinson, jun., Scary] c)et-i, the charges against the others being dismissed. A WIFE'S ESCAPE BY MEANS OF A LADDER.</p>
        <p>At the Warrington Borough Police Court on Monday, John Taylor, 243, Knutsford-road, was charged with having assaulted his wife on the 21st August.—The complainant, who bore distinct marks on her face of rc- cent ill-usage, said that on the afternoon in question,be- tween two and three o'clock, she was standing at her Iroqt door when she saw her husband approaching, and, teeing that he was'* in drink." she went into the house. He subsequently Came in, and made US9 of bad lan- guage and also rruck her in the face without having received the slightest provocation. He rose for the pur- pose of endeavouring to bolt the door, but she made her escape and took refuge in a neighbour's house. He asked her toreturn,but she declined ti do so, as she was afraidl he would "hammer her." he subs,quently sent for her again, and at length she conceited to return, but when begot her in the house he struck her across her face with the poker and also with his fist. She screamed " Murder ' and "Police." She could not get out of the house as the doors were locked, but some neighb)urs came to her assistance, and at an early hour on the following morning she escaped from the house by means of a ladder through one of the bedroom windows into the street.—Mrs. Ashcroft, a neighbour, stated that she saw the cl,- fendant strike his wife with a poker. He also attempted to strike witness. Early one merning she heard the complainant calling out ‘*Fle is killing me," and witness told her not to jump through the window as she would kill herself if she did so. A ladder was procurrd,and the complainant escaped. She considered that the defenda, t was a "regular brute" to his wife.—Taylor said his wife was always in the house of the last wittiess,drinking away his money. He had found five pawn tickets upon her.— Mr. Birchall : But that does not jusiify yoe in abusing the woman.—Defendant: I did not strike her.—Arthur Taylor (9), the defendant's son, said he saw his father strike his mother. He had done so Previously. —Defend- ant : Oh. yes ! This is a concolted ail tir.—The Cnair- man said the Bench were of opinion that the defendant had been euilty of an aggravated assault, and he wauld be fined 203. It would be for the wife liter on to apply for a separation order.—Before the Court rose a separa- tion order was granted his wife. The Benci ordered him to pay her 171. 61. a week, and gave her the cus:ody of the children.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-09-01_WidnesExaminer_art0093" n="art0093">
        <head>MINOR CASES.</head>
        <p>MINOR CASES.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON BOROUGH POLICE couar.</p>
        <p>FRlDAY.—Before the Mayor (Alderman Wright) in the chair, Councillor Smethurst, Dr. Goma!' and Mr. J. Hepherd.</p>
        <p>AGGRAVATED ASSAULT ON A LITTLE GIRL.—John Smith, 15, Dolmans-lane, 70 years of age, was charged with havin4 committed an aggravated assault on May Litton° the previous aftereoon.—The Chief Constable said this was really a ease of indecent assault but the charge had beeis reduced to th t of aggravated assault to enable their worships to dell with it. —May Litton. 13 years, daughter of Mr George Litton,Green- lawn. Padgete-lane said she was going to selalol about a quarter past one when the defendant, who was cutting grass In a garden. called her to him and eonarnitted the assault complained of.—Mary Wien Kinsley. 1 Walter-street gave corroborative evidence and defendant was committed to prison for one month. A PALTRY Casg.—Sarals Jane Midge, 1. Leonard-street was cha.ged with assaulting Sarah Jane Robinson. 15 Sharp-street, and there was a .cross-summons for assault, on the same date.—Sarah Jane Robiason said on the day in question after some cenversation with Mrs. Illidge about money matters she shut her door and defendansttrnok her with an umbrella throw the closing door.—Mr. Browne, who ,epre- sented Mrs. Illidge, said he was instructed to say that the door was pushed open by the umbrella which accidently went inside.—Sarah Midge said Mrs. Robinson was closing the door in her face when the umbrella went in quite acct- dentally. Her foot was hurt by the door.—The Bench dis- missed the case, each party to pay their own costs. MONDAY.—Before Mr. F. Monks (in the chair). Councillors Smethurst and Shaw Green, and Messrs. Blrchall and S. Wright. DRUNICENNESS.—David McQueen, 33. Iloyle-street, was lined 5s for being drunk and disorderly in Church-Street at, 6-45 on Saturday evening ; John William Jackson.Garabaldi- street was fined 5s and costs for being drunk and aisorderly in Percy-street, at 7-20 on Saturday evening. OBSCENE LANGUAGE.—Mary Cambell, 45, Old-road, was lined.ss for making use of obscene language in Old-road on the 22nd inst.—Annie Hares, 1, Berry's-yard was fined 5s for making use of obscene language In Berry's-yard on the 19th , inst.—Sarah Wilson, 332, Knutsford-road, was ordered to pay' the costs (3s 6d) for making use of obscene 'language in I Knutsford-road an the 22nd inst.</p>
        <p>AN OBJECTIONABLE PHACTICE.—john Sampson. School Brow, was summoned for causing a nuisance in School Brow on the 18th August by throwing litter on the street.—lnspec- tor Norman said that on the day in question be visited dr- fendant's premises. Outside the shop he saw two men with three or four boxes of onions. They were throwing the husks on the ground. and the wind blew the litter all over the street. lle inspected defendant's house and yard and he lvd never seen a more beAstly plac3 in his life, there being rubbish all over the place. He bad cautioned defendant time after time about throwing material on the footpath, and he asked the magistrates to inflict a substantial penalty. De-</p>
        <p>fendant WAS setting the authorities at defiance.—Defendant said that he was away from home on the date mentioned,and his wife told him that the men wera unloading a cart. and one of the boxes being very frill it broke in two, and the contents went on the ground. The Inspector had only cautioned him once.— ta. fine of 108 was imposed. PROPERTY OWNER SUMMlNED.—Theadjourned else. in which Alfred Marsh was summoned for not abatine nuisances existing at the houses ~os, 3,5, 7, and 9, Boardman's Yard, owned by him, again came up for hex lug Insp-ctor Nor- man said that nothing had 13.3 en dme to improve the condi- tion of the property. He asked the Bench to make an order for the closing of the honies.—The order was granted. DRUNK IN CitannE.—David Plinston, 85, .1-byte-st., was summoned for being drunk in charge of a horse and cart, and not having a lizhted lamp attached to his vehicle at 10-30 p.m. on August 17th.—P.O. Johnson said that at 10-30 p.m. lon the date in question he was on duty in Lovely-lane, when Ihe saw defendant in charge of a horse and cart. There was no lamp attawied to the vehicle, and defendant was very drunk. His father was in the cart with him, and they both had hold of the reins. He accompanied them home and re- ported defendant.— Defendant: I was as sober as a judge. I never tasted any drink that day.—The Chairman : Which judge? (Laughter.) Defendant continuing, said he had driy.n from St. Helens and lost his lamp. —A fine of 5s and costs for being drunk in charge, and Sc for driving without a light was imposed.</p>
        <p>TIIUBSDAY.—Before Messrs John Fairc!ough (In the chair),</p>
        <p>and Hepherd.</p>
        <p>FACTORY ACT PROSECUTION.—Thomas Rushworth and Sons,Clegg-street, were summoned for commencing to occupy a factory wi hoot giving notice- to the factory inspector.— Mr Rustaworth appeared on behalf of the firm and pleaded guilty.—lnspector Smith, who prosecuted said that the de- fendants were the occupiers of a factory situated in Clegg-st. off Orford-lane, where they carried on business as bass fibre dressers. He visited the premises on July 26th and found upwards o 7 twenty persons employed there. fie ascertained that defendants had occupied the place for five months, but notice had not been sent to him, It was essen- tial that the authorities should be notified as early as possible, so that they could see if the requirements of the act were carried outt Notic i W4B received on the 29th July, three days after his visit. Defendants were the occu- piers of two large factories in Manchester, so they should have known the regulation. The penalty was not exceedinc, £5. Mr. Rushworth said that the firm were ignorant that they had to give notice, or they would have done so. They had occupied a factory for a great namber of years, and haul never given notice—a flue of 103. including costs, was im- posed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-09-01_WidnesExaminer_art0094" n="art0094">
        <head>MOST NUTRITIOUS.</head>
        <p>THE MOST NUTRITIOUS.</p>
        <p>EPPS'S</p>
        <p>GRATEFUL-COMFORTING.</p>
        <p>OCOA</p>
        <p>BREAKFAST AND SUPPER:</p>
