      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-09-01_WidnesExaminer_art0095" n="art0095">
        <head>EXAMINER, FRIDAY, SEPTEMBER 1 18J9</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, FRIDAY, SEPTEMBER 1 18J9</p>
        <p>DARESBURY PETTY SESSIONS.</p>
        <p>TUE5DAY.—Before Mr. Parr (in the chair) and Colonel Ridgway. _ _</p>
        <p>TRANSFER OF LICENSE.—The license of the Cat and Lion, Stretton. was transferred from Wllli4m Ostler to Joseph Broady, Superintendent Pearson rem.irking that the police had no objection. They had made the usual inquiries, and had found that the applicint was a man of exemplary char- acter.</p>
        <p>DRUNKENNESS.—Henry Shaw, drunk and disorderly at Stockton Heath, on the llth July. was fined 10s.—John Mil- ner was fined 103 for a similar offence at Stockton Heath, on the 30th July.—For being .Irunk and disorderly in Walton- road, Stockton Heath, on the 16th August, Charles Birdsall was fined Ss.</p>
        <p>DISTURBANCE AT STOCKTON HEATH.—Mary Payne. a mar- ried woman, was charged with being drunk and disorderly on the highway at Stockton Heath, on the 22nd July.—lx- Sergeant Gosling Gosling proved the case. He stated that the defen- dant came to the Police Station on the night in question close upon II o'clock. when he saw her kicking at the back door. She then went round to the trout door and continued the disturbance. Her conduct was disgraceful, and if he had not had to go to the Assizes on the following Monday, he would have locked her up. —Defendant : The police never do their duty there.—The Clerk : Don't say that —Defend- ant : My temper might have made me drunk.— The Police-Constable stationed at Stockton Heath said the defendant could hardly speak —D-fendant : Was I drunk? --The officer: You could hardly speak. —D3fendant : How could not I spe tkiwhen I spoke? (Laughter.) —The defeEdant asked for the case to be adjourned to the next Sessions, and the magistrates consented.—The Clerk (to deiennant) :J;And you had better not come in the state you are in now. DoG AT LARGE.—JOhn William Miller. of Walton was firled 3s 6dr including costs for allowing his dog to be at large un- muzzled at Walton.</p>
        <p>WILFUL DAMAGE.—James Churchill. a lad, was fined 51 for damaging the framework of a window at Stockton Heath' on the 17th August, the property of William Arnold.—Tha mother of the defendant said the fine would not b 3 paid as the defendant was innocent —The Clerk : Don't talk that nonsense in a court of justice. You will find that you will have to pay the money. A SLEEPY EilwlCEß.—John Deakin, a hawker, living in Winwick-road, Warrington. was fined 10s far being asleep in his cart at Stretton, on the 27th August. FRIENDLY SOCIETY MEMBERS AT VARIANCE.—MYSTERIOU3 AFFAIR AT LITTLE LElGlL—Alfrei Backer, was summeaed for having at Little Legh. on the 12th August, assaulted William Moores.—Mr. Jenkins appeared for the complainant, who. he said, was in the employ of the Cheshire County Couneil, the defendant being a farm labourer, working at Little Leigh. On the night of the 1204 August the complain- ant, defendant, and others bad attended a meeting at a public-house at Acton Bridge in connection with the Ancient Order of Druids Friendly Society, and during the course of the evening the complainant raised some objection, in a reasonale way, to some payments it was contemplated make- lug. Thet seemed to have given consider Ole umbrage to some of the members of tha society. The complainant accompanied a man named Shoenaek home, and rejoined in the main road Om defend Int and others who had been to the meeting.. After he had procee3ed a few yards, witheut the slightest werning, he was struck a most violent blow on the head, and it was a wonder he was not snore Ferlously injured. The stick which was used cut through the complaint's straw hat. There was a swelling on the complainant's head twice the s'ze of a man's fist,ane it required attention nearly all day on sunday. He had to admit that he did not know who struck the first Wow, but the complainant's boy saw the defendant strike the second bow on his father's shoulder, and the conclusion was.therefore.that the defendant had struck the first blew. He had experienced some difficulty in ;this case,beceuse althe men were'chernsi One of he men, Picker pramised to come to his (Mr. Jenains') office to %Live evidence, but he hai not kept his promise. He had seen him that morning, and had found him to be as very unsatisfactory witness. He should, therefore ask the permission of the Beech to be allowed to treat him as an unwilling witness The complainant then gave evidence,but he could not say who , struck him He had charged one or two persons with the offence.— The complainant's son was then called. He said he sew the defendant is' rike the second blow with a stick. He sub- sequently heard a canversatioa between the men to the effect that '• Ginger" Barker had struck the blow—the description " ginger" being applied to the defendant because of the colour of his hair.—John Pickering was then called, and is cross-examination by Mr Jenkins admitted that neither George Moores. Henry Kerbishley. nor Frank Limb struck the complainant. The only other man present was the de-' fendant He saw no blow struck, bat he heard a whack" --"his was the case for the complainant. —Several witnesses were celled for the defence, inaluding Goorge Moeres, Ker- blehiey, and Limb, bet none of them saw any blow struck —The Bench came to the conclusion that there was not evi- dence sufffdent to ehow who struck tke blow.—The case was therefore dismissed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-09-01_WidnesExaminer_art0096" n="art0096">
        <head>RUNCORN PET FY SERP,IONA.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN PET FY SERP,IONA.</p>
        <p>MONDAY. —Before Messrs. F. J. Norman, and A. R.</p>
        <p>Norman.</p>
        <p>ORDERED TO PLY HIS OAS BlLL.—Henry C. Barnes was 811A1132011 by the Runcorn Gas Co.. for 18s gas rent.-- Defendant did not apoear.—Mr. H. Chadwick proved the claim for the Gas Company. and the amount was ordered to be paid in one month with 6s costs.—Judgment went by default.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-09-01_WidnesExaminer_art0097" n="art0097">
        <head>Beforo Messrs. James Handley and A. R. Norman.</head>
        <p>Beforo Messrs. James Handley and A. R. Norman.</p>
        <p>DRONE AND WANTING TO FIGHT.---John Brookes, of Penketh lane. against whom 43 previous convictions were recorded, was summoned for being drunk and disorderly in Penketh-lane on the 19th of August. —P.C. Carrington who proved the case sated he saw the defendant in Penketh- lane very drunk, he was very disorderly and wanted to fight ana..her man.—Fined 5s and costs or 7 days. A CHANGEABLE PLEIDER.—JOseph Leathwecal, was sum- moned for using. ohszene language in the streets on the 19th of August. —He pleaded "that he knew nothing about it." —The Magistrates' Clerk : Well say not guilty.—P.C. Fry stated about 9-31, on the 19th of the present month, he was called Into Wilson's-road where he found the defendant flourishing a poker and going to strike a woman. At the same time he was coming out with the filthy expressions submitted.— Defendant : That is not so I—The Chairman: I thought you knew nothing about it ; you said so at first.—Defendant: So I did, and thac's the— truth —The Chairman : You have evidently been having some beer, now don't go and take any more. It being your first offence you will be let off with a fine of 51 or 7 days. OElel,. LANGUAGE IT WESTON.—Iohn Killgross, of Weston. was charged with using obscene language in Heath- road, Weston. on the 19th of August.—He pleaded guilty.— P.C. Wylie who proved the case stated he found the man in Heath-road about ten minutes past ten. He was in drink at the time and used vile language. There were a number of 'females about. Witness had received many complaints about the defendant.—The Chairman (to defendant) : When were you here last ?—Defendant: Two years ago.—The Chairmen: Yes : Now take care it is many and many a year before yoa come again. Fined ss. DISORDERLY CONDUCr AND AD7TTlNG.—Harriet Baxter was charged with indecent behaviour in Mersey-street the previous day, and Robert Southport, described as a young sailor, was brcught up on a charge of aiding and abetting.— Both pleaded guilty, and the eases were proved by Police Constable T. S. Bowyer.—The Bench committed Baxter for three months with hard labour, and fined Southport 105 and 8s costs, it being a first offence. ASSAULTING THE POLICE t VIOLENT PRISONER Michael O'Niel, Heath-road, Weston, was brought up in custody upon two charges: Drunk and disorderly, and assaulting Police-constable W. Writs, whilst in the execu- tion 6f his duty at Weston. on the 26th of August. —Prisoner pleaded guilty to both charges —Police-Constable Wylie slated at 9 p.m. on Saturday night he was on duty in Heith-road. WeAton, and had occasion to go and ask prisoner who was misbehaving himself to desist and go home. He repeated the request six or seven times. Prisoner was then quarrelling with his brother but at length turned to Witness and said he (witness) could not lock him up. Witness then found it necessary to close with the prisoner, and a scuffle took place in which prisoner kicked him and tore off button from his tunic and lost his whistle.—The prisoner who continued very violent was eventually locked-up. —Two youths Henry Flannsgan. and Joseph Rtchardson corroborated the officer's statement. —The Bench fined pri•oner 5s and costs, or 7 days, for each offence remarking they considered the assault on the police a very serious offence and 'warned prisoner against committing any such thing again. The prisoner was called upon to pay 21s or go for 14 days hard.—The money was paid. DRUlCE.—Charged with being drunk Mad disorderly in Green way-roadon'the 19th of August. William Sutton, stated that he was drunk but not disorderly.—Y.C. Wylie said he was on duty at 11.15 at the top of Greenway-road. Runeorn and saw the defendant coming up with another young man. He was disorderly and singing at the top of his voice. On being asked to go home quietly defendant, began cursing and swearing and witness would have locked him up only some other people interfered on his behalf and promised to take him home safely.—Fined 5s or 7 days.</p>
      </div>
