      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-09-01_WidnesExaminer_art0102" n="art0102">
        <head>BOWLING.</head>
        <p>BOWLING.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON CONSERVATIVE CLUB v. WARRING-</p>
        <p>TON LIBERAL CLUB.</p>
        <p>A pleasant series of gamas was played on the Con- servative Clab green on Saturday. Victory rett.ed with the home teem by th: narrow majorEy of four p4ints. The return games will bc played at tee Libt!ral Club, Score : -- CDNSERVITIVE CLUB. LIBEILLL CLUB. S. D. S. D. S Caldwell 9 1 J Albinson ll } 15 7 Taylor lOl 0 T Ceitchley T Horrabin D Rawling H Williams ll f 15 IN Hill 15 J Sankey 8 r . 11 I. W Miilingtm J Thornton (captain) 11 t .1, A Jones J Harrison 4l • ' G Strange (captain) 11 ) - T B Potter ll 1 lc H Daintith ...... ... 7 1 0 W J Wilcock ll f " W Monks J Simister —l7l 10 W Barton J i 1, Podmore ll Mitchell J Leigh 15 J Bennion 9 W J Wilson 11 Dutton 4 Rotherham } 15</p>
        <p>148 91 139 96 Conservatives (doubles and singles) Liberals :doubles and singles) 235</p>
        <p>Majority for Conservatives 4 A hearty vote ot thanks was accorded the Conserva- tives, on the proposition of Mr. Mitchell, supported by Mr. Albinson, for the very kindi way in which they had tveated the visitors and looked after their wants. Coun- cillor John Rstrarts acknowledge I the vote of thanks. WINNINGTON v. SANKEY BRIDGES.</p>
        <p>The return match between these ora—ni—za—t-i.o.ns took place on Saturday, on the green of the htter. The day's play resulted in a victory for the home team by 44 poioti, but the aggregate majlrity on home-and-home match's for VVinnington was 53 points. The following are the resuts of Saturday's gamei</p>
        <p>WINNINGTON.</p>
        <p>SANKEY BRIDGES.</p>
        <p>8. D. - s. D. W Hughes caps C Hinton F Hint a. E Mills T Smith 1 1 ~ T Ellison il il3 W Drysdale C Shaw J Unsworth S Foster 0 Booth T Whitby 3 T Brough Bl 13 T Henley R Bowden 'l} A Timmins T Devonport lO u J Graham W Uns worth 4} I Hough J Ilillington 9 5 J Willianssou ...... ... 11 13</p>
        <p>Total 143 53 Total 144 96 196 Z4O Majority for Sankey Badges 44 points on the clay's play. Aggregate majority for Winnington on home-and-home matches 53 points. EARLESTOVVN v. RAINHILL.</p>
        <p>The return fixture between the Institute Bowling Club and Rainhill, was played at Rainhill on Saturday afternoon. The game was closely cmtested through. our, but ultimately Rainhill ran out winners by 34 points. Rainhill defeated Raciest° urn on the home ground in the opening fixture by one p Ant, which gives them a clear lead of 35 points on the two games. The players afterwards sat down to a knite and fork tea which was heartily enjoyed. BOWLING MATCH AT NEWTON.</p>
        <p>On Saturday afternoon a match at bawls for £2O was played on the green of the Oak Tre_te Hotel. between C. Gorse, and Seth Hodson, before abut 503 spectators. The game was 81 up, and the betting was 6to 4 on Gorse. At the interval Hodson led by 7, but on the resumption Gorse gradually overhauled his opponent, and ultimately won a hard fou4lit game by 13 points ; the final score bling Garse, 81, Hodson 68.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-09-01_WidnesExaminer_art0103" n="art0103">
        <head>4,000 WORKMEN AFFECTED.</head>
        <p>4,000 WORKMEN AFFECTED.</p>
        <p>Th) biscuit workers of Reading nutnber four thousand and a statement by one of Messrs. Huntley and Palmer's men in the " Resding Standard" discloses particulars affecting all workers. The matter is put forth with the greatest candour and detail, the full name and address of the speaker being published—Mr. George I3ollick, 7, Weldale-street, Reading. He is an intelligent, good- looking man, as his portrait shows, who has been in the army, and weight attaches to his opinion. He said : "In 1897, for the first time in my life, I began to feel I was not myself. I had no relish for my meals ; i trembled and shook, and the slightest movement caused me to perspire dread- fully. My tongue was coated over; and later, I was attacked by influenza and asthma. " For five months," he continued," I was on sick pay. But when almost all hope was dead I read of a similar case cured by Dr. Williams' pink pills for pale people. "Although when I first suffered from this acute indigestion and asthma, I tried other pills as a substi- tute, they had done me no good. I now took same of Dr. Williams' pink pills. After the first we,k I com- menced to relish my food—quite a new experience. I continued the pills and soon returned to my work. If I were out of sorts I should at once purchase another box."</p>
        <p>Mrs. Hollick added: " Some people think Dr. Williams' pink pills can only be had from London, but they can be bad of chemists, provided the purchaser Insists upon having them with the full name printed on the wrapper—Dr. Williams' pink pills for pale people." The hot air of a'factory will often set up indigestion and asthma to' ; and, particularly at this season, the benefit which workers may derive from Dr. Williams' pink pills can hardly be too strongly insisted upon. By giving direct strength and nourishment to the blood and nerves for they are not debilitating as all purgative medicines are), they have cured many other disorders, including consumption, bronLhi,is, paralysis, rheuma- tism, St. Vitus' dance, nervous prostration, eczema and skin eruptions, and the characteristic ailments of ladies. Sold by all chemists, two and ninepence a box.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-09-01_WidnesExaminer_art0104" n="art0104">
        <head>WESTON ANNUAL SPO US.</head>
        <p>WESTON ANNUAL SPO US.</p>
        <p>The third annual sports were held in a field, kindly lent by Mrs. Rankes, on Saturday afternoon. The weather was all that could be desired for the occasion, and there was a good attendance of visitors. Too sports were under the management of the following officials :—Judges, Rev W H Stables, Messrs J Aspey, J Carson, and J Fraser ; starter, Mr James Tudor; clerks of the course. Mr J E Bankes, J G Lightfoot, A Tudor, R Nesbitt, F Robinson. J Garner, J Earle, and W Pendlebury ; field marshals, Mesi.rs. Joseph Tudor, and W Hope; corn- rtitora tent, Messrs. J E Hough,C Williams, G Palmer, C Hough, G Joncs, A Davies; telegraph board, Messrs. A Hopwood, and P Robinson ; committeA' Messrs W Hope, J E Tudor, F Dovaston, 'l' Jones, T Pickering and P Williams.; sports secretary. Mr. Walter Tudor. _ _</p>
        <p>The WestOn.Church brass band was in attendarce and played selections of music during the proceedings. The awards were difitributed by Miss Bankes. Results :- 120 lards Flat Race Handicap.-Winners of heats: F W Matthews, Widnes, 74 yards; E Plioston, Warrington. 9 yards; H Green, Runcorn, 10i yards : J Would, Helsby, 64 yards; J Sherlock, Runcorn, 12 yards ; F Cartwright, Fielsby. 6 yards ; J Hump'ireys, Hel-bv, 8 yards. First trial heat: 1, F W Mathews ; 2, H Green; 3 E Plinston. Second trial heat: 1. J Sherlock; 2. F Bruhl, Warrington, 6 yards; 3, J Humphrey. [feisty. Deciding heat: 1, F Bruhl ;2, J Sherlock; 3, H Green. 150 Yards Flat Race for BlYs.-First heat : 1, P Morgan, Weston, 16 yards; 2, F Thomas, Rocksavage, nine yards; 3, J Furnival, Weston. scratch. S.cond heat: 1, J Booth, Rocksavage, 13 yards; 2, H Corney, Warrington,tbre.e yards; 3, E Harris-m,Runcorn,ccratch. Third brat: 1, A B Praeger,Warrington, five yards ;2, L Timmins, Runcorn, nine yards ; 3, IT Whitlev,Overton six yards. Deciding heat : 1, A B Praeger ;2, J Booth; 3, P Morgan: 220 Yards Flat Race Flandican.-First heat ; 1, T Summerfield. Weston, twelve yards ; 2, J Sherlock, Runcorn ; 16 yards ; 3, J Weston, Runcorn, ten yards. Second heat: 1, R Hough. Runcorn, 14 yards ; 2. D Price, Warrington, nine yards ;3. 11 Pdounfield, War- rington. twelve yards. Third heat : 1., II Green, Run- ern, 15 yards ; 2, F Brohl, Warrington, eight yards ; 3, E Harrison, Runcorn, 25 yards. Deciding heat : 1, J Sherlock ; 2, 'l' Summerfield ; 3 E Harrisoi. 600 Yards Obstacle Thce.-First heat 1, C Heffern, Frpdsham, 25 yards; 2, T Surnmerfield, Weston, 20 yards. Second best : 1, J Jones, Helsby, scratch ;2, J Worrell, Helsbv, 30 yards. Third heat : 1, J Go. dell, Frodsham, 18 yards; 2. J Humphreys, Helsby, 25 yards. Final heat : 1. J Goodall ; 2, C Heffern; 3, J Jonas. 440 Yards Flat Race Flindican.-First heat : 1, E Plinston, Warrington, 25 yards ; 2, J Eleesoaa, Warring- ton, scratch ;3. F W Matthews, Widnes, 20 yards. Second heat : 1, J Malvneux, Weston, 35 yards ; 2, T Summerfield, Weston, 20 yards; 3. F C Dean, Tarvin, scratch, Final: 1, E Plinston ; 2, J Molyneux ;3, F W Matthews.</p>
        <p>880 Yards Flat Race Handicap : 1, H BPrcon, North- %via], scrAtch ; 2, 1 intingon, Warrington, 30 yards ; 3, E Williams, Flint, 35 yards.</p>
