      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-09-01_WidnesExaminer_art0107" n="art0107">
        <head>ARTHUR ADAMS. (From a photograph.)</head>
        <p>ARTHUR ADAMS. (From a photograph.)</p>
        <p>If your baby is fat and well ronrished it will stand a good chance of going through the oorind.of teething without much distress. On the other hand the weak, thin, delicate babies have no end of trouble. It is like a erotic, vigorous adult, who resists a disease that would result in the death of a weak person. Do not think that you are especially different from your baby. The only difference is that the organism of a child is more susceptible to outside influences than that of an adult, and the child must also have material for growth. Here is an object lesson for parents who have teething babies. " 26, Heyford Avenue, •' South Lambeth, "London, S.W. " March 7th, 1899. _</p>
        <p>" Gentlemen,- I beg to testify to the excellence of your Scott's Emulsion for blbies during teething. I have also found it a grand remedy for coughs and colds. I enclose my son's photo. He is fight months old, has eight teeth, and has cut them with- out fits, etc., or even a single bad night.—Wishing Scott's Emulsion the success it deserves, I am, gentlemen, yours sincerely, (Signed) A. L. ADAMS."</p>
        <p>If baby is teething, or if baby does not thrive, we suggest that you try a little Scott's Emulsla+ and see how quickly this preparation will restore health, flesh, and strength. Scott's Emulsion or tains tue hypophosphites of lime and soda, whiob, are useful in the formation of healthy bones. oø are also beneficial to the nerves and to digestion. P' addition to this, Scott's Emulsion comprises the purest Norway cod-liver oil and glycerine. The formula of Scott's Emulsion is freely given to tbo medical profession, and highly endorsed bt all medical men, Scott's Emulsion is Ff: a secret remedy, and it has more uses in human system than any other preparation of !;.; kind. You will find Scott's Emulsion perfectl7 palatable and of a positive benefit to the digestife, system, especially of an infant or growing chilli' Children thrive on Scott's Emulsion when no other remedy seems to do them any good. You can obtain a sample of Scott's Emulsion bt sending threepence to cover postage to Scott air Bowne, Limited, Manufacturing Chemists 9°, Great Saffron Hill. London, E C., and mendoniog this paper. All chemists sell Scott's Emulsion.</p>
        <p>Art Gallery :-69, Lime-street.</p>
        <p>HEWITT, MARKET-PLACE, WARRINGTON, FOR SPECTACLES AND FANCY GOODS</p>
        <p>OF ALL DESCRIPTIONS. OPEN EVERY DAY.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-09-01_WidnesExaminer_art0108" n="art0108">
        <head>NOTES ON GAMES AND ATHLETICS.</head>
        <p>NOTES ON GAMES AND ATHLETICS.</p>
        <p>CRICKET.</p>
        <p>Warrington are maintaining their good reputation, as on Saturday they easily disposed of Cheadle Hulme on their own ground. It will be remembered that with the first engagement this season. time robbed the Warring- tonians ef a victory, the total on that occasion being Cheadle [Mime 149, Warrington 137. for three wickets. The match on Saturday was set apart for the benefit of Teather, the home professional.and although Ih3 number of spectators was not excessive, a satisfactory amount was realized. Bibby, the Warrington ciptain, won the toss, and decided to bat. J. Callon and Thewlis were opposed by Teather and G. E. Radford. The amateur was in a scoring mood during Teather's first three overs and got him thrice over the boundary. With the score at 33 Callon was out to a clever catch at squaw lee, having made 20 in clashine, style. Ihe vacancy was filled by E. A. Connor, who soon lost the company cf Thewlis, who played one hard on to his off stump.</p>
        <p>G.Clarke came next and was repeatPdly in difficulties, but Connor was in splendid form and quickly sent up the fifty with a fine drive off Radford. Thrice in one over did Connor place TPather for four, causing that player's retirement, Redfern relieving Radford at 68, that bowler taking up the attack at the pavilion end. The new bowler at once met with success b mling Clarke for a single, whilst in the same over Connor had his middle peg disturbed. The retiring batsman had made 30 by free cricket, and included-in his innings were six fours. J. W. Bibby played steadily fr.r nine, whilst J. Thompson anlll R. Pierpoint added 31 for the seventh wicket before Pierpoint was bowled. Tea%her again resumed,and Thompson was applauded for drivinghome the professional thrice in suc7ession to the boundary and causing the century to be hoisted. et 121 R4dford bowled Thompson for an invaluable innings of 27, and the venture soon closed for 128.</p>
        <p>Cheadle Hulme were left with nearly two hours in which to get the runs, and commenced their ess iv with C H Taylor and E Radford, the bowlers being E A Con- nor and Thewlis. Disasters were early in store for the locals, for with only six runs on the board Taylor fell a victim to a grand catch in the slips by the visiting cap- tain. The second ball by Cadman proved fatal. Ramp- son effecting a brilliant catch off the fast bowler. H C Royle came flex', and saw Radford place Connor for four, but in the next over the last corner was palp- ably leg before to the Warrington prlfessional. G. E. Haworth filled the vacancy, arid experienced a slice of luck, being twice missed in as many minutes. A. 16 J. W. Bibby bowled an over to allow of the bowlers to cross over, and saw Radford missed in the"country." Despite these mistakas, the fielding of the vi,iters was very keen, and a sharp return by Clarke at cover almost cost Haworth his wicket. The score stgadily mountel, and at 37 Bibby relieved Thewlise The change proved to be a judicious one, for with his second ball the mew bowler completely beat Haworth with a fine ball, whilst three runs later he defeated Radford "all the way," the retiring batsman having made the highest score for his side. The remaining batsmen faile I completely against the deliveries of both bowlers, and the innings closed for 58.</p>
        <p>The catch by }lampoon which dismissed Rome was particularly worthy of note, the ball being taken very low down off one of Connor's expresses. Bibby bowled in capital style taking five wickets for twelve runs,which must stand out as one of his bNit accomplishments. Be bowled a splendid length and made the ball break al- most the width of the wicket. Connor, too, bowled in gond form and the fielding of the side was particularly praiseworthy.</p>
        <p>The second team achieve.' an easy victory over Cheadle Holm°. Battina, first, th,. visitors were dismissed 'for the small total of 48. Plinston did good work with the ball taking six wicklts for ten runs. Going to the wicket the home team made 111 runs for the loss of five wickets. Si!rock and Pickton batted in good form, scoring 30 and 28 respectively.</p>
        <p>Sankey Wire Mills sustained their first defeat this season on Saturday, their conwrors being Earlostown 2nd. Earlestown batted first, and piled on runs at a very rapid rate, the closure being applied when 180 had been reached for the loss of two wickets, Davies (63) and Butler (62), having done good service for the team. The Wire Mills responded with only 62, the best efforts being made by W Evans (15) and Clarke (13).</p>
        <p>Frodsham gained an easy victory over Vale Royal yesterday week, the totals being 239 to 109. C. E. Linaker batted in grand styl. for the victors, knocking up 120. and Selby played •vp;l f.r his 31 not out. F. H. Cooke (32) and Twiss (12) were the principal contribu- Ors for Vale Royal.</p>
        <p>On Saturiar the Frocialiana team met Weaverham, which club they itelreed by a majority of 87. For the winners R. C. Selby batted in capital form. arid scored 60, the only player for WEaverbam who reached double figures being Johnson who knocked up 18. Sankey Wesleyans suffered defeat at the hands of Grappenhall making c,nly 13 totheir opponents 48. Good batting on eithAr side was consnictrus by its abenc.. Ellis (18) of Grappenball being the only ple.„ rr to reach double figures.</p>
      </div>
