      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-09-01_WidnesExaminer_art0134" n="art0134">
        <head>?Letters to the etittor.</head>
        <p>?Letters to the etittor.</p>
        <p>NOTICE TO CORRESPONDENTS. Authors of ietters intended for publication must in at casei send their names and addresses to the Editor. atters intendEd for publication must b 3 written on one side only of the paper. Correspondents are stronzly advised to write briefly. A short letter has much greater chance of insertion than a long one.</p>
        <p>PROPOSED COTTAGE HOSPITAL Sir,—On behalf of our Committee we beg t acknow- ledge the enthusiastic work of all those who contributed to the success of the swimming gala in aid of the above and respectfully ask them to accept our best thanks for their untiring energies in the cause —Faithfully yours, W. E DUDLEY) W. BOOTH, Hon. sees. j"</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-09-01_WidnesExaminer_art0135" n="art0135">
        <head>TITHE RENT CHARGES (RATES) BILL.</head>
        <p>THE TITHE RENT CHARGES (RATES) BILL.</p>
        <p>Sir,—This measure affords a very striking illustration of the tender care with which Conservative Governments look after the pecuniary interests of their ecclesiasti :tat friends. The cry had gone up for some time tbat,owing to depreciation in the value of agricultural produce, some of the clergy were suffering from reduced incomes. Iles cry has had the effect of bringing speedy relief from their friends in office. And this relief came in the form of a Bill which confers a gratuity of £87,000 a year for three years, on the richest church in the world ! A li'tle frfe cr ticism on this remarkable specimen of class legislation will do no harm. In the first place, the measure is undoubtedly Anti- Christian. because it still further endows a well paid m inistry—such a ministry as never entered into the minds of Christ and His Anostles. The most careful scrutiny of the New Testament fails to discover either precept or example for associating hireliogism with the Gospel of the grace of Gd. Vast and momettous as were the s7rviees of Christ it. the days of his flesh, they were all willingly and graciously rendered " without money and without pr7ce." For the Saviour's reward lay not in the tithe or salary but in the consciousness that all his words and actions redounded to the praise and glory of Him who had sent htm on his great mission to the world. One can imagine the holy indig- nation with which he, who had whipped the money traffickers out of His Father's templiewould have spurned an off,.r of salary or the contents of a collection box ! And the same unselfishness and dependence upon God whizh distinguished Him above all men were reflec- ted in His Apostles. Strong in the power of Him who had called them ioto His service, these humble men went forth and preached the gospel, and at the same time laboured day and night with their own hands that they might not burden or impoverish their converts in any way. it was reserved fnr teachers in after years, when Christianity te4an to be corrupted by admixture with the world, to discover that a title and a comfor.- able position were essential requisites of the Christian ministry. Another objection to the Bill is its injustice. If all the people of this country were Churchmen, and were willing that their rulers should tax them for the support of the clergy, no one could complain on the plea of human unfairness, although one might demur to such a levy on the ground that it was contrary to the free gospel principle of the New Testament. But when we remember that perhaps not much more than one half of the inhabitants of the country are in communion with the State Church, some really, and more only nominally, how flagrantly unjust it se.ms to be to compel Roman Catholics and Nonconformists of all shades to pay for religious services to which they conscientiously object. Nor does the injustice stop here, but actually affects many of the clergy themselves. In all proba- bility the whole of the £87,000 will go into the pockets of the beneficed clergy, while the poorly paid curates, wire really do all the practical and useful work of the Church, will derive no benefit whatever from the dole. And lastly, the Bill, although emanating from "the gentlemen of England," has a strong flavour of mean- ness about it. The clergy are not the only persons in the country who suffer at times from diminished means. Manufacturers, commercial and professional men, and working people are often in sore straits through a fal- ling off of income. But who ever heard of any of these classes going to Parliament, hat in hand, for money to tide them over their difficulties. The clergy, however. seem to be capable of dding this kind or thing, and while we condemn their action, we must not forget the fact that Othere is something in their eccupation which seems to predispose to it. Is it not a deplorable fact that the cry of the churches and chapels for money has become so acute and persistent as to render it hard for the best of ministers to keep clear of the pecuniary mania. And then, again. was it not rather mean of the lordly bishops of the Church, and the aristocracy who pasture in its folds, to allow an appeal to be made to Parliament for help for their distressed brethren in the cosey deaneries and rectories of the country? How much more Christian-like it would have been if they had put their bands into their own pcckets and sub- scribed the dole rather than allow the church to come upon the rates.—Ycurs truly, lONA. 26th August, 1899.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-09-01_WidnesExaminer_art0136" n="art0136">
        <head>ItIINCCIRN NOTES.</head>
        <p>ItIINCCIRN NOTES.</p>
        <p>[By A CORRESPONDENT.] For the sixth time the Runcorn Bench of Magis- trates have expressed with no uncertain voice their determination not to grant another license for the sale of intoxicating liquors within the district. On all hands their de-Usions have given extreme satis- faction. There are admittedly too many public houses in RuLicorn ; and the facilities for obtaining drink are far too numerous. The Bench have there- fore, done a public servico in refusing the two applications which came before them on Monday.</p>
        <p>In point of fact, the cases were extremely weak ones, and Mr. H. Davies, (the opposing solicitor), disposed of them without very much difficulty. One side of the question, however, should not be lost sight of. viz., the argument so largely drawn upon from time to time citing in favour of the grocer's shop license :—"That people would rather send their children to such a place than to a public house." It is an argument that is extremely danger- ous and fallacious; for what do we find in actual experience? But whether the child gets its father's jug of ale from the "public" or the "shop" it will in the same percentage of cases take a "sip out o' th' jug" on the way ho,Le jut the same. Further, the grocer's license is a source of temptatien to wives and women generally.</p>
        <p>With regard to the remarks of the Bench respec- ting the serving of children apparently under the age of 13 years "that they hoped the publicans would co-operate with the authorities in this mat- ter," the fact remains that numbers of children un- der the age of 13 are served with liquor, as can be easily seen by anyone casually strolling through the streets of Runcorn at night time. Numbers of little children apparently nearer the ages of 8 and 10 than that of 13 years, can be met with carrying home a jug of ale. Obviously, the question arises : " Where did they get it ?" And naturally one con- cludes they have been served at the nearest public- house. Certainly, the significance of the fact that the child is there, the ale there, and the public- house is not far off, cannot be overlooked.</p>
        <p>Whilst discussing the question of the appoint- ment of an engineer to superintend the extension of sewage works at Weston, last Tuesday, the Rural District Cotracil touched upon a question of great importance to the ratepayers : that of re-arrange- ment of the Council's servants duties. The Council's surveyors have a prescribed duty to perform—and the feeling of many of the Councillors is that if the Surveyors fulfil that prescribed Laity they are suffi- ciently employed—but they are occasionally called upon to do that which is outside their regular sphere of labour and being competent men, are employed as draughtsmen and engineers, for which naturally enough they expect and get a remunera- tion. .This system, however, does not appear to the majority of the Councillors to be the most ecomomi- cal, and possibly before long the subject of a better employment of the" Council's talent," to use Mr. Woodward's expression, will be considered, so that " little jobs "like that at Weston can be adequately dealt with.</p>
        <p>The action of certain people in Blackpool refus- ing to accommodate the little children of Runcorn Uni-ai for a half day holiday there, bimply because they were " Workhouse children " is arousing deep 6ndignation in Runcorn. It is a most contemptible reason to asign and one that reminds us there are still people living in England who would stigmatise rather than elevate "the poor we have always with 113.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-09-01_WidnesExaminer_art0137" n="art0137">
        <head>WIRRAL AND BIRKENHEAD AGRICUL– TITRAL SOCIETY.</head>
        <p>WIRRAL AND BIRKENHEAD AGRICUL- - TITRAL SOCIETY.</p>
        <p>The following is a• statement of the numbnr of entries in the various classe3 of the show on Wednesday and Tlfursday next -161 sheep, 36.; cattle, 108; hnises, 483 ; pig7ons, 1,680 ; poultry, 1,223 ; dogs, 1,547 ,• grain and roots. 53; cheese, 58 ; butter and eggs, 144 ; horticulture 9 599 ; honey. 115 ; stands and machinery. 90; total, 6,152. The total last rear was 4,471, and the ptevious record of entry was 5,505, so that this year's eclipses any of its predecrssors, and many entries for implecnerns have had to be returned owing to the want of sheddirig,and the Committee much regret the fact.</p>
        <p>CYCLES. Premiers. £lO 103 haat:Wort), £l2 Os Od New Rapids. £l5 lOs Od. Sole agent J. S. GARLAND.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-09-01_WidnesExaminer_art0138" n="art0138">
        <head>EXAMINEtt, FRIDAY, SEPTEMBER 1, 1599</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINEtt, FRIDAY, SEPTEMBER 1, 1599</p>
        <p>EARLESTOWN &amp; NEWTON</p>
        <p>CHURCH OF ENGLAND TRIIPERANCE SOCIETY. Sermons were preached on Sunday last at Nt vv- church in the morning and evening by the Rev. J. Hirst, secretary of the Diocesan C.E.T.S. Offer- tories were also male in aid of the society.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-09-01_WidnesExaminer_art0139" n="art0139">
        <head>POLICE CASES.</head>
        <p>POLICE CASES.</p>
        <p>At the Town Hall, Newton, on Monday morning before Mr. T. J. Gillespie, Michael Caffrey for being drunk was let off with a fine of 5s including casts on promising to sign the pledge.—Thomas Downes for a similar offence was fined 28 61 and costs. THE STRIKE AT THE VULCAN FOUNDRY.</p>
        <p>The strike which was initiated by the strikers engaged at the Valcan Foundry, last week, for an increase in wages of 2s per week, has collapsed as suddenly as it sprang into life. All the men have returned to work on the old terms, but three of them who were connected with the United Smiths and Strikers' Association have been dismissed. PIC-NIC FROM HAYDOCK.</p>
        <p>On Saturday morning the members of the Mothers' Meeting Class, held by Mrs. Evans, of the Hayes, Haydock, held their annual pic-nic. A through carriage was attached to the 845 train from Earlestown for their ahcommodation, and the day being beautifully fime, they passed a most enj3yable time at Southport, which was their destination. They returned from douthport about 7 o'clock, thoroughly satisfied with their outing.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-09-01_WidnesExaminer_art0140" n="art0140">
        <head>MCCORQUODALE AND CO's. EXCURSIONS.</head>
        <p>MCCORQUODALE AND CO's. EXCURSIONS.</p>
        <p>The annual excursion promoted by Messrs. McCorquodale and Co.,Newtoo,took place on Satur- day. As in previous years tha excursion was divided,one portion going to Blackpool and another to Liverpool. As it was not restricted to the em- ploye3 a large number of people took advantage of the cheap fares, and two well filled trains left for each place, the first leaving Earlestown soon after six o'clock for Blackpool, the first train for Liver- pool leaving about half-past seven. The day was beautifully fin,i and the tripp,srs had an enjoyable time. Most of those who accompanied the trip to Liverpool, journeyed across the river to New Brighten, whilst those who favoured Blackpool, lounged about on the beach, and visitei the various show place, but owing to the extremely crowded condition of the town locomotion was difficult. The excursion was of a most successful character, the last train retching home soon after one o'clock on Sunday morning.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-09-01_WidnesExaminer_art0141" n="art0141">
        <head>GOOD TEMPLARY.</head>
        <p>GOOD TEMPLARY.</p>
        <p>The weekly meeting of the 1.0 G.T. Hope of Earlestown Lodge No. 1644 was held in the Co- operative Hall, Wargrave-road, on Monday, Bro Francis C.T. presiding. After the opening services, Sister Grant was elected District Lodge Representa- tive in place of Bro. Williams who declined. The secretary presented several bills which were ordered t 3 be paid. Apologies read from absent officers, letters read from C.C.T. urging all to try and gain new members and to help the convention executive to try and form new lodges in fresh localities. Visiting and Business Committees arranged to meet to make arrangements for anniversary and open air meetings, Bro. Clarke appointed to invite Bro. Rev. J A Lee, DOT,, to preach the anniversary sermons. A visitor from the Hone of Barrow Lodge tendered the gree'ing of that and the Furness Lodge which were accepted and the Sister was asked to convey the greetings of this lodge to them. Arrange- ments were then made for visiting the Hope of Sutton Lodge after which the members joined in singing ode No. 203" When Christians will vote as they pray " ; reading, " Takkimg th' Census," Bro. W arburt en; song, "Ere the sun goes down" ;Sis. Owen reading, Bro. Clarke; reading, Bro. Newman; after which the lodge was closed by Bro. Francis, C.T. - . ?</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-09-01_WidnesExaminer_art0142" n="art0142">
        <head>ST. HELENS.</head>
        <p>ST. HELENS.</p>
        <p>ACCIDENT TO A ST. HELENS BOY.</p>
        <p>Thomas Daivies, aged tea, oi 6, Leach-street, St. Helena, went for a walk on Sunday afternoon with a companion named Fletcber. While picking blackberries in a field near the Bottle and Glass public-house they began playing with a small waggon usel for carting clay and sand, when Davies was knocked down and his thigh broken. Be was removed to the Providence Hos- pital, attetded by Dr. Jackson.</p>
        <p>SIT YEARS OF MARRIED MISERY.</p>
        <p>At Sr. Helens,on Saturdav,John Burns, of 79.Victoria- street, was charged with assaulting his wife Ann Jane. Mrs. Burns said that on Sunday morning last her husband drove her out of the house, and then ran at her in the striset and kicked her and struck her. He did not appear at Court on Friday, and had to be arrested on warrant. She had a neighbour willing to come as a witness, but her husband had terrorised her so much that she dare not come. On Friday night he ran her about the house with a razor. She bad never bad any pleasure since she married him six years ago. He had broken all the furniture many a time, and set the house on fire.—Con- stable Sellers said he livei next door to the prisoner' Mrs Burns was a hard-working woman.and had to work for herself and children. The husband was constantly threatening her, and brcußht all sorts of men to the house.—Burns was fined 13s 61 or fourteen days.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-09-01_WidnesExaminer_art0143" n="art0143">
        <head>FIRE AT A ST. HELENS CHEMICAL WORKS.</head>
        <p>FIRE AT A ST. HELENS CHEMICAL WORKS.</p>
        <p>Some excitement was occasioned at St. Helens on Wednesday afternoon by an alarm of fire at the Hardt shaw Brook Chemical Wo:ks,owned by the United Atka, Company. The news immediately brought to mind the disastrous expiosion which occurred in May last, and,as it leaked out that the file on Wednesday was in the chlo- rate 'nous( —the seat of the calamity just referred to— fears were entertained in some quarters that another disaster was impending. The Corporation Fire Brigade, under Sergeant Sisson, with the steamer and hose carrla2e, were quickly at the works, but on their arrival their services were not required, as the outbreak had been suppressed. It appeared that in the chlorate department a wooden fan is in operation for supplying hot air to the revolvers, and through some cause (supposed to be friction) the fan Mew out, and the woodwork ignited. The fire brigade were im- mediately summoned as a matter of precaution, but in the meantime the flames were extinguished with buckets of water. The damage, fortunately, was very slight.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-09-01_WidnesExaminer_art0144" n="art0144">
        <head>WATER. POLO.</head>
        <p>WATER. POLO.</p>
        <p>ST. HELENS v. MANCHESTER OSBORNE.</p>
        <p>A large number of polo enthusiasts accompanied the St Helens team to Liverpool on Tuesday night, where the Lodge-lane Baths was the seen of the great match between Manchester o,l3)rne (English champions) and St. Helens,Ltverpool and district champions in the semi- final tie for till Northern Counties water polo champion- ship cup. This was the first meeting of the clubs this season, and the tuilding was filled with a large and enthusiastic crowd. ',Wither team placed their full strength in the water, the English champions calling upon several reserves, whilst, unfortunately for the chances of St. Helena. W Walker was an absentee through an accident. Final result : Osborne. six goals; St. Helens, 1 goal; WARRINGTON v. SEFTON.</p>
        <p>ThP great match was preceded by the semi-final tie in the Liverpool and Dißtrict junior squadron competi- tion between Warringtm and Sefton. A good race was witnessed, and ended in a victory for Sefton by half-a- length. The times were—Sefton Bmin. 59sec., Warring- ton 9min. 15sec.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-09-01_WidnesExaminer_art0145" n="art0145">
        <head>WARRINGTON DISTRICT NURSING</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON DISTRICT NURSING</p>
        <p>ASSOCIATION.</p>
        <p>A street and house collection was made on behalf of the above A.ssociation,which is ding an inestimable amount of good in the town, en Saturday. A large number of adults and children were provided with collecting boxes, and stalls were to be seen in the principal thoroughfares. The collection was carried out under the direction of the Warringttn Band of Hope Union, the Young People's Society of Christian Endeavour and the British Temper- ance Association, special praise being due to the secre- taries, Mr. E. Lloyd Jones, and Misses B Lees and E. Bennett. The following amounts have bet n received:— Stalls. Superintendents. Amounts. £ s. d.</p>
        <p>Green-street and Bank Misses J and C Birch Quay Park, shops &amp; alio 8... Miss B Dutton. Misses Gornall. Taylor, and Pair- dough 8 3 6 Trinity Bridge-street Mason (Emmanuel) Central Station Buttermarket-street (Cairo-street) Bewsev-road. Latchford Stockton Heath and Miss Edwards 2 15 0.1 Market Hall Mrs Jones and Mr T Long- shaw Fennel-street</p>
        <p>Total 43 18 9 Te-d.ay ccllections will be made at the different works in the town.</p>
        <p>ISPECIAL VISIT TO C REWE. —Mr. Thomas Isom, °enlist, and Aurist (of Ison's Eve and Ear Dispensary, Leeds), established 1871, will visit tiw Royal Hotel, Crewe, Mon- day, September 4th, Hours 12 to 7.30, and the Royal Hotel, Wigan, Thursday, SPptember 28tb, Hours 2 to 6, and may be consulted on all cases ot failing sight and hear:ng. New treatment without operation. .Sufferers should avail themselves of the present opportunity of consultation. Spectacles adapted for wea..b or defective sight.—Advt. /</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-09-01_WidnesExaminer_art0146" n="art0146">
        <head>BREWSTER SESSIONS.</head>
        <p>BREWSTER SESSIONS.</p>
        <p>[Continued from Page 3.] WA RUINGTON COUNTY PETTY SESSIONAL DIVISION.</p>
        <p>At Newton Town Hall on Thursday, the general annual licensing sessions in cennection with the War- rington County Petty Sessional Division were held. Mr. T. J. Gillespie presided, and the other magistrates on the bench were Colonel Wood, Mr. C. B. F. Borron and Mr. John J.ckson. THE SUPERINTENDEN l''S REPORTS. Superintendent Higginbotham, in his report, stated that the total number of license holders in his section of the division was the same as last year, viz. : Licensed victuallers, 47; beersellers (on and off), 17; grocers' and wine licenses, 2 ; total, 66. In a comparative return showing the number of persens proceeded against for drunkenness, the Superintendent said the mas,,istrates would observe that the number proceeded against last year was 281, against 314 in the preceding year, show- ing a decrease of 33 persons. Generally the licensed persons had conducted their houses, satisfactorily, and there had been no complaints. Superintendent Brassington also submitted a report in which he stated that there were within the townships of Golborne and Haydock, which formed the Wigan por- tion of the Warrington Petty Sessional Division, 33 licensed persons. viz. :-13 innkeepers, 18 beereellers (13 on and 5 off),and 2 with the wine and spirit licenses. The number of persons proceeded against for drunkenness and drunk and disorderly conduct was 141, an increase of 10 as cempared with loot year. The houses,generally speaking, had been well conducted, and he had no ob- jection to make to the renewal of the licenses. An ap- plication would be made by Charles Rose, of Liverpool- road, Ashton-in-Makerfield, for a license to sell intoxi- cants at a hotel proposed to be erected near Ashton.in- Makerfield new railway station, which is in the town- ship of_Haydock.</p>
        <p>The Chairman said the magiitrates were very glad to see such: satisfactory reports. He saw that in Wigan section :there was a diminution of 33 cases, but in the Warrington district 10 more. However, the whole of the statistics given for the year were very satisfactory. The Clerk : Are there any ebj-Ttion-4 to the existing licenses in the Warrington Pety Se;sional Division. The Chairman : The licenses will bq granted. ALTERATIONS TO THE SWAN INN, NEWTON. _ .</p>
        <p>Mr. Lon2ton said he was instructed by 'Messrs. Green- all, Whi:ley and Company, of St. Helens, to ask for per- mission to make certain alteration to the Swan Inn at Newton Common. This was rather an old house and not particularly well adapted from a sanitary point of view for present day require:nents. It was therefore proposed to make some improvemeats. The whole of the alterations were external except that in one or two cases the windows would be enlarged. There was an CA- isting door which it was proposed to CTO3O. The Chairman : Da the improvements increase the drinking facilities. Mr. Longton : No ; the premises are not being enlarged in any way. The Chairman ; Have the police any objections ? Superintendent Higginbotham : We do not object to the alterations. The improvements will remove one of the entrances to the house and the drinking facilities will not b? increased. The application was granted.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-09-01_WidnesExaminer_art0147" n="art0147">
        <head>-0.--- THE SHIP CANAL.</head>
        <p>-0.--- THE SHIP CANAL.</p>
        <p>FAVOURABLY REGARDED AMONG LONDON SHIPOWNERS.</p>
        <p>The Manchester Ship Canal, says a correspondent who has been making inquiries among London mer- chants, has during the year made considerable pro- gress in the good opinion of London shippers. Mr. Colder never did a better service to the Canal than when he conciliated the shipping ring, and secured for Manchestf.r the position of the final port for one line of steamers ta: Australia. This puts Manchester in a better position than ever as a shipping centre to Bombay, and Australasia. Formerly shipping goods by Man- chester not also meant a higher freightage than at Liverpool, but it only meant Mac goods which were taken on board there were simply dumped down anyhow into the ship's hold and then thrown out again on to the docks at Liverpool, and in that wayithe severe handling the packeges received seriously affected their condition, and complaints were numerous from the consignees as to the condition in which the goods arrived at their des- tination. Now all cause of compla nt is removed, and Mambas:or being the final port of call, not only can goods be received later there for shipment, but there is no danger (other than that which aff4cts the whole cargo) of their condition being injured in transit. There is a good deal of speculation wt,ether Messrs. Imray and Company will not shortly put a new line of steamers on for service in the Canal. The growth of the im- portance of the Canal has led to another development which promises nothing but good to it. This is that shipowners are becoming alive to the necessity of con- s* ructing ships with telescopic masts, so that they can negotiate all the bridges that cross the Manchester waterway with ease,and only recently one of the largest London firms has given orders for all its vessels to lo? fitted with these telescopic masts. The importance of this is obvious. It means that ship owners are recog- nising the tact that their vessels may be at any time ordered to go up the canal, and it is, therefore.necessary for them to see that their ships are ie a position to do so. A year ago it was stated by the officials of the Ship Canal that tney believed that the crisis had passsd, and that they had begun to m ice a favourable impres- sion on the big shipping magnates. The history of the past year in London has emphas'zed that point, and has, it is claimed, more than proved the solid basis on which the opinion was formed. REPORT OF MANCHESTER CORPORATION DIRECTORS.</p>
        <p>The Corporation directors of the Manchester Ship Canal have drawn up a report for the half-ycar ended June last, which will be presented to the next meeting of the City Council. The report says :—" The accounts under consideration show that the total net revenue earned from both Ship Canal and Bridgewa'er was £54,729, to which should be added the sum of £1,006 for bankers and general interest, making a total profit on the half year of £55,795. This not only enables pay- ment of the interest on the first and second mort- gage debentures, which absorbed £44,743, and the in- terest on the mortgage on surplus lands, which requires £l,OOO mor?, but enables the directors to pay the sum of £10,053 to the Manchester Corporation on account of the interest due. This sum to,ether with the £16,270 paid in the last half of 1893 (in all, £26,323), leaves a balance of £24,754 to be earned durirg the latter half of this year to make up the sum of £51,077 required to meet the first instalment of the Sinking Fund due Ist January, 1900, and it appears reasonable to anticipate that the amount required will be forthcoming." Then follows a table showing the receipts and expen- diture since the canal was opened : Reeeip,s. Exp,mditure, 1894 97,095 1895 18961 182 330 1887 1898 1899 (first half) 124,183 *Dredging and maintenance charged to capital. Predging charged to capital. CANAL DEVELOPMENTS.</p>
        <p>In speaking of the development of the canal the report says one of the most important works has been bring- ing leo use of the long length of wharfage fronting the, river from No. 1 Dcek Pomona, to Wpden-street foot- bridge, and what is known by the name of "Cornbrock Wharf."</p>
        <p>The Wharf wall itself was completed during the con- struction of the canal, but the excavation alongside and approach thereto, has hitherto been left in abey- ance. This will now add a length of 600 yards to quay space available at the docks, and is, moreover, within the boundary of the city of Manchester. A considerable sum will also be expended to complete this site during the current half year. A further amount of capital ex- penditure both on new works and equipment is contem- plated, and will amount to about £24,500, of which it is expected about £lB,OOO will be expended during the current half-year. This is mainly for the completion of the Cornbrook Wharf and a new wharf near the grain elevator, new storage ground and jettiex at the site of the old Throstle Nest Lock, new locomotives and waggons, besides considerable extensions to the railways on the dock system. Ihe funds that the canal company have in hand ren- dered it unnecessary to immediately raise any further capital on mortgage of surplus lands, the consent for which was given by the Manchester Corporation at the beginning of the present half-year.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-09-01_WidnesExaminer_art0148" n="art0148">
        <head>INTEREST- DUE.</head>
        <p>INTEREST- DUE.</p>
        <p>The amount of interest dun to the Manchester Corpor- ation on the 30:11 June. 1899, amounted to £939,979.</p>
        <p>STOCKTON HEATH.</p>
        <p>PABOCIIIAL PICNIC.—The annual parochial picnic in connection with St. Thomas's Church took place on Saturday, the place visited being Frodsham. About 80 left Victoria-square in 'buses at 1.30, and on arrival at Frodsham gam S &amp;.;. were indulged in. A start was made for home at 8 o'clock, Stockton Heath being reached about t.n o'cicck, the outing kaving proved a most successful one.</p>
        <p>ACCIDENT TO A Eol7.—Shortly after one o'clock on Wednes:lay aftetnoon a boy named Samuel Welsby of St. JAmos'-3trcet, Latchford, met with an accident at Stockten Heath. He had been on an errand, and when paing to Mulberry Tree Inn it is supposed that he struck a horse which was attached to a cart, belonging _ . . . . . .</p>
        <p>to John Pennington, of Appleton Dingle, and which-w; r.tanding in the road. The horsa started, kßocked the the boy down, and kicked his fa?e. He was tAten to the house of his. grandmother, with whom he had been staying at Stockton Heath, and attended by Dr. Parker. He was found to be severely cut about the face.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-09-01_WidnesExaminer_art0149" n="art0149">
        <head>NEWTON PETTY SESSIONS.</head>
        <p>NEWTON PETTY SESSIONS.</p>
        <p>FIRST COURT.</p>
        <p>DlURlDly.—Refare Mr. T 3. Gillespie (chairman). and Mr.</p>
        <p>Jackson.</p>
        <p>DRUNK AND DI4ORDERLY.—.I!e following were fined for being drunk and d sorderly Kent on the 12th August, 2. 6d and costs; Margaret Knight on the 13th August, 5s and costs; James Downs on the 20th August; 5s and c nts ; John Klts on the 26th August, 2s 6:1 and Costs. OBSCENE LANGUAGE --30 ha Terllelr, for having made use of obscene language at Newton, was fined 5s and costa. Doe WITHOUT LICENBE.—Thomas Jones, for having kept a dog without a license at Newton, was fined on the informa- tion of P.C. Schofield.</p>
        <p>CART WITHOUT LIGHT.—John Armstrong was charged with having driven a cart without a lighted lamp in Hay- doek-street, Harlestown. P.C. Linnie proved the case and the defendant was fined 5s and costs. A SLEEPY DRIVER.—John Hewitt was fined 5s and costs for having been asleep in charge of a horse and cart at Park Bide. Newton SHOCKING CRUELTY TO HORSES.—Bertie Herrin and Hz -it Herrin, travelling gypsies, were charged with cruelly ill- treating a horse at Newton on the 12th August by working' the same whilst in an unfit state.—lnspector Robinson, of the .R.S.P.O.A.,gave evidence in support of the charge stating that the horse was in a shocking condition and totally unfit for work. The defendants had travelled the horse from Bolton. It was in a very lame coodition and ought not to have been travelled at all. The defendants sold the horse for slaughtering purposes for 15s. alter witness had stopped the animal.—lnspector Canning said he was in comp iny with the last witness when they met the defendants. rh, horse was terribly lame and unfit to walk.—By the Chair- man: The poor brute was in very great pain?—The Inspec- tor: It was.—The Betilli fined each del, ndant 20s and costs.—Thomas Entwistle was charged with cruelly incest- log a h rse at Newton by working it in an unfit state on the 12th of August.—lnspector Canning said he was in Ashton-road. Newton, on the date named when he saw the defendant driving a grey gelding attached to a osrt. It was Irons suffering fro diseased fetlock joints and strained tendons. It also had a sore on the back and on the near shoulder. It was worn out and utterly unfit for any kind of work. The defendant afterwards sold it for 10s.—The Bench imposei a fine of 20s and costs._</p>
        <p>BOUND OVER TO KEEP TEE PELCT.—Mary A. Ellis was charged with using threats towards Annie Wathurton in Haydook-street, Earlestown, on the 7th August.—Mr Knowles represented the complainant and stated that on August Bank Holiday the complainant was going along Haydock-street, Earlestown in company with two others, when the defendant who had got a mistaken notion in her head as to the com- plainant being too Intimate with her (defendant's) husband, used threats towards her. Mrs. Warburton was about to go to her to tell her that she was mistaken when the defendant picked up the beam of a pair of scales, and threa'enrd to hit her with it.—Evidence in c irroboration of Mr. Knowles' statement having been given, the magistrates bound the de- fendant over to keep the peace for three months in her own recognisances and two sureties of £5 each. UNFRIENDLY NEIGHBOURS.—Margaret Stonehouse was charved with assaulting an elderly woman named Mrs Acton, on the 18th August, in Viaduct-street. Earlestown, and a cross-summons had been issued by Stonehouse charging Acton with an assault at the same time. She also charged Edward Powell with assaulting her on the 19th bast —Mr Knowles represented Stonehouse.—lividence was given by Acton as to a quarrel taking place. Stonehouse tore witness's clothes and dragged her to the Sour and assaulted her. She had b en under medical treatment since.—Evidence in corro- boration was given by neighbours.—Mr Knowles stated that his client only acted in self defence- Mrs. Acton struck her a violent blow on the head with a can, and charged her with being an immoral woman. The charge against Powell was that on the following day he struck Mrs. Stoneh.tuse.— %lacy Stonehouse, of 59, Viaduct-street, Earlestown, was then called, and the magistrates after hearing several witnesses dismissed buth eases.</p>
        <p>" A Holum or SAVIGES."—A case was heard in which Mary Broadbeht was charged with assault- ing Rachael Jones in Athol-street. liarlestown. on the 25th August.—The complainant stated that on k the lidate named she went to the defendant's house for a blacking brush which she had lent to her. The defend- ant without any provocation struck witness a blow in the eye which discoloured it. Mrs Edwards gave corroborative evidence.—Gabriel Moon, auctioneer, of Barlestown, said the complainant was a peaceable, quiet woman. This district was infested with a horde of savages who were continually damaging the property. He had suffered to the extent of £6o.—Constable Clarke said the people in the neighbourhood were often quarrelling and coming to him for assistance. They were a perfect nuisance.—The magistrates bound the defendant over to keep the peace f3r six months in her own recognisances of £5. DAMAGE To GinowiNG CPOPS--William Bates,a boy.pleaded guilty to haying en the 21st August committed damage to clover roots to the exte tof one shilling, the property of John Hatton.—The magistrates ordered tim to pay the damage and also the costs of the summons.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-09-01_WidnesExaminer_art0150" n="art0150">
        <head>SECOND COURT.</head>
        <p>SECOND COURT.</p>
        <p>Before Mr. C. B. F. Borron (chairman) and Colonel Wood. DESTROYING OATS —Arthur Bolton, of Go!borne was fined 78 including costs and ordered to pay 2e damage for destroy- ing some oats in a field belonging to Charles Jenkinson on the 29th Julv.--P.O. Wilde proved the case. BREACH OF THE PEkCE.—Edward 'Arilliams, was charged with being drunk ant disorderly at Golborne on the 11th inst and further with committing a breach of the peace on the sth August. He was fined 108 and costs and bound over to keep the peace for six months in the sum of £5. DRUNKENNESS —The following were dealt with: Joseph Scott, drunk at Haydock on the 13th inst. 2s 6d and costs; Michael Hovey, drunk at Haydock on the 14th lust, 2s 61 and costs ; David Jones, drunk at Haydock on the 4th August. 2s 6d and costs; Abraham Appleton, drunk and disorderly at Haydock, on the sth August 5s and casts; William Green, drunk and disorderly on the 7th inst. 2s 6d and costs; John Lewis, drunk and disorderly at Haydock on the 12th inst, 2e 6d and c its;: John Byrom, William Seddon and Robert Taylor, drank and disorderly at Haydock en the 19th met: Taylor was fined 5s and costs, and the other de- fendants Es 6d and cots. DISORDERLY CONDUCT.—Elizabeth Appleton, of Haydock, for disorderly conduct on the sth August was bound over to keep the peace in the sum of £5 for six months. GAMING WITH CARDS. —Walter Naylor,' Walter Steele and Aquilla Steele were charged with committing this offence at Golborne.—The magistrates said a doubt seemed to exist as regarded Walter Steele and the case against him would te dismisaed The two other defendants were fined is and costs each.</p>
        <p>CRUEL TREATMENT OF HortsEs.--Henry Forester of St. Helens,was charge i with ill-treating two horses by working them in an unfit state and on the 16th Au.zust Messrs John Twist and Sons,Mineral Water Manufacturers, of St. Helens, were charged with allowing them to be worked.--P.C. Dobson stated that at 1t..33 on the night of the 16th inst., he was on duty, at If aydock and saw Forester in charge of two horses attached to a lurry laden with mineral waters. The horses were standing on the road and he found them sweating very heavily The defendant tried to urge them on, but they were disinclined to proceed further. He examined the inside horse, and found it suffering from wounds on the shoulders, flank and shin. The off-hind leg was a mass cf cuts and bruises which appeared to nave been recently dane. Witness asked Forestersto account for the state of the horses, and he stated that with the exception of the cuts under the hind leg the horse was in that state wnen he brought it out. The other animal was a gray mare and was also coffer- ing from wounds on the shoulder.- Inspector Canning said he saw one of the horses on the 18th Augur. at the Ham's Head stables. It was entirely unfit for work and in poor nondition.—Forester was fined 2Ga and costs aim Messrs. Twist and Sons. 40s and costs. _</p>
        <p>• A COLLIER'S MEAN ACT.—Torn Greenall, a collier, of Hay- dock. was charged with having on the 25th July unlawfully attempted to steal is 10?-ii the monies of Messrs. Richard Evans and Company.—Mr. Pearce, of Liverpool, prosecuted on behalf of the Company and stated that the defendant was'a collier employed at the Princes Pit,Haydock.He was entitled to certain amounts for coal brougidt to the surface and on the 25th inst he was noticed to take another man's tally off tubs of coal and substitute his own. By this means the defendant was attempting to steal is I.old. The company wished the bench to make an example ot him.—The defendant now ex- pressed his sorrow and was fined 5s and Costs. DAMAGING Tuursres. —Michael Clonnev, Thomas Maguire and Martin King were chargsd with trespassing cn a field belonging to William Cowley at Haydock on the 17th inst and cornmittihg damage to turnips,—The complainant said he had suffered severely from trespaasers this year. The defendants were ordered to pay a fine of is including costs and pay the damage. CASES DISMISSED.—James Faulkner, of Golborne was charged with assaulting James Carney on the 26th August. —The complainarat said he met the defendant in High-street, Golborne, who struck him in th. eye. He went to Dr. Part to have it attended to —The defendant denied the assault and the case was dismissed.—Eli Proscot was charged with using threats towards MHz Jones, on the 28th August at Golborne.—The magistrates dismissed the case TRANSFER OF LICENSE.—John Foster applied for the trans- fer of an off-license from John Parr, of 65, High-street, Golborne.—There being no objection, the application was granted.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-09-01_WidnesExaminer_art0151" n="art0151">
        <head>DEATH OF AN OM; WARRINGTONIAN.</head>
        <p>DEATH OF AN OM; WARRINGTONIAN.</p>
        <p>It is with regret that we announce the death of Mr. James Houghton, gardener, an old Warrington- ian. For 11 years he had the management of the Arpley Bowling Green, where by strict attention to his work, and his kind and genial disposition be won the respect of the whole of the members of the club. Be was 79 years old, and his rather sud- den death, which took plac3 on tie 26th August, will be regretted by many.</p>
        <p>LADY GUARDIANS POSITION WITH REGARD TO</p>
        <p>CONTRACTS.</p>
        <p>It will be remembered that at the meeting of the Warrington Board of Guardions when the question of accepting a contract of Messrs. Williams and Clay was under discussion, Mr. Knowles raised the question whether the Board could accept a tender from a firm of which a lady guardian was wife of one of the members of the company, The question of the legality of the tender has been raised in the "Justice of the Peace," and that j )urnal's question and answer ara given as follow "Local Government Act, 1894, section 40. Hus- band of lady guardian—contract. A is a lady guar- dian. Her husband is a chief partner and manager in a firm (not a limited liability company) who have tendered to supply goods and do work for this Union. In your opinion is this a legal course? Lancastrian answer.—There is nothiag illegal in tendering. The only question is whether or nat the lady guardian will become disqualified if the ten- der is accepted. Section 43 (1) (e) of the Local Government Act, 1894, disqualifies a member of a Board of Guardians who is concerned in or partici- pates in the profits of any contract with the Board. It is a little doubtful as to whether a wile can be said to participate in the profits of her husband's coatracs.. The old doctrine that a husband and wife are one is still in existence, although much raolified by statute, and we are inclined to think that the lady woald be disqualified."</p>
        <p>OkAtialr's Cocox is absautely pare, being entirely fre from kola, malt, ho, alkali, or dny foreign s.dmixture Caution ! The publis should insist on haying OA-DBURY'S —sold only in Packets andains—a3 other Cocoas are often übatitutea for the sake of extra proilt.—Ativt. (4).</p>
        <p>RUNCORN VOLUNTEERS.</p>
        <p>ANNUAL PRIZE SHOOTING.</p>
        <p>The annual prize shooting competition held in connec- tion with the Runcorn Detachment of the 2nd V.B. Earl Cheshire Regiment took plao3 at Hartford ou Mon- day last. Captain G. F. Asbton and Deutenant Linaker were present as well as a large wtenclance of non- commissioned officers and men. The firing commenced at about 9 o'clock and continued until shortly after 6 o'clock in the evening. The weather was lowering all day, and the wind and light variable,so tbat good shoot- ing was somewhat hindered; but with the exception of one sharp shower about noon the rain kept off. The scoring on the whole was not of So high a standard as that of last year ; though the recruits tar surpassed toe general perform.ance of last years re,c.:ruits. Last year toe first prize (recruits) was taken with 46 points,whilst this year 50 was the score which secured the trophy. Sergeant Edwards, whom it will be remembere..l secured the battalion prize last year, again succeeded in winning the detachment priz, and also received first in the "extra s:ries" competition, circumstances which prove he is in admirable form. Colour-Sergeant Harvey whose name is greatly respected amongst his fellow volunteers, again secured the drill prize for his company. the fol- lowing is the full list :</p>
