      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-09-01_WidnesExaminer_art0153" n="art0153">
        <head>DEATH OF MR. WILLIAM THOMPSON E ARLESTOWN.</head>
        <p>DEATH OF MR. WILLIAM THOMPSON E ARLESTOWN.</p>
        <p>Another of Earlestown's old and highly respected inhabitants in the person of Mr. William Thompson of Sankey Hill, Earlestown, passed away, after a re- markably short and sudden illness, at the age of 68 years. ') he deceased gentleman's health has for some time been in a precarious condition, which occasioned his family some anxiety, but no imme- diate fatal result was anticipated. On Thursday last he was about his ordinary duties as manager of the Sankey Sugar Works, and it was then ob- served that he was not looking very well. On Friday morning he was suddenly taken seriously ill, and Dr. Valentine, his son-in-law was sent for who on seeing him at once recommended further medical advice. This was at once obtained, but it was found that his condition was hopeless, and he expired in the presence ot his family on. Saturday evening. Mr. Thompson has been connected with the Sankey Sugar Company for 44 years, in fact ever since the works were established at Earlestown, and for a considerable portion of the time has held the position of manager. He was highly esteemed and held in much respect by all the emplo3'6s and everybody connected with the works,and sincere regret is expressed at his demise. Although a Conservative and a Churchman,he never took any active part in politics, but for many years he was actively connected with the Wargrave Church during the rectorship of the late Rev. Canon Whitley,first as sidesman and afterwards as warden. Tie was also an ardent supporter of the school which at one time was so successfully carried on in Vitriol Square, under the patronage of the Sankey Sugar Company, and there are many in Earlestown who will look back with pleasure upon his bene- ficial influence in connection with the school which has now been closed several years. Mrs. Thompson pre-deceased her husband six years ago. THE FUNERAL.</p>
        <p>The interment of the body took place on Tuesday afternoon in the family vault in Wargrave Church yard. The funerel cortege left Sankey Hill at 3-30 all the workmen who were on duty lin- ing up on each side, as it passed along from the• house. The principal mourners were Mr William and Mr Walter Thompson (sons), Mr James Thompson of Bury (brother) and two nephews; Dr Valentine (son-in-law). Amongst others present were Mr E C Turner (Liverpool) principal of the firm; Colonel Morrison (Liverpool), Mr W 11 D Brown, and Mr II E Jones, represent- ing the Liverpool Office, Mr C B F Borron, -J.P., Mr Bardesley, Manchester, Mr John Vicars, Mr Hu!me, Mr Barton (Newton), Mr C Whitley (Stretton), Mr Thomas Molyneux, Rev J C &amp;mock, Mr T ilaselden and about 50 of the employes. The body was en- close I in a polished oak coffin with brass mounts, and bore a plate with the inscription " William Thompson, died August 260, 1899, aced 68 years." The carriers were Messrs. flaselden, White, Monks, B Dolen, T Kenny, A Wrigley. Thomas Tunstall, T Cave, John Eckersley, and J Booth, all old employes. The service was vesy impressively read by the Rev. T. 11. Matthews, vicar of Earlestown,</p>
