        <p>BOROUGH OF WARRINGTON,—The SANITARY WORKS COMMITTEE of the Council of the Borough are prepared to RRCEIVE3 TENDERS for the WHOLE SUPPLY of VITRIOL 148 degrees twaddle, required at Longford Depot, for the next twelve months, to be delivered free, at such times and in such quantities as may be required from time to time. Any further information may be obtained from the under- signed. Tenders, addrf seed to the Chairman of the Comatit- tee endorsed "Tender for Vitriol."must be delivered here mot later than Four o'clock on WEDNESDAY NEXT, September 6th. The lowest or any Tender will not necessarily be accepted. JAMES DEAS. Bank House, 30th August, 1899. - BOROUGH OF WARRINGTON CLERK OF WORKS. The COUNCIL of the Borough of Warrington require the services of a thoroughly COMPETENT CLERK of WORKS in connection with the erection of new Police Buildings now being erected frorn the designs of R. Burns Dick,;Esq., Archi- tect. Newcastle-on-Tyne. Applicants must state age, experience, and salary required and send copies of not more than three recent testimonials to the undersigned not later than 12 oVock on Saturday the 9th day of September, 1899, endorsed" Clerk of Works." THOMAS LONGDIN, Borough Surveyor. Town Hall, Warrington.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON CORPORATION.—wATER- WORKS EXTENSIONS 1899 —CONTRACT No 1. TO BoRING CONTRACTORS. The WARRINGTON CORPORATION are prepared to RECEIVE TENDERS from Competent Persons willing to enter into a Contract for the attention of a 6 inch TRIAL BORING and TEST PUMPING. Copies of the Specificatlen and Form if Tender may be Obtained at the Office of the Engineer, JAMES DEA.S, C.E.. M.1.M.E., Bank bolls', Warrington, on and after MONDAY, 4th SEPTEMBER, on drp ,sit of one guinea, Which will be returoed on receipt of a bona-fide tender and the return of tke docunu-nts. Sealed Tenders, addressed " The Chairman. Water Com- mittee, Town Mall, Warrington," and endorsed" Tender for Trial Boring " to be delivered at my office not later than SATURDAY, 16th SEPTEMBER, 1899. The Corporation do not bind themselves to accept the lowest or any Tender. J. LYON WHITTLE, Town Clerk. Town Hall. Warrington, let September, 1899.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON UNION. Persons willing to CONTRACT for the suoply of any of the under- Mentioned articles trom Michaelmas to Lady Day next, ex-lept where stated for a year, to be delivered free of ex- Dense at the Warrington Union Workhouse, or at the Industrial Schools, Padgate, at such times and in such quantities as the Board of Guardians shall direct, are requested to send in TENDERS. properly sealed, endorsed, and free of expense, adcirt,,secl to the Clerk to the Guardians. before four o'clock p.m. on TUESDAY, the 12th day of ShPTBMBER inst. The following are the articles for which TENDERS are required : Groceries and Provisions Shoes, Clogs and Slippers Beef and Mutton Glazing „ Joints for Officers Brushes Milk for a year Coffins Flour and Irish pin head meal Best Oakum rope for picking Loaves and Bread Best Penmaentnawr Stone Beer and Porter for breaking purposes Straw and Potatoes Leather Drapery ece. Cord and Twill Coal, Slack, and Gas Coke Samples of any of the above may be seen on application to the Master of the Workhouse. The Coal and Slack supplied must be to the satisfaction of the House and Schools Commit- tees. The Pit and quality of coal must be named in the Tender. Forms of tender and full particulars as to the probable quantities of each article required may be obtained at the Clerk's Office. Each testier must be made upon the prescribed form and properly enclosed and endorsed with the name of the article tenilered for. Each artzeie tendered for must be of size, material and quality shown it the Workhouse. No tenders for other articles will be accepted. Each contra?tor must be prepared (if required) to enter into security for the due performance of his contract. The Guardians do not bind themselves to accept the lowest or any tender. All contract accounts will be paid quarterly—By order. JAMES CLOWES SUTTON. Clerk to the Guardians. Bewley Chambers, Warrington, September let 1899.</p>
        <p>G"'S HO6PITA I, MEDICAL SCHOOL—The WINTER -ESSION will BEGIN on MONDAY. Oct. 2. Entrance scholarships of the combined value of £4lO are awarded annuaily, ant numerous prizes and medals are open for competition by students of the school. The number of patients treated in the wards during last Fear exceeded 6,500. All hospital appointments are made strictly in accordance with the merits of the candidates. and without extra pay- ment. Them are 28 resident appointments open to students of the HoLpital annually,without payment of ad-litioaal fees, and numerous non-resident appointments in the general and oPeelal departments. The Queen Victoria Ward, recently re- opened, will provide additional accommodation for gyncceo- logical and maternity cases. The College accommo-lates 60 students, under the super- vision of a resident warden. The Dental School provides the full curriculum required for the L.D.S. E. gland. The Clubs' Union Athletic Ground is easily accessible. A handbook of information or those about to enter the rnedicll profession will be forwarded on application. For the P, ospectus or the School, containing full particu- lars as to fees, course of study advised, regulation of the Cot ,ge, 8:o., apply personally, Cr by letter, to the DEAN Guy's Hospital, London-bridge, SE.</p>
