        <p>FRODSHAII. STEWS FIRE AT NETHERTON.-011 Saturday afternoon a large stack of hay, valued at nearly £2OO, became ignited at Nntherton Farm, near Frodsham, occupied by Mr. Ralph Bate. Runcorn and lielshy Fire Brigades attended, but owing to the great drought water was ',carte, and little could be done towards extinguishing the flames. WEEKLY REPORT ON ?RE HEALTH OF WARRINGTON. Week Ending August 19th, 1899. During the week the following births and deaths were registered : Births. Deaths. Warrington Registration District 53 37 The excess of births over deaths was 16. The Birth Rate was equal to 43 per 1,000. The death-rate from all canaes was equal to 30 per 1,000, against 16-4 the average of the previous week, and against 16.5 the corresponding period of last year. The death - rate from the seven chief zvmotic diseases was equal to 14.6 per 1,000. against 4-6 the average rate of the previous week, and against 0.8 the corresponding period of 1898. The ages of mortality were- -0-1 1-5 5-15 15-25 25-65 65 up. 37 21 4 2 1 7 2 The deaths included-17 from diarrt•cea and dysen- tery, 1 from pbthisis. 1 from bronchitis, pneumonia etc.. 1 from injuries. 1 from whooping cough, 16 from all other causes. Total 37 The following cases of Infectious Disease have been reported during the week :—Scarlet fever 11 cases. enteric 2 cases. Total number of patients in hospital week ending Sat- urday 19th August, 1899, 50. METEOROLOGY—The mean temper atlre was 61-6 Fah. and therefore the same as the preceding week. The maximum temperature 83 F., was recorded on the 13th inst., and the minimum temperature 47 F., on the 16 th inst. The Rainfall measured 37 inch. The. general direc- tion was Westerly. JOHN H. GORNALL, Medical Officer of Health.</p>
