        <p>ANNUAL POLICE SPORTS. AN UNDOUBTED SUCCESS. " Policeinee have nearts as hie:e and as wel'-regula ted as thoe- of ordinary mortals !" and on Teursday last week, the Warrington B Iroiigh police fully evioene.ed the apaorisin, in the prom it ou of a most successful Athletic and Gemnastic Sp irtA, theobjet of wnicu was the argum-ntieg of the funds of the Warrington Infir- mary and the District Nursing Association. TB9 sports were held on the ground of the Warrington Rugby Foot- ball Club, Wiidersnocl road, and attracted a large concourse of spectators. The day was delightfully brigbt -a trifle too ho!, perbaps-',he track was a fairly fast one; the metal present, in the shape of competitors was good and plentiful ; and the management was efficient ; so that to say the whole arrangements were successfully carrie i out, and highly appreciated by the beholden is not at all surprising. Then, added to all this, the procee:liogs were graced with the attendance of the Mayor and Mayoress of Warrington, and numerous other lalies and geutlemea patrons, whose sympathy and support to al good works in the district is so well known. Punctually at three o'clock the sports commenced, and by about 7.20 the whole proceedings were concluded. During the afternoon, and whilst the sports were in Progress, a capital display of gymnaatic ability, was given by several members of the Warrington Gym- nasium, under the direction of Professor Kinslev, of Warrington, and Professor Wall, of Rochdale. Com- mencing with dumbell mass exercises, evolutions on the parallell and horizontal bars, bewildering yet fascinating exercises with the Indian clubs followed. Mr. J. W. Lee, of Rochdale,gave a splendid exhibition of solo-clubs; an excellent amateur performance being con- chided with a fisished display on the "vaulting horse." Professors Kinsley and W all also gave exhibitions with the single sticks and quarter staffs. The latter contest especially, evoking considerable applaipe and, no little merriment. Without doubt, this al fresco interlude tended greatly to make the sports the success it was. A boxing exhibition by P.C.'s Whittaker and Howard, of the Borough Police, was also an interesting feature, and showed that at least some of the police have a useful knowledge of" fisticuffs" and could make a creditable. sho 1, if called upon. At the c )nclusion of the sports the prizes were dis- tributesi by the Mayoress of Warrington (Mrs. Wright) after which Coun. D. TINNION said he had a pleasing duty to per- formo.nd one, which lie thought,although last, was one of the most important in connection with sports of that kind; and that was to thank the Mayoress for so kindly coming amongst them that day to so graciously distribute the prizes. He thought it was a very good thing to win a prize ; but, he was sure, it greatly enhanced its value when presented by a lady as on that eccasion. (Rear, hear). Whilst speaking he wished to extend the thanka of the Committee to all those ladies and gentlemen who had done so much for the sports and done it so well. He accorded the Mayoress their heart- felt thanks, and hoped she and her huapand (the Mayor) would long live to enjoy the respect of the good people of Warrington, as they were always so willing to help forward any good movement of that kind. (Applause). Mr. bTANLEY WILSON seconded. Mr. L. TALBOT (Chief Constable) responding, on behalf on the Mayor and Mayoress, said he had to apologise for the absence of the Mayor, who had been obliged to leave:before the distribution of prizes came off He could assure them the vote of thanks was appreci- ated. He wished also to take that opportunity of extend- ing his thanks to all who had done such good work for them, and ale, wished to thank the Warrington public, who had made the sports the success they had been. (Hear,hear.) Re hoped too that that sort of thing would inspire the fostering of better confidences between the public and the police, and no doubt the result would be much better in the future. (Applause.) The se,verat events resulted as follows One hundred yards flat handicap,(confined to Borough Police), first eight-day striking clock; seeond case of carvers ; third, silver-plated teapot ; fourth jam dish :- First heat: 1, P C Whittaker scratch, 2, Detective Bar- low, 11. Second heat: 1, P C Pennington, 9; 2,1" C Woods, 13, Third heat: 1, P C Newsy. 9; 2, P S Evans. 16. Final: 1, P C Newsy; 2, Detective Bar low ; 3, P S Evans ; 4, P C Whittaker. One mile bicycle handicap (confined to 'Berough Police). Prize" Royal Court Cup 'S presented by Mr F Crabtree (Cup to be :won twice before becoming pro- perty of winner) ; also goal medal :-1, P C F Whit- taker. Five competed easy win. One hundred yards handicap (open) first, marble timepiece and bronzes ; secend gentleman's fitted dress- ing case ; MIA biscuit barrel :-Winnars of heats : Ist, W Rogers, Ashton•underlsyne,Bl, (in this heat T Horan, Warrington was disqualified for three false starts). 2nd, J W Davies, Wallasey Harriers, 91; 3rd. L Shield, St. Helens, 9 ; 4th. J Flatten, Warrington, 81; sth, 3 Cress- well, Salford, 10; 6th, E Hodkinson, Sankey Harriers, ; 7th. G W Mee, Farnworth Harriers, 9*; Bth, G Adams, Liverpool, P AS. 9i ; 9th, A E Curran, Birken- head, 9i, had a walk over ; lOch, J W Park, Broughton Harriers, 8; 11th, J Prince, Sankey Harriers, 10. Semi final • W Rogers, J W Park, E Hodkinson, J Prince. Final : Ist, J W Park ; 2nd, J Prince ; 3rd, W Rogers. Capital race for final. Two-hundred and twenty yards flat handicap (open to all police forces), first, case of cutlery and carvers ; sec trid, barometer and chronometer combined ; third, silver plated butter dish :-Winners of the heats: let, H Stephens, Liverpool P A S, 13 ; P Scholes, Manchester PAC,I4 ; J Eamnshaw, Manchester, PA C, 16. 2nd, G Adams, Liverpool P A S, scratch; E Lawton, Manishester PAC,I6 ; J W Lawson, Macchester PA C. 13. Final: let, P Scholes ; 2nd, G Adams; 3rd, 13 Stephens. Won easy ; second and third a yard. Orie mile novice bicycle handicap (open), first,Tantalus spirit stand ; second, silver plated tea pot ; third, barometer :-Winners of heats: Ist, J Cilgrin, Winning- ton Park, FC, 100 ; A Bangley, Cadishead, 100. 2nd, H Coppack, Over CC, 75; R Walker, Manchester Wneelers, 100. 3rd. L M Needham, Altrincham, 100; W T Nicholls,Prescett C C, 100. 4th. D CeopereWoelston 100 •, J Rogers, Liverpool PA 8, 110. Final: Ist, R Walker ; 2nd, A Bangley ; 3rd W T Nicholls. During this race several riders had nasty spills and several machines were wrecked. The riders, however, luckily escaped injury. Capital finish between first and second. Four-hundred and forty yards handicap (open), first, gentlemad's large fitted dressing bag ; second, silver plated spirit kettle and stand ; third, cruet stand :- Winners of heats: Ist, G H Horton, Liverpool, 35 ;J T Hulme, Manchester, 26 ; 2nd, J Robinsen, Warrington 1' AC,2B ; R Wailing, Widnes' 35. 3rd, A Abel, Run- corn, 28; G W Mee,Farnworth Harriers. 4th. C Spencer Earlestown, 35 ; J Sage, Sefton Harriers, 28. Final : Ist, A Abel ; 2nd, G W Mee; 3rd, J Robinson. The final of this race was a superb one Abel winning by about six inches. Robinson also ran well and was close be- hind. Tug of War (confined to Police and Works in the Borough) six aside and captain: First, seven gold cen- tre medals •, Isecond, seven silver medals. First round: P Walker and Sons (Dallarn-lane), captain J Tilling, beat Crosfield and Sons. captain W Warburton; Borough Police, captain Sergeant Evans, beat P Walker and Sons (King-street), captain J Taylor; Fleming and Co., captain J Boyle, heat Richmond and Co., captain A Sills ; Pierpoint and Bryant, captain G Wright, a bye. Second round : Borough Police beat P Walker and Sons (Dallara-lane) ; Fleming and Co. beat Pierpoint and Bryant, after a capital bout. Final : Borough Police beat Fleming and Co. The whole of the teams showed fairly even strength, and not in any case were the winners able to run away. The officials responsible for the carrying out of the programme were :-President, Mr. Is. Talbot, Chief Constable ; vice-president. Detective-Superintendent Monag ban; judges, Councillor Tinnion, Mr. Stanley Wilson, and Mr. Harvey ; stewards of the course, Chief- Inspector Phillips, Inspectors Taylor, Rowbotham, and Sergeant Cheeney •, marksmen, Messrs. A Bailey and El Greenall ; starter, Mr T Wainwright, Salford Harriers ; secretary, William Evans, Sergeant; Mr E Pickavance, N • At intervals the Warrington Borough Band, under the conductership of Mr. T. Giffin, submitted some of the following items :-March, "Jurgurat," Swift; selection. "La Favorite," Donezctti ; lancers, " Royal Irish," Wright ; echottische, " Welcome," Walters ; quadrille, "Oryintha," Hound; fantasia, "Eureka," Round ; galop, " Geed Morning," Fogson ; waltz, " Bright Angels," Fecorini; selection, " Masanielo," Auber ; polka," Jolly Boys," Cavell ;'barn dance, "Plantation," Field ; lancers "Royal English." Wright; waltz, "Italy," Round.</p>
