        <p>HAVE YOU A BAD LEG wnstrownonuxed; witht inflammationdischarge d swollen, otherwise, that . ewit an you press your finger on the inflamed part it leaves the im oression ? If so, under the skin you have poison that defies all the remedies you have tried, which. if not extracted, you never can recover, but go on suffering till death releases you. Perhaps your knees are swollen, the joints being ulcerated; the same with the ankles, round which the skin may be dis- coloured, or there may be wounds, the disease if allowed to continue will deprive you of the power to walk. You may tinve :,ttended various hospitals ad had medical advice, ani Peen told your case is hopeless, or advised to submit to am- putation; but do not. for I CAN clam YOU. I don't say perhaps; but I WILL. Because others have failed is no reason for not now being cured. Send at once a Postal Order for 2s. 6d. to C. W. ALBERT, 73, FARRINGDoN-STRIDET LONDON, and you will receive a box of GRASSHOPPER OINTMENT AND PILIA which is a certain remedy for the cure of Bad Legs lionse- maids' Knee, Ulcerated Joints, Carbuncles, P015)26.1 Hands, Bunions. (Registered Copy. ight.)</p>
        <p>STOCKTON HEATH. - MEETING OF THE PAROCHIAL COMMITTEE. A meeting of the Parochial Committee was held in the National schoolroom, Stockton Heath, on Thursday last week, Mr. Masou presided, an d there were also pre- sent : Messrs Whitley, Clarke, Mc Intyre, Pemberton. with Mr G F Ashton (clerk), Mr Swinton (surveyor) and Mr Farrington (inspector). THU GAS BILL. The Clerk stated that in accordance with instructions he wrote to the Warrington Corporation Gas Depart- ment for an amenued account tor the surply of gas in the township. He now produced an amended account which was for £34 14. 61. Mr. Clarke : Are the etre( t lamps righted now P—The Clerk : They have been lighted since the 14th August .—Mr. Whitey moved that the attention of the Gas Conamittte be called to the fact that the lamp at the corner ot Ellesmere-road and Old London-road Bridge had not been lighted. This was agreed to and the account was passed. SEWERS IN PRIVATE STREETS. The Clerk stated that at the last meeting of the com- mittee be was instructed to report as to the laying of sewers in private streets. He had gone very carefully into the matter and he found by section 150 of the Pub- lic Health Act of 1875 that Urban District Councils were given power to compel owners in laying out any streets to sewer, level, flag and pave the streets. That section, however, bad not been granted to Stockton Heath ncr for tbat matter to any parish in the Rural District Council of Runcorn. Urban District Councils were given power to make bye- laws with respect to sewering, Sic., of streets. The Stockton Heath Committee adopted byelaws in 1896 with respect to the p aving • and flagging, &amp;c., of streets, but the Local Government Board did not sanction any byelaws for the sew ering of streets. Where a main sewer had been laid they coul d compel the owners of property to connect with it, providing it was within 100 feet of the property. If the sewer were more than 100 feet away, they could compel the owner to provide other means for disposing of the sewage, and in the event of the sewer being brought within 100 feet at some future date they could compel the owner to con- nect with the sewer despite the fact that he had adopted a cesspool or some other means of disposing ot the sewage. He was of opinion that the committee should apply to the Local Government Board giving them power to adopt section 150 of the Public Health Act. He could not say whether the application would be granted. They could not compel owners to construct a sewer until they had that power. A discussion ensued in which Mr. Pemberton stated that wherever a sewerage scheme was in existence, the main sewers were constructed at the public expense. Mr. Whitley stated that property owneri had to pay their share of the cost. Mt. Pemberton denied that this was so. Mr. Clarke moved that the Surveyor be instructed to prepare a plan showing the sewer at Warburton-street (where the difficulty bad arisen), and giving the length of sewer necessary to be constructed to enable the property owner to connect up. This was agreed to, and the subject is to be further considered at a special meeting of the Committee a fort- night hence. The Surveyor, in answer to Mr. Pemberton, said the distance he proposed to lay the sewer was 240 lineal 'yards. The present sewer was not satisfactory, and the new one be proposed was to replace it. It would cost about £35. Only £5 would be necessary to connect up from the existing sewer, but he would not recommend the adoption ef this course, as that sewer was not large enough for a public sewer. THE SCARLET FEVER EPIDEMIC. Mr. Farringtoo, in reply to a question by Mr. Clarke, said the epidemic of scarlet fever had abated and no fresh cases had been notified. TIM ADOPTION OF THE PAIL SYSTEM. Mr. Pemberton moved that the committee accept the sample of pails submitted by Mr. Joseph Albinson. Mr. Mclntyre seconded and it was agreed to.—lt was decided to leave the number to be obtained in the hands Of the clerk. This concluded the business, PARISH COUNCIL MEETING. THE LATE CLERK AND THE DISTRICT DOCUMENTS. A meeting of the Stockton Heath Parish Council was held on Thursday last week, Mr. Mason presided and there were also present :—Messrs Pemberton, Clarke, Mclntyre. and E. Bradshaw (clerk). TUB COUNTY RATE BASIS. The Clerk reported that after carefully considering the county rate basis it was decided not to oppose the basis, THE GIVING UP OF DOCUMENTS. The Clerk read the following correspondence which bad taken place between himself and the late clerk with reference to giving •up documents relating to the township. "Stockton Heath, 3rd July 1899.—Mr. John Ellison, the School House, Stretton.—Dear sir,—At a:meeting of the Council held on Tuesday last, the following resolu- tion was adopted :—*That the Clerk write to Mr. John Ellison, asking him to forth with deliver up to the present clerk, all plans and documents in his (Mr. Ellison's) possession, belonging to the Stockton Heath Parish Council.' I shall be glad if you will do this as early as possible. Amongst them appear to he plans relating to the footpath leading from Mill-lane to Fairfield-road, and the sketch of Mr. Warburton's. showing the line of six feet to be given up by him on side of Old London-road.—Yours truly, Edwin Brad- shasv." The following reply was received :—" School House, Stretten, July 9, 1899.—Dear sir,—ln answer to your communication of the 3rd inst., I have to inform you that I have not in my possession any plans or documents belonging to the Parish Council of Stockton Heath. The two plans you name I obtained on instructions given to me by the Appleton Parish Council, and it will be impos- sible for me to hand them over to you until I receive instructions to do so. If you will forward me a request for them to be delivered to you, I will lay the same before the Appleton Parish Council at their next meet- ing.—Yours truly, John Ellison." The Clerk to the Parish Council replied as follows "Stockton Heath, 2nd August. Dear sir,—l am in re- ceipt of yours of the 9th ult., the contents of which sur- prise me. You surely do not dispute the right of the Stockton Heath Parish Council to the possession of do- cuments relating to that portion of the old township of , Appleton known as the Stockton Heath ward. When the two parishes were divided the old Parish Council of Appleton was dissolved and two entirely new Councils , were created. At that time all papers and documents dealing with business transactions by the old council relating to the Stockton Heath Ward ought to have been handed over to the Parish Council of Stockton Heath and presumably as you were clerk to that council you had custody of them on their behalf. In these circum- stances I must ask you to be gocd enough to deliver up to me as early as possible what is asked for in the resolution adopted by my council, a copy of which I sent you on the 3rd July last. Yours truly, Edwin Bradshaw." The Clerk said he had received no reply to that letter. The Clerk thought it was a piece of red-tapeism to be asked to apply to the Appleton Parish Council for the documents. . The Chairman said the Clerk was playing on ,the • words "Appleton Parish Council." It was not the present authority bearing that name which gave Mr. Ellison authority .to get the documents. After further discussion it was decided on the propo- sition of Mr. Clarke,seconded by Mr. Mclntyre that the • Clerk be instructed to communicate with the Chairman 1 of the Appleton Parish Council, asking him to bring the matter before his Council. NlThls concluded the business.</p>
