        <p>DYNAMITE OUTRAGE IN THE STATES. Another diabolical outrage was perpetrated on Wed. nesday night at Cleveland. Tao miscreants, it is stated, were workmen now on strike. A dynamite cartridge was placed on the metals so as to explode when a tram' car passed over it. The result was terrible. The car was completely smashed. Eighteen of the passengers were injured, six of them very seriously. The buildings in the vicinity were much damaged, all the glass being' broken. No arrests have yet been been made. SEIZURE OF ROYALIST PAPERS IN PARIS. PARIS ,THURSDAY. According to the "Figaro," search was made yesterdaY at the roomsoof two young men moving in good society and connected with Royalist Leagues. Highly limper- tant documents are stated to have beam found. The young men will not, however, be arrested for the pro - sent. YACHT DISASTER. SUPPOSED LOSS OF FOUR LIVES. A body landed at Harwich on Tuesday night waS identified yesterday as that of Wm. Pnillip Anningson, an engineer' in the employ of the London and India Joint Dock Co. Mr. Annirgson's brother states that deceased left Ramsgate on Wf-dnezlay in last week With a Mr. Beavis, solicitor, of Land )n ; a Mr. Cornell, et Lloyd's Bank, Seven Oaks; and a lad. They sailed frau' Ramsgate in Mr. Beavia's yacht Vesta, porq cf which are now at Harwich, having been picked up at sea. It is believed the Vesta was run down. Mr. A.uningßOß was 39 years of age, and a bachelor. LATEST LOCAL WEATHER FORECAST. The weather forecast for the North-west of England and North Wales up to noon to-day is as follows :--- Wind backing to the south and freshening ; fair at first: then dull and rainy. ELECTRICAL WORKS DESTROYED BY FIRE. PARIS, THURSDAY: A serious fire broke out en Wednesday evening in some large electrical works at La Chapelle Triage, by which the Chapelle and St. Dennis Railway Stations are supplied. Damage was done to the extent of one mil' lion francs. No one was injured. BIRMINGHAM BREWERS AND SUPERFLUOUS LICENSES. FORTY-FOUR LICENSES SURRENDERED. At Birmingham Licensing Ses,ions, yesterday it avae stated by the Chairman (Mr. Arthur Chamberlain) that the brewers, acting in conjunction with the magistrates, had agreed to voluntarily surrender this year forty-four licenses, and they were prepared to surrender fifty-fiva others as soon as negotiations could be completed. Last year twenty licenses were 'given up to the :nagistrateso. who are quite satisfied with the punlic-spirited action et the brewers. YESTERDAY'S CRICKET. CLOSE oi, PLAY SCORES. LANCASHIRE v. MIDDLESEX :—Miidlesex 246 all out. Rain stopped play. SURREY v. HAMPSHIRE :—Surrz,y, 341 for 8. C. I. THORNTON'S ENGLAND XI. v. AUSTRA- LIANS :—Australians, 233; Tnornton's XI. 3 for no wickets. SUSSEX V. KENT :—Kent all out 230: ESSEX v. WARWlCKSHlRE.—Warwickshire, 189 fat 6. 411. A SUCCESSFUL RUNCORI4 DOG OWNER. We tinderstand that Mr. W. Bankes. of High-streetp Runcorn, was successful in carrying off a 3rd prize iii the smooth-haired dog species, at the Cheshire Agrictil` tural show, held at Northwich. DEATH OF MR. ROBERT CHAPMAN. It is with regret we learn of the death of Mr. Robert Chapman, landlord of the Bowling Green Arms, Chester, which occurred on Wednesday about noon, with lamentable suddenness. Mr. Chapman was well-known in Warrington and Runcorn, being a near relative of many respected reeidents of botn places. He leaves a widow, and family of four children, the eldest of whom is about eleven years. General sympathy is expressed on all hands with the sorrowing relatives, and especially the young widow. The deceased, who was a native of Gres- • ford, married Miss Chrimes, the eldest daughter of the late Mr. Jo.eph Chrimes, who was Mayor of Warrington in 1855-6-7. For many years Mr. Chap- man was traveller for Mr. John Geddes, tea merchants Warrington. ASK for CUNNINGHAM'S HIGH-CLASS MINERAL. WATBES.—Advt. 0</p>
